      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>AT.vVkA IT IS The Examiner, Friday, February 27, 1903. McDOUGALL's Pateqt SELF-RISING FLOUR THE BEST</p>
        <p>THE NIGHT OF THE CRIME.</p>
        <p>"Why do your father and Will's father hats one another so?" _ _</p>
        <p>-- "I carettell. They have not spoken for year "Pigs l" muttered Miss Gillard under her breath, and without any clear knowledge why she used the term. "Has Will written?"</p>
        <p>"He wrote just after he left Dartbridge, telling me that his father had insisted* he should go to London. Since then I have had no letter, although I have been expecting one. He promised so write." _</p>
        <p>___ • ____. "And forgot all about it !" cried Sue. "So like a man! If a lover is only a proper lover, he ought to write every day." "That would be too much to expect," said DMA, with a sigh, "and you must remember that I am forbidden by my father to answer Will's letters." "What a shame! But for all that, Will ought to write daily."</p>
        <p>Dora paused, and looked at her companion. "I think be hes written at least one letter, Sue," this said quietly, "and I believe that it has been inter- cepted by Mrs. Bedowen." "What cheek !". said Sue, again reverting to the use of .he brothers' =fettered speech. "Why should she do that?"</p>
        <p>"Because she knows that my father does no 6 approve of this marriage. Ever since she came here five years ago she has been devoted to my father. I believe he knew her 'when. she Was girl. He trusts her more than he trusts anyone ekse, and I feel certain that he has asked her to keep all eye on me. If so, she is bound to have kept back my letter,--that is if one came; and I am sure Will has written. The other morning I caught Mrs. Bedowen looking at me in that stealthy triumphant manner she assnunes whenever she has done anything she considers clever."</p>
        <p>- - "Wt will she do with the letter if She has it?" asked Sue.</p>
        <p>"Show it to my father. Then there will be trouble."</p>
        <p>Sue clenched her fist, as though desirous—as she certainly was—of hitting Mrs. Bedowen. "I never liked her; never! She is one of those motherly tabby cats, who look so demure, yet who can scratch so 'hard. Why, there she is at the window. Ah I speak of the she-devil and " Sue Sue! You really must moderate your language. She is beckoning us to come in to tea. It is in my mother's room." "And Mrs. Bedowen beckons—a servant What oheek !"</p>
        <p>"I hardly knew if she is my father's servant or his friend," said Miss Tremewan with a shrug; "she certainly takes extraordinary liberties which he never resents. Come in and have something to eat, Sue. I klAll sure you are hungry. And Mrs. Bedowen won't be in the room to spoil your appetite." " It would take an army of such as her to do that," said Sue, as they entered the house. "I wish she had my father to Ideal with! But no 1 I don't want to see father hanged." The girls with their arms round one .a.nother's waists repaired to Mrs. Tremewan's private sit- ting-room. In contrast to the. rest of the house, which was bare, it was luxuriously furnished and decorated. Mr. Tremewan idolised his frail wife, and thlught nothing too good for her. The room was as beautiful as wealth and taste and love could make it. Tea was laid out here as Mrs. Tre- anewan objected to exchange the warm atmosphere of her own apartment tor the colder one of the dining-room. For this meal was not what is usually known as afternoon tea, but one peculiar to the house, which dated from Georgian times. It was like a dinner of one course with tea instead of wine, and Sue with sparkling eyes beheld a table well covered with delicate food- She was usually on short commons when at home—the Colonel did not believe in women gorging—and the dainty fare of Mrs. Tremewan's table, where she was able to eat as much as she liked, was a Lord Mayor's banquet to her. She oltly wished the boys could have, been present. Bat even that well-spread table would not have sufficed for the five hungry_ Gillard lads.</p>
        <p>'"How do you do Mrs-. -Treme.wan?" said Sue, awkwardly kissing the faded cheek of that martyr</p>
        <p>"I exist; that is a," sighed Mrs` Tremewan, returning to her sofa by the fire. "But I feel wretched—my nerves, you know." She did know, as he had heard often enough about those same, ,nprves. But she could not comprehend the sufferings of Mrs. Tremewan, and did not believe that she suffered at all,—per- thaps because she possessed no nerves herself to speak of. Nerves were not allowed in the Gillard household '; the Colonel did not permit women .to be fanciful. Finding no answer to make, Sue sighed, which did just as well, and then sat down to tell Mrs. Treniewan the latest gossip. That martyr listened with the most profound attention, as she loved to hear scandal, and occasionally just to vary the subject she 'found fault with Dora's way of making .the tea. 'lt is not the may I should snake it, Dora," she sighed, "and my way is the right One. But you will never learn, never. What did you say' about Mrs. Lazby, Sue? Fifteen pounds for that dress, and with her husband's small salary! I call it simply criminal." Mrs. Tresnewan was tall and slender, and still beautiful. Her hair was yet golden, her skin almost without a wrinkle, and she took the great- est Care to preserve the good looks. which her husband admired so much. She was a passionless negative woman, with the instinct of a cat for warmth and idleness. Her vanity was enormous, and had been fostered by a husband who adored the very ground she walked on. Mrs. Tremewan quite believed that if it had not been for her nerves, she would have been a modern Cleopatra. She often told her husband that she was foolish to bury her beauty in a dull Devonshire town for Jove of him, and clever as Francis Tremewan we in many ways he actually believed this fiction. His infatuation for this golden-haired doll amounted almost to a disease.</p>
        <p>For the most part of the day, Mrs. 'rremewan lay on a luxurious couch near ,the fir p dressed in a dainty tea-gown, and with her golden hair becomingly dressed. the read a great many novels, all the ladies' papers-so as to keep herself abreast of the fashions, and at times indulged in kitting and fancy work. She had covered every chair with antimacassars, and had draped every mantel-shelf with impossible embroideries. "One must do one's work in this !life," she would say with a faint smile. "I wish to make my dear husband's home look beautiful.," And she was under the delusion that her scraps of fancy-work were worth untold god. The pathetic thing was, that in the eyes of Tremewan, they certainly were.</p>
        <p>"1 ,shall not come to the table Dora," sighed Mrs. Tremewan wearily; it was part of her business in life to be constantly weary. "My nervw are really dreadful to-day. You and Sue sit in. A cup of tea and a very very small cake dear. I have no appitite," and she sighed. "II you would oriy walk on the terrace mother," said Dora, pouring out the tea, very strong tea for martyr with nerves:</p>
        <p>" Walk on the terrace—and on 41 day like this! Do you want to ka me, you unnatural child? It is all I can do to keep alive. I envy your rude health Dora, and your mother's health Sue. She can. work."</p>
        <p>"She would hear about it if she „did not," replied Sue gloomily; "*we don't keep a servant, and mother and I have to slave f" " You Should be content. Sue," reproved Mrs. Tremewan, languidly. "I dis:ike t-eeing a young girl discontented. No sugar, Dora—you know how bad it is for me. I had to give it up for my health. And the very pktinest piece of cake, dear. • Ali 1" sighed the 6ady,:, when she was sup- plied,, "how much I 'have, to give up to keep myself fairly wel. I often ;yonder that lam not deal. Nothing amuses me now," and to prove the truth of this she asked Sue for the latest news. "The new doctor has arrived," replied Miss Gillard, spreading clotited cream on a large Ace of bread with a lavish hand; "he is young and good-looking. That's 'jolly! I .do wish a few young men like him would setVe here, and get up a dance; I am just dying to dance in a white dress widit a pearl necklace—if I could get it," she added, incoherently. "But this hole, is so dull. I don't know how I am ever (going to get married. Still Dr. Paticcx) is a beginning—l mean he is a young man, and others may come." Dora laughed. 'You may Inarry him, Sue. Is he a bachelor?",</p>
        <p>"Yes. That is the first thing I asked. No, it wasn't," said Sue, stopping to think. "I asked if he was (rood-locking, first. Mrs. Bennet said he was, so then I ersked if he was married." "I don't believe much in unmarried doctors," said Mrs. Tremewan, "they know so little about women as a rule."</p>
        <p>"This one knows a lot. Mrs. Bennet says he has wicked eye in his head. I haven't seen him yet." "Mrs. Bennet is a confirmed gossip," rebuked Dora.</p>
        <p>"She's a very good charwoman," retorted Sue. "I don't know how mother. and I would aloha us</p>
        <p>the house without her. Those von max, such u beastly mess. As to .goasip--this place is so dull, I like to hear the news. So does Mrs. Tremewa,n."</p>
        <p>"I take very little interest in anything," sighed the invalid untruthfully. "it was my duty to bury my,,zelf here with Mr. Tremewan, and I have never regretted the sacrifice. But lam glad tie hear that a new doctor has come. He may under- stand me. Pascoe, you soy his name is?" "John Pasooe," assented Sue. "I suppose his daunts call him Jack."</p>
        <p>"Perhaps you'll call him Jack yourself sores day, Sue, said Dora. "I don't think he'll look at me while you are about. dear. There is no beauty about me. for wear and tear. You take care, Dora, or Will liannaford will be jealous." Mrs. Tremewan raised herself up with more alacrity than her nerves warranted, and east an anxious look at the door. " Don't talk of young Rannaford here, Sue," she said, hurriedly; "if my husband heard you I don't know what he would say:, "I do," muttered, Sue, who had heard Tre- 111.01Vail swear. "Doet you like Will?" she asked</p>
        <p>aloud and rather "What is the use of my liking him?" replied Mrs. Tremewan, fretfully. "My husband does not approve of him. The trouble I have had over that wretched boy, though he is good-looking, as I never denied. But it is too bad he should by. Dora, and - _</p>
        <p>"Don't say anything more, mother—please." " -.411, that is so like you, Dora. You bring up a most disagreeable subject, and then want to stop it after my nerves are all unstrung." "It is my fault," said Sue, penitently. "But I thought you liked Will?" "He is a nice boy—but only a boy," returned the elder woman; "only I do wish in spite of his good looks that he had never come here. Hie father can't get on with Mr. Tremewan. sure I 'lim't know IVIly?", "Father doc3n't like Mr. Ilannaford," said Dora, looking .up with a shrug. Mrs. Treanewan flushed with a gratified vanity. " There is a reason for that," she anurmured. When I was a girl, he-----" here she broke off, for the eyes of her daughter were fixed upon her in a wondering manner. "I have not seen George Hannaford for years," she added, hurriedly. "I knew him—that is—" here Mrs. Tremewan again became confused, but was saved from further explanation by the en- trance of her husband. Her face grew a trifle pale as he appeared. She thought he might have heard her using the forbidden name.</p>
        <p>But if 'llremewan had, he gave no sign of his knowledge. With a fond smile on his hard face he .anoved quickly to the couch, and kissed the delicate hand of his wife. The two girls, rather embarrassed by these endearments, rose 'hurriedly. Don't move, Gilla,rd," said Tremewan, still hokling his wife's .hand, and speaking good- -humouredly. "I don't want to disturb. you." .` Will you ihave some tea, dear?" asked 'his wife languidly.</p>
        <p>I have sclaleithing to do. Don't rise, any own:" He made her lie down ,again ot(he couch. ." You are not strong enough. It is Dora I want."</p>
        <p>"Do you .want me, father?" asked Dora quietly. While speating to Sue, Tremewan had been polite; when attending to his wife he had been demonstratively affectionate. But when he ad- dressed his daughter his face grew dark, and his voice became harsh. "I do want you," he said Aeruly; "come to my study." Then his tone altered again as he bent over his wife. "I'll return to receive a cup of tea from these dear hands," he mid, and kissing them ,agaiji left the room.</p>
        <p>Dora faltered and looked at her mother. But Mrs. Treniewan 'took not the slightest notice of the mute appeal. Sae looked angry, but she could do nothing. "1 wilt see you bsfore you Sue," said 'Dora quietly, and went away to aye wile* she felt would .be an unpleasant interview.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>Good ':44eginning</head>
        <p>A Good ':44eginning</p>
        <p>is half the battle, so see that you begin your day well by taking a cup of Van Houten's Cocoa at breakfast time. It is a nourishing and delicious beverage, strengthening the body for the coming fatigues of the day, and fortifying the nerves against the strain of the rush and tear of this work-a-day world.</p>
