      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>FERGUS HLTME.</head>
        <p>FERGUS HLTME.</p>
        <p>Author of "The Mystery of a Hansom Cab." " The Pagan's Cup," "The Crime of the Crystal," "Woman—the Sphinx," etc., etc., etc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CHAPTER L</head>
        <p>CHAPTER L</p>
        <p>FIVE YEARS LATER.</p>
        <p>Like that city mentioned in the Bible, Tr+ znewan's house was set upon a hill. The =asks was eccentric in appearance, in situation still mord so, Which was ,quite in keeping with the reputa- tion of the family as "u queer kit." This square three-storey building, with its bare pay walls and barred windows, resembled a gaol. A Trentewan of Georgian times had built it as a fottress, for h. was suspected of being a smuggler, and there was a probabi.lity that the house might be besieged by meddlesome preventive officers. And sure enough the siege took plaice ; but, thanks to the thick walls, the muskets of the defenders, and the friendly sympathy of the neighbours, it deservedly failed. After that one attempt to vindicate the majesty of the law, the Tremewans were left severely alone.</p>
        <p>_ Confident that they could hold their own, as had been proved by the siege, the Tremewans had never enclosed their property. Even the acres im- mediately round the house were open, and the house itself was dumped down in a clump of firs, as though it had been dropped there by a careless genie. It stood unprotected, with the gorse growing up to the very turrace upon which it was built, and a flagged way—useful in wet weather—running, from front door to the high road. This last ran straight across the common, and was the means of communication between Dart- bridge and the world beyond. On the brow of the hill, and a few yards past The Gray House— as Tremewan's mansion was called—it ceased to be a highway, and became a High Street. Had it been called Steep Street the name would have beem more appropriate, for it sloped precipitously between quaint slate-roofed houses to a square of fiat land at its foot. This, terminated by the Dartbridge estuary, was called The Quay, and here dwelt the descendants of the smugglers, now as poor as their forefathers had been rich. Illxirty or forty years before the town was thronged with people, and the Pool. with craft. In those days fruit schooners ran as far as the Azores, and on the fruit trade depended the prosperity of Dart- bridge. But steamboats and railways diverted the traffic, and ended the fat years. The inhabi- tants lost heart and settled down to a bovine exis- tence, as dull as that of the Dotes-Eaters, and much lees poetical. And through all the years from prosperity to adversity the grim house looked down on the despondent town. It was inhabited by Francis Tremewan, his wife, and his daughter Dora. Also, if authority went for anything, Mrs. Bedowen, the housekeeper, was to be reckoned. She managed everyone and every- thing, save her master, and even he appeared to be a trifle afraid of her. Yet it seemed ridiculous that such a strong, black-bearded man should be afraid of anyone. The present representative of the Tremewans looked fierce enough to be a pirate, much lees a smuggler. In these peaceful times he was neither, bat his temper was so violent that amongst the good-natured Devordane he was like a tiger in a sheepfold. No man in the parish was so dreaded and hated as Francis Tro- mewan. _ _</p>
        <p>Dora as thinking of the unpopularity of her father, as she walked on the terrace- To this there was no balustrade, and the gorse grew right up to what was little else than a stone shelf. Seen from this vantage-point, the country looked like a gigantic chess board, so clivided it was by hedges into parti-coloured squares. In the folds of the hills gleamed the grey waters of the estuary, and the hills themselves mingled so confusedly with the misty sky that no definite horizon was discern- ible. Immediately below lay the town, a huddled mass of slate-coloured houses, dull and unpictu- resque. A cutting east wind was blowing, and al- though at times came a gleam of sunshine, yet the day was particularly typical of the month. A raw, chilly grey January day, and not unlike her own desolate life, as Dora thought, For a wonder, she was not alone. Beside her walked a red-haired girl whose homely fea- tures were redeemed from positive ugliness by a good-natured smile which displayed very white teeth. Sue Gillard was one of those useful people who go through life bearing the burdens of others and getting small thanks for doing so. Already she had commenced, for her father Colonel Gillazd was poor and selfish. His weary wife shared with her only daughter the difficult task of rearing five unruly boys on nothing a year. It was rarely that poor 'Sue could snatch a holiday, as the Colonel thought .women were happiest when at work. Bull when she was .so fortunate, she invariably spent the precious hours with Dora. Then, as on the present occasion, she talked over the one romance of Dora's life. An affair of the heart, of course. ." Quite like 'a three volume novel," said Sue_, hanging on fondly to Dora's arm; "you love Will Hanna.ford with a love which will carry you to the silent tomb."</p>
        <p>I hope not," replied Dora hastily, smiling at ithe extravagance of this speech. "Will does nob •want me to, seek the tomb on his account. How you do exaggerate, Sue dear! My romance, es you. call it, is .very sirn:!e. Will and I love one another, but our resi.k.utive parents do not approVe 44 our marriage." ".1 should defy .my cruel father and fly to the arms Of :Inv own true knight. Fire and water would not keep me from his side." 'Perhaps common sense would, my dear. Will'a tatter is, quite a.s much opposed to the marriage ea aline is. if the poor boy defied that cold un- bending Mr. .Idannalord ;he .would.be cut off with a shilling." "What of that?" urged the .romantic Sue. "When your savage father dies you will have heaps and heaps of nioney:" "I'm not so sure of that. His fortune will be left to my mother. Ido not •understand why you should cal .my father savage, Sue." "All fathers are savage," replied Sue decisively, "that is, all those I know. Yours; Will's; and mine. Bears and pigs every one .of them 1" "Sue, you really must .not let your tongue run away with you." "I'd let anyone or anything run away with me, if I could only make up my mind to leave poor dear mother, Dora.. My lather is getting too awful for words. Don't .let us talk of him 1" she added, coaxingly, ".or I shall waste my holiday in swooning on the terrace. How is your mother? But need .I ask? She's. ill. Nerves, of course, and no one can understand her sufferings." "She is not very well," replied Dora, half smiling at this very correct description of Mrs. Tremewan's daily cry. "We'll go in soon, and you can see her for vounself.." Humph I" said Slue, not allured by this pros- pect. "I tell ,you what, Dora," she continued with surprising suddenness, "you oughtn't to wait too For what dear?" asked Miss Tremewan ab- sently.</p>
        <p>"}or Will. Your mother and father are so taken up with one another that they have no love to give you. Now, Will has, and he wants to make you young Mrs. Hannaford. Besides the poor dear went to London six. weeks ago to make his for- tune,—out of noveLs, wa.sn't let"</p>
        <p>"Will does hope to become famous as aa author," said Dora, sighing. "Then he'll make. no end of money," answered Sue, decisively, as though the - question were settled, "and he can come back to marry you. If the parents," she said spitefully, "don't like it, the parents can lump it.' "Sue, how dreadfully vulgar! Some of the boys' slang?" "Sue nodded. I always use it when I get ex- cited. It is better to copy the boys' slang than father's language. If I used his words." Sue lifted up her hands and waggled them in the air, "Well ' —words failed her, and she .crewed up her mouth to express her . inability to describe the Colonel's command of epithets. Dora. said no- thing, and they walked the length of the terrace in silence.' Then the irrepressible Miss Gillard burst out again. eoh !if I had a lover how I athouletrust the poor darling !" she cried, irrele- vantly.</p>
        <p>• ".Aly dear, Will and I understand atm another perfectly. We shall always be true to one another. But in the face of family opposi- tion —"</p>
        <p>" Bother opposition! It's meant to be over- come."</p>
        <p>"'Mat might he at the cost of ruin to both. We had better Wait. 1311f1-"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CHAPTER IL</head>
        <p>CHAPTER IL</p>
        <p>MRS. lIEDOViEN'S OFFER.</p>
        <p>Tremewan was not precisely unkind to his daughter, but he treated her with a chill courtesy, which the learned:carted girl found worse than down-right bad temper. Even as a child she had never received moll -affection from him. All the love he possessed was given to her mother, and he appeared to .censider his daughter an encum- brance. If he had only waxed angry with Dora on oceasions she fe!t• that she could have borne it better, hut h.e was always as coldly polite as though she were a steel-neer. The girl was much perplexed by this behaviour. On one occasion she had asked her mother for an explanation, but had received no answer :ikely to enlighten her. Mrs. Bedowen, who apparently knew everything, might have informed her of the reason, if reason there were; but Dora mistrusted the housekeeper too much to make a in:end of her. In. the cold in- different (atmosphere of the house the girl was very unhappy. he knew from the mote in her father's voice that she was about to have an unpleasant interview, and followed him to hi.s study with inward quaking. Habit, however, had steeled her into concealing her feelines. The study was barely furnished, and contained none of the 'elegancies of decoration and furniture such as made Mrs. Tremewan's sitting-room habitable. The walls were lined with old books mostly of a re'igious tendency, for the Tremewans had at one time been fervent 'followers of Wesley. Between the bookcaeee, stuffed birds with glass eyes glared coldly down, and here and the're was a steel-engraving of one of Martin's 'terrible pic- tures. In a corner stook a green-painted safe, in another a washstand, and before the window a desk piled with papers in the most untidy manner. Three old-fashioned leather-covered chairs formed the rest of the furniture, and these stood on a small square a red fe'Jt. The light which revealed this charming apartment came from a French window, looking towards the high road, and opening directly' on to the Common. This was usually closed at night with iron shuttens, but to-day these were still open. The lamp on the desk diffused a dull yellow light, and made the window from within appear a black le!aaak square, wihieh Dora, in her then state Of mind; found stuffier trying. The fireplace was a litter of torn papers, and evidently a 'fire had not been seen behind its rusty bars for many a long day. Th'ere was no need for Tremewan to occupy so comfort- less an apartment; but he ,had a habit of martyr- ising himself in this especial way. Some people said that it was on 'account of an uneasy conscience; but the master of The Gray House did not .look like a man who would be troubled with a con- science, either good Or bad. • -1,1 masterful man was Tremewan, and not popular. - -</p>
        <p>He sat,down at his-de-sk and turned up the lamp. When Dora had dosed :the door, and was standing ready to hear what he had to say, he opened the interview by pointing to a letter which lay before him on the red blotting paper. She bent forward and recognised the handwriting at once. Never- theless she remained calm.</p>
        <p>"It is from Will Hannaford," the eaid quietly, arid waited.</p>
        <p>"So I guessed,". replied her father, with icy politeness. "Will you be so good as to tell me why you have set my orders at defiance?"</p>
        <p>" I have not done so. As to this letter "—Alio paused and with as proud a look as her fathet could assume, faced him badly," Mrs. Bedowen g-ave it to you I suppose?" "Never you mind." _ _</p>
        <p>Dora clenched her hands. "But I do mind,* (the answered. "I am your daughter after all, and I ihave a right to some sort of respect in this house. How dare a servant stop my letter and give it to you?" "If any servan,t did such a thing she would be discharged," said her :father. "Even Mrs. Bedowen?"</p>
        <p>"She is not a common servant," replied Tre- inewan, shirking a direct answer, "and as it happens she did mot give me the letter. I found it on the haV_-table, and recognising the writing I kept it so that I might ask you wiry it came." "Ai I have not read it I can hardly say," said Dora, coldly. _ _</p>
        <p>" You know what I mean. You cor- respond with this man." " No doubt you have opened .the letter and know that."</p>
        <p>Tremewan politely Landed her the letter. a You .can see for yourself that the seal is un- broken," he said. "I lam not the one to real your correspondence, even though you have dis- obeyed me." "I: lave not done so. You can read this letter ROW if you choose." She ;hurriedly tore the en- velope and handed the inside sheet to her father. "If Will chooses to write to me I cannot stop him: nor would I if I could. I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <p>teeter in my room. You oan read that also. But in neither will you find that I have been acting 'contrary to your express order. You should know aie better, father." • "Have I your permission to read this letterr asked Tremewan, without noticing her appeal. On receiving a bow in the ,affirmative, he glanced over the three sheets, holding them close to the tamp that he might see the more clearly. Dora sank into a chair, clasping together her hands in an agony of anxiety. She felt how shameful it was that her lover's letter should be thus desecrated. Perhaps her father felt so also, for the read no more than the first page. Handing it back to her with a bow, he apologised. "I am sorry," he said; "there is nothing in this which leads me to think that you have disobeyed me. So far that is all right, Dora. But you must understand that I will not allow you to throw yourself away on this man." I should like to know your reason," she said, quietly. "I should think all Dartbridge knew it," re- plied Tremewan, the veins of his forehead swell- ing with suppressed wrath. "I don't dislike the toy. There is nothing wrong about him. But us father—l hate him. I would sooner see you ia TOUT grave than married to the son of that man, George Hannaford. We have been enemies foe years, and shall be to the end. Yes," cried -Tre- mewan, bringing down his fist on the desk, "tø the bitter end r</p>
        <p>"W4iy do you hale tint so, faeher?"</p>
        <p>_ Por-a reason which; if you will pardon an saying so, does not conceal you. And even if I allowed the marriage' Han ch nafoad would not con- sent. He is as mu against it as I am. Read the letter and see what obstacles he has put in the .way of your love." Dora hurriedly glanced at the letter, and on mastering its contents looked again at her father who was moodily staling at his desk. "Will says in this ,ffiat tMT. Hannaford ihas threatened to dis- inherit him if he comes back to Darthridge with- out permission." Tremewan laughed bitterly. "You see! Hanna- ford is against you also," he said. Rather than Will should marry you he will cut him off with the proverbial shilling. "And I would lather not marry Will if his devotion to me has to cost such a price," said Dora, steadily; "you must allow me to write and tell him so, father." "And you will also vow to wait for years !" sneered her father. "oth, I know your silly girlish romance. Well, You can write. Will may as well know that you intend to obey me." "He has known that all along," replied, Dora, with a bitterness foreign to her nature; "he wanted me to run away with him, but I refused. I love him too well to risk his becoming a pauper for my sake. But," she added, ring proudly, "if Mr. Hann.aford consented, I would marry him to-morrow."</p>
        <p>"And disobey me," said her father, darkly. "Have you done so im;uch to gain my affection?" cried the girl, passionately. "All my life I have been an outcast from your heart. Why, Ido not know. If I married Will I should at least have someone to love and cherish me, and that-I have never known--here."</p>
        <p>"You are hyisterioal," said Tremewan, rising also, and inot a whit moved by her outburst; "you have a good home and money, and all that a girl can want. What more do you-" "The tender love of a father," she put in.</p>
        <p>Trem.ewan shrugged his shoulders, and without • reply walked to the door. "I am not a man to show sentimental affection," he said, as he held it open. "I protect you, allow you money, and give you your position as my daughter. That must suffioe. If I have been too harsh I beg your pardon. I have every wish to treat you well. Please let this subject be closed between us," and he left the room. Dora sank into the chair he lead vacated, and wept bitterly. Her position was terribly lonely. Neither loather nor father cared for her, and the man who could have given that affection her starved heart desired was far away. To come to her, he would be obliged to risk the loss of his inheritance, and this Dora did not desire him to do. But she won- dered why everyone was so cruel. "And lam so lonely—so lonely," she moaned. A slight touch caused her to spring to her feet. IShe was face to face with Mrs. Bedowen, and the comely housekeeper had an eager expression of curiosity tin her black eyes. "I hope you are well, Miss," she said, looking searchingly into the tear- stained face. "There is nothing wrong, I hope? Your father Dora started back. She neither Eked no: trusted this woman. "There is nothing wrong, thank you," she replied, coldly. "Please leave we."</p>
        <p>But Mrs. Bedowen was not to be so easily dis- missed. She was a placid and motherly-looking woman, quietly dressed in black silk, with a white lace cap. Her blue eyes were still clear, and there were but few streaks of grey in iher black hair. Rubbing her plump hands one over the other, she looked at ie girl keenly as though to read, her very sou&amp; Coca coloured under that intense gaze, a.nd lowered her eyes. It is not always the good woman who can outstare the bad one. "You are angry with me, Miss Dora?" said the housekeeper in wheedling tone. "I assure you that I did not intercept the letter." "How do you know I said you did?" asked the girl quickly. "From your manner, Miss. You are not fond of me. I know that. You never were, but at lea.st we have been good friends on the whole. But now I see that you are hostile. I saw a letter_for you in • your father's Jhands, and knew that he wished to speak to you about it. I guessed from that it was Mr. Will's letter, and I guessed, also, that you would think that.l had taken it."</p>
        <p>" I know you did not. My father said so." "Then you did think I took it," sneered Mrs</p>
        <p>Bedowen</p>
        <p>"I did," admitted Dora quietly, I thought you wished to ,curry ,flavour ivith imy father." Mrs. Bedowen's eyes flashed with an unpleasant light. "I do not think you should speak to me in that manner, Miss," she said, evidently deeply offended. "I am only a servant; but I have my feelings. Ido not wish to curry favour with any- one, but to do my duty. That is all. If your father is not satisfied with my servi.ces he can dis- pense with them at any moment. I stay here because he wiShes it, not from my own desire." "I beg your pardon," said Dora, feeling that she had been unjust. "I am sorry if I spoke harshly; but lam much worried. Let us say no more." And she moved towards the door: to intimate that the interview was at an end. But Mrs. Bedowen was not so easily dismissed. She bad an end to gain, and she intended to gain it in the face of all discouragurnent. Without deli- cacy herself, she scoffed at the idea that it should tte possessed by others; and accordingly persisted in forcing her .company on Dora. . "Don't be in such a hurry, Miss," she said, coolly, "You make a. mistake in casting me off like this. I want to be your friend, if you will let me. And," she added, looking significantly at the girl, "I can assure you that my friendship is worth having." "I do not doubt it. But so far as I am con- cerned you must permit me to decline your offer% We can ne,hr be friends, Mrs. Bedowen " "Why not, Miss Dora"</p>
        <p>"I can give o rea-son likely to satisfy you. But since you seem determined that I shall give one, I can only say that I do not think we should ever get on together."</p>
        <p>"rain sorry- you should look upon me as an enemy," muttered the other, and her plump hands busied themselves with the frills of her apron. "I certainly do not. There is no reason that I ould be your enemy. Do not let us misunder- stand one another. 'Me difference between our natures is so great that it is impossib,:e for us to be friends in the way you wish. But there is no seed that we should be enemies." Mrs. Bedowen till persisted. "I could do much. tor you, Miss. I could even induce your father so let you marry young Mr. Hantraford." Dora was almost too indignant- to reply to this piece of impertinence. However, she kept her temper. "Probably," she rePliped, with chill emphasis. "I do not know the limit ,of your power with any father. Nevertheless, you can have no influence with Mr. Hanna-ford, senior." Dora was annoyed at herself for making this last remark, but it Came out before she had time to consider its wisdom. Nor was she pleased at Bedowen's prompt reply. "Don't you be too sure of that, Miss."</p>
        <p>ii;-nojt understand you." 7 • "There is ILO need .for that," replied the woman, iiheerfully "Accept me as your friend, and—you will get your wish." "What I do ,wish r what I do not wish has nothing to do with you. As you will act take a hint, Mrs. Eedowen, permit me to speak plainly. I am quite competent to manage my own affairs, said I can dispense even With your table assistance." These bitter words made Dora an enemy. They Were unditiontatict af,,,,be wrz:, wdlt bn4,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <p>In the 4ace of the housekeeper's persistence miss: Tremeinan was unable to see how else she could answer. !Besides she was at a loss to conjecture why Mrs. Bedowen should behave in this way. Hitherto. be had shown am disposition to be friondly, and for five years She had been in the house. There was no apparent reason that she should coms. forward now, and press her attentions. But what- ever the reason' Mrs. ‘Bedowen saw that she could not gain What she wanted—at all events for the present. She resolved to try later, being a woman, of wonderful tenacity of purpose. In the mean- time she drew back with an ironical ourtesy.</p>
        <p>"I will say no more, Miss," she oliserved,_ quietly, "and you need never fear that I shall trouble you again." Here she folded her hands and. Looked prim. "I apologise, Miss, for. having stepped out of my position. Put it down to zeal if you pease." Dora bowed, and opened the dear. There was nothing more to say, and she wished to leave this troublesome woman as speedily as was possible. Mrs. Bedowen remained where he was, smiling: demurely with her eyes modestly cast down. if she was angry she Showed no signs of it. Won- glering stila more at this strange behaviour, Dom passed into the hall folding up Will's letter. It then occurred to her that Sue was waiting her return in the sitting-room, and thither the went. Long experience had taught Dora haw to control the outward expression of her feelings, and when. she re-entered her mother's room, she was per- fectly calm. No one would have guessed that she bad been so agitated by her lather, and so per- Plated by his housekeepet</p>
        <p>Sue was not in the room. Mrs. Tremewan Si lay on the soh.' and beside her low,lt her husband hold*" her hand. He turned with a frown when his daughter entered, but did not ehange hit position.</p>
        <p>" What is it?" he asked quietly. "I.am looking ..for Sue, father."</p>
        <p>, "She has gone," replied Mrs. Tremeiran quickly. "I really oould not stand aer chatter any longer Dora. My nerves are particularly bad to-day. don't 'think she is a very intelligent girl eather. ?he will talk about herself, and I do dislike that personal note. To a woman of my capability. Dora, it is particularly unpleasant." Then the next time Sue comes, mother, yoga need not see her."</p>
        <p>But Mrs. Tre.mewan was not prepared to go thus far. "Oh !if my company gives her pleasure I really do not mind putting myself out. One should always do one's duty even when it is dis- agreeable. 1 " Amy ! Amy !" said her husband, gently. "You really must not excite yourself. Lie down and rest. I will do the talking. Dora?" "I need not wait," replied the girl, seeing in that interrogation a desire that she should depart. "Ring when yon want me, mother." "So ridiculous !" were the last words Dora caught, as she closed the door, and they came from her another. "Ring for her!! Just as. though I looked upon my own child as a servant. But that is so like Dora."</p>
        <p>The girl could have laughed. Her mother waC so absolutely blind to everything but her omit feelings that the could not see how selfishly she -was behaving. Dora felt that had she indee4 been a servant she would have been better offc since in thich a position she could have at least earned her board and lodging. At present she felt that both her parents regarded her as little better than an encumbrance. As she was banished from the sitting-room, and was too weary to again walk on the terrace, she resolved to go to her bedroom. It was her usual refuge when troubled, as there at least she was left undisturbed. When paling towards the. stairs She saw a letter on the hall table. It was addressed to Mrs. Bedowen in red ink in an illiter- ate hand. Wondering wham it was from—al- though it !was none of her business—Dora was about to lay it down When Mrs. Bedoweu, stiLl smiling, entered from, the study. "For you I think," said Miss Tremewan, anct banded the letter to the housekeeper. Hardly had she got to the top of the stairs when she heard a kind of shriek, and looked over the railing to see Mrs. Beclowen drop on the floor. The letter was open in her hand. Thinking she was taken with a fit, Dora ran down to her assistance. But before She could reach her, the house- keeper, in spite of her stoutness, was on her feet again. Her face was ashen, and her lips white. She looked over and past, Dora, and stumbled out of the hall, panting. "That devil---that devil The dead are alive-. at7.ive I" _ -</p>
        <p>[To' be Continued.]</p>
      </div>
