      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>THE MEMBER FOR DUBLIN UNIVERSITY.</p>
        <p>As foretold some months ago, the Right Hon. IV. E. H. Lecky, M.P. for Dublin University, has resigned his seat immediately upon the opening of Parliament, accepting from the •Chancellor of the Exchequer the formal post of "Steward and Bailiff of the Chiltern Hundreds." Mr. Leckey's resignation and the cause which necessitated it will be regretted on all sides, and by it the House of Commons loses one of its most distin- guished mem- bers. Infrequent though his speeches were, they were always highly scholarly, and raised the tone of any de- bate in which he took part. His career in Parlia- ment has been short, as it only lasted eight years. In 1895, upon the elevation of Mr. Plunkett to the peerage, Mr. Lecky was elected by a majority of 746 as member for Dublin Univer- RT. HON. W. E. H. LECKY, sity, and since M.P. then he has re- WHO HAS RESIGNED HIS SEAT tamed the un- broken tenure of his seat. His 'reputation, however, is not that of a Parliamentarian so much as that of an hi4torian, and Mr. Lecky, who is a Privy Counellor' has obtained a higher place in literature than any Irishman of the past century. His best known works are "Leaders of Public Opinion," published in 1861, "History of European Morals from Augustus to Charlemagne," History of England in the Eighteenth Century," " Demo- cracy and Liberty," and "The Map of Life." The contest for the vacancy created by this retirement lies between Mr. J. H. Campbell, the Solicitor- 'General for Ireland, and Mr. A. W. Samuels, K.C., both of whom are Unionists and supporters of the present Government and differ only on questions of Dublin University, the Church of England in Ireland and the Catholic University proposal. Thus the election will make no practical alteration in the balance of parties and will be.of purely local interest.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER DOOMED HISTORIC BIT OF LONDON.</p>
        <p>The famous old Quest House and "four Ahoppes " in Cripplegate, London, are to suffer the demolition that so many historic spots in the city have lately undergone. One by one, the links with the past that make London so interesting to the country visitor are disappearing as the result of modern exigencies, and though the latter must be admitted to be of more import than the mere sentiment that revolts at the destruction of these eloquent reminders of hygone days, there are not many Londoners who are so vandalic as not to experience some regret when it is made known that a link binding the old London to the new is to be swept away in the course of necessary improve- ments. Comparatively little is known of the Quest House itself though it is possible that on the site of the present building, which has existed St least a hundred years longer than the four</p>
        <p>THE OLD QUEST ROUSE AND "FOUR SHOPPES ' IN CRIPPLEGATE, LONDON. shops, there was a still earlier Quest House. Of -the four shops, however, we have a continuous history, in 1654 the Vestry of St. Giles found them- -Nivea in possession of a sum of £26o—representing then a much larger sum than those figures now do —as a "fine" for a lease of property belonging te the parish in Sugar Loaf Court. The vestry •decided to put this money to a benevolent use, that of building the "four shoppes " and devoting the rent to the support of the poor children of the Parish. The shops were ready in 1656, and pre- sumably the children benefited from them till 1830, when, for some reason, the vestry did not find it expedient to renew the lease, and the shopi Passed away from them. There was litigation over them between the new tenants, and eventually Private individuals derived such advantage as there was from their possession, the parish ceasing to benefit.</p>
