      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>GREAT JAP.</head>
        <p>A GREAT JAP.</p>
        <p>This week has seen the interment of one whom .Tapan could ill spare. He was Prince Komatsu, one of the ablest soldiers and statesmen that the Plucky little eastern state possessed. He was only fifty-seven years old, having been born on Februat y 11th, 1846. He was head of the House of Komatsu, one of the nine Princely families which constitute the Imperial House of Japan. Destined at first for the priesthood, he received his early education in the Temple of Ninnaji in Kioto. His progressive tastes, however, induced him to embrace a secular career, and at the age of twenty-two he was aPpointed commander of the troops operating against the Toku- gawa rebels. Three years later he obtained per- mission to visit Europe. He came to England, and</p>
        <p>was received with much dis- tinction by Queen Victoria. On his return to Japan he went through a regular' course of military train- ing on the new model. In 77 he commanded the Japanese Guards during the Satsuma re- THE LATE PRINCE KOMATSU bellion. In 1887 ' he again came to TEE FAMOUS SAPANF.SE SOLDIER AND STATESMAN. England, on this</p>
        <p>occasion acting as the official representative of the Mikado at Queen Victoria's •Jubilee. Previously to his departute from England he was invested with the Grand Cross of the Bath. During the epoch-making Chinese War he again •commanded the Japanese Guards, and towards the end of the operations the supreme command of the Zilikado's forces in the field was confided to him, With the rank of Field-Marshal. Returning home in triumph, he was appointed Chief of the Staff. but after a brief tenure of that post, he retired from the army and devoted himself to civilian occupations. He paid a third visit to England last :year as the representative of the Mikado at the King's Coronation.</p>
