      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>LORD YARMOUTH'S ENGAGEMENT.</head>
        <p>LORD YARMOUTH'S ENGAGEMENT.</p>
        <p>The union of Yankee dollars, sometimes coupled with beauty, and British peerages is nowadays a subject of no surprise, but rather an expected event, when an Englishman of title, but no means, makes a trip across the "herring- pond," In fact, the American papers, secretly pleased, are pretending to be highly indignant at the rebbery of these eminently eligible ladies by preelatory peers. The latest lady, who will shortly be added to the list of American pcasesses, is 3,115 s Alice Thaw. of Washington, who is to marry the Earl of Yarmouth, eldest son of the Marquis of Hertford. He is one of the young peers who has of recent years kept himself, or been kept, well in the .public eye, sin c.e the days when he gyrated grace- fully as a skirt-dancer onwards. He has been out in America for the last two or three years, ap- pearing from time to time on the stage as "Mr. Eric Hope," and many rumours of Yankee heiresses, who have accepted or refused him, have been</p>
        <p>rife ever since. big last it is definitely an. nounced that Miss Alice Thaw has consented to become the next Marchioness of Hertford, after several contradictions of the report. Miss Alice Thaw is one of the reat beakties of Washington, and an heiress of no small calibre to boot. Her father, who made his pile as an iron manufacturer, died ten years ago, leaving each of his children a million dollars, and his wife ten times as much, which will in due course come to them. How- mlss ALICE THAW, ever, until re- WHO WILL MARRY LORD YARMOUTH. cently it is said that Mrs. Thaw has been opposed to the match; but lately her great mansion in Washington has been very gay with dances and entertainments of all sorts, to which Lord Yarmouth was invited as the boon com- panion of Mr. Harry Thaw, a young gentleman who distinguished himself in Paris by some notable supper parties. Now all has ended happily, like an old romance, instead of a modern one, and Miss Thaw will become Countess of Yarmouth within the next few months.</p>
        <p>LIO ; -,.._ :Arfjc. ' it? (c-p,"• c. 3</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>FIGHT FOR WOOLWICH.</head>
        <p>THE FIGHT FOR WOOLWICH.</p>
        <p>For some time it has been common property that Lord Charles Beresford, M.P. for Wool- wich, would not long be kept off the active list; and now that his appointment to the command of the Channel Squadren has come, it mceisi- tates. his immediate retirement from Parliament under the rules of the service. This means that towards the end of the first fortnight in March this - con- stituency will see a stiff tight be- tween a Con,terva7 tive nd a LAbour candidate. For years Woolwich has been looked upon as a Con- servative strong- hold, ,and a safe seta, and upon the retirement of Colonel ll:pyrites last spring, Lord</p>
        <p>Charle',Ber'esford</p>
        <p>MR. WILLIAM CROOKS, stepped n nop- WHO IS OPPOSING MR. G. DRAGE IN posed _ into his</p>
        <p>THE WOOLWICH VACANCY. shoes. Now,how- ever, Mr. Wifliam Crooks, one of the leading men of the Labour party, has decided to throw down the gauntlet to Mr. Geoffrey Drage, the Conservative candidate who, though a well-known man' does not carry the weight of popularity behind him that Lord Charles Beresford did. Again, as the majority of the voters in the constituency are labourers, prin- cipally at the Arsenal, it gives a tremendous opening for a Labour Candidate.</p>
