      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>LATE BISHOP OF SOUTHAMPTON.</head>
        <p>THE LATE BISHOP OF SOUTHAMPTON.</p>
        <p>The late Right Rev. Arthur Temple Lyttelton Bishop of Southampton, whose death has just occurred, was a member of a family which has been prominent in many directions in rendering service to the State. His father, fourth Baron Lyttelton, was a scholar of great attainments, who in the course of his career was Under Secretary for the Colonies and Chief Commissioner of Endowed Schools. Lord Lyttelton and Mr. Gladstone married, in 1839, the daughters of Sir Stephen Richard Glynne, the eighth baronet. Lady Ly ttel- died in 1857, leaving a numerous family, who, besides maintain- ing the traditions</p>
        <p>or the house in</p>
        <p>Church and State have also greatly</p>
        <p>distingu—ishe-d</p>
        <p>themselves in the cricket field and in other forms of</p>
        <p>The }lon. Temple who</p>
        <p>sport Arthur Lyttelton,</p>
        <p>w.as destined to</p>
        <p>reach the ranks of</p>
        <p>Episcopacy,</p>
        <p>was born in Lou.</p>
        <p>don on January 7th, 1852, and as</p>
        <p>a lad was from 1861 till 1468 a THE LATE RT. REV. ARTHUR Page of Honour to Her late</p>
        <p>TEMPLE LrrIELTON, D.D.</p>
        <p>laSllol' OF SO uTuAm PTON,</p>
        <p>Majesty. He was</p>
        <p>Beni to Eton, and</p>
        <p>from Eton was entered at Trinity College, Cambridge, where he obt:dned honours. After graduating, he went into residence at Cuddesdon College, and in 1876 was ordained by the late Dr. Mackarness, Bishop of Oxford, and licensed to the curacy of St. Mary the Virgin, Reading. Here he remained for three years, when, in 1879, he was elected tutor and lecturer at Keble College, Oxford, of which his brother-in-law, Dr. E. S. Talbot, present Bishop of Rochester. was then Warden. His stay here lasted till 1882. In 1898 be was made Provost of S. Nicholas College, Lancing, and consecrated third Bishop of Southampton as Suffragan for the diocese of Winchester, in suc- cession to Dr. G. C. Fisher, now Bishop of Ipswich. For the last two years he had been Archdeacon of Winchester.</p>
