      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ROYAL SILVER WEDDING.</head>
        <p>A ROYAL SILVER WEDDING.</p>
        <p>The Hereditary Prince and Princess of Saxe- Meiningen who have just celebrated their silver wedding may be said, without exaggeration, to be one of the happiest married couples, not only in Royalty, but throughout the entire realm of matrimony. The Princess is the eldest sister of the German Emperor, and consequently the eldest niece of King Edward. She was born in 1860, and was married on February 18th, 1878, to Prince Bernard of Saxe-Meiningen. There are few mem- bers ef the German reigning houses who are more popular than Prince Bernard and his accomplished wife. The Prince was born on April Ist, 1851, hie father being the reigning Duke of Sae-Meiningen, George 11., now in his seventy-seventh year. He, was educated first in the Gymnasium at Hildburg- hausen, and then at the University of Heidleberg. His stay at the University was cut short by the Franco-Prussian war. Prince Bernard had already</p>
        <p>T.R.H. THE HEREDITARY PRINCE AND PRINCESS</p>
        <p>OF SAXE-MEININGEN.</p>
        <p>been made a second lieutenant of Thuringian In- fantry when he was fifteen years old, and he at once joined his regiment to serve in the field. He fought at Worth, Sedan, Soigny-Poupry, Orleans, and Beaugency-Cravant, all hotly-contested battles, and his gallantry earned him the Iron Cross and other minor decorations. The war over, he returned to his studies at Heidelberg. Here he distinguished himself in languages, history, and literature, and took high honours in Greek. He also became an accomplished musician, and composed music to accompany several of the Greek plays. These com- positions have been frequently performed in public. At the same time he continued his military studies. In 1875 he became a captain of Fusilier Guards, and three years later, on his wedding day, he was promoted to Major and Staff Officer of the First Foot Guards. In May, 1882, he joined the General Staff of the German army. His colonelcy came to him five years later. As Lieutenant-General he command the Twenty-Second Division at Cassel.</p>
        <p>The fashions of the minute make powerful demands on the most intricate an elaborate stitching, and skilled and dainty workmanship, and much of the beautiful embroidery which is a feature of some of the smartest gowns is done by hand. Sprays of flowers worked in natural colours are most effective when on silk arranged in the form of bands or a deep cape-like collar, while hand-painted satin is quite in demand as a trimming of evening toilettes. There are, therefore, many excellent opportunities for any- one possessed of taste and a certain amount of skill to utilize many spare half hours in planning and carrying out these ornamental additions of an up-to-date toilette. In the scheme of dress everywhere is a tendency to flowing lines, and this is very noticeable on the shoulders of all gowns whether for street or for house wear. Deep triple or single capes appear on nearly all walking toilettes, but as they require a great amount of skill in cutting should not be attempted by the girl who makes her own clothes. Instead I should advise the substitu- tion of a deep lace collar subh as can be seen in</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, February 27, 1903. FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
