      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>PRETTY HAT.</head>
        <p>A PRETTY HAT.</p>
        <p>adopt any extravagances of style suggested by their dressmaker by way of a variety. Some of these innovations and elaborations of the skirt cannot be called either artistic or pretty, but— and here lies the keynote of all fashions—they are a change, and variety is as the breath of life to the votaries of les modes. Among the most popular of the new skirts is that in which kilt or box pleats come , from a shaped hip emprecement. In this way the trimness of the waist is preserved. The cape which forms a part of the fashionable coats which accompany these skirts are generally made detachable, and are sometimes trimmed with lace appliqué and finished off with fringe.</p>
        <p>Millinery is very- charming even now, and this Feason will see some of the most charming models ever displayed for our ruination. The pretty hat sketched is not, however, of a costly nature, although it is distinguished by its smartness. The hat is of straw, the edge of the brim being coloured a pale green. The crown is low and Hat, and the brim which curves up from the face is trimmed inside with a bird in whose plumage white and an irridescent greenish black with a hint of blue</p>
        <p>6,614, wi,±1.77-7- - .. ..,, ,1 3( I- 1 i ..:A,N, olt:,: V./ ' •••• ~ j it \ , 47/// • :7t 4-44. 4. . ~ -- 6- ii \\</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SIMPLE BLOUSE.</head>
        <p>A SIMPLE BLOUSE.</p>
        <p>in it are intermingled as in that of the magpie. A bow of bright green mirror velvet rests on the hair. There is a slight twist of good patterned lace of a soft tone of cream draped round the crown forming its sole trimming. There is something very smart looking about this style of hat with its upturned brim, and I expect we shall see it in many charming forms trimmed with different tones of tulle intermingled 'or wreathed with flowers of the small blossomed variety. The perfectly flat toque or hat (as either name is equally applicable) with a stiff military aigrette arranged slightly to one side is also much in vogue and is to be seen made entirely of pale toned net or chiffon, or in some instances entirely of • small flowers, particularly violets. They are very becoming and certainly chic. Blouses are still as delightful and as varied as ever. A pretty sample is illustrated suitable for morning wear and is of a mode which is uncommon. The material is sea green viyella without a pattern, and the trimming is lace insertigE. of a straw tint piped at the edges with a deeplff tint of green than the colour of the blouse hes*. the piping being of silk. The blouse fastens bo one side with invisible hooks and eyes, the three enamelled buttons being simply ornamental. There are many styles of blouses but this one is rather above the average in design and is, therefore; worthy of imitation. Silk and Oriental satin are used in conjunction with lace for the creation 0 the smartest louses.</p>
        <p>71QTJETTI4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Lady Butler's famous picture the" Roll Call," attrac- ted more attention than perhaps any other ever exhibi- ted at the Royal Academy, but comparatively few women have made their, mark as painters of pictures. To some extent this may be attributed to the unfair treatment they have received from the Academicians, who have never placed the two sexes upon terms of equality. At the recent elections however, several votes were recopded for Miss Kemp-Welch, and possibly this recognition may be the first step towards the election iof women associates. There s no legal disability attaching to the election of women artists, who ought to be admitted to the full fellowship and privileges of the Royal Academy. The Earl of Yarmouth's engagements have more than once been the subject of romantic stories, but there seems no doubt that he is to marry Miss Thaw,a wealthy American heiress. In that case she will still further in- crease the large number of women in the British peerage who are daughters of Americans. Mrs. George Corn- wallis West (Lady Randolph Churchill) refers to this subject in an article she contributes to one of the March magazines. "Americans," she says "are often credited with loving titles." She admits that in a sense this is true, but only because it is a natural conclusion that the aristocracy represent the most culturedand refinedclass of people. "There are some," she adds, "that do not discriminate, and who are dazzled by a high sounding title, whatever the origin, and whoever the bearer may be." But if this is a weakness Mrs. Cornwallis West does not believe that it is by any means confined to American women.</p>
        <p>Every now and then we hear of women forming a Society for having nothing more to do with ridiculous man. There have been many such Societies in America, but they are usually very short lived, and no one will take very seriously "The Society for Promoting Man- Indifference Amongst Women," which has been started in Guildford, and seems to have greatly disturbed the peace of that picturesque ktSurrey town. Yet it is claimed that it has already a numerous membership, with a president and secretary, and the rules are very precise. Members are not to affect masculine ways or manners. They must wear long skirts and dress their hair in a becoming manner, and they are invited to make their appearance as attractive as possible. They may even extend their friendship to men but this must not de- generate into silly sentiment, and marriage is not to be thought of. The usual fate of suoh societies is,however, that one member after another gets expelled for breach of rules and in the end even the "more pronounced spinsters" give it up as a failure. The domestic cat is soon to have a journal all to itself, as one called the "Cat World" will be amongst the periodicals shortly coming out. Some women, like our late Queen Victoria do not care for cats, but as a rule these home pets find their best friends in the feminine world, and they have no more ardent champion than Princess Victoria of Schleswig Holstein, the elder of the tivo daughters of Princess Christian. The Duchess of Bedford, who is president of the National Cat Club, is another devoted friend to pussy. There is a little tombstone at Woburn which marks the grave of one of her pets, but the Duchess takes a general interest in the whole feline race, and she is a patroness of Mrs. Gordon's home for lost and stray cats. Toy bulldogs are just now the special canine fancy, and several well-known women in Society were the most successful exhibitors at arecent show in London. These included the Hon. Mrs. Baillie of Dochfour, and Lady Gooch, but, probably the greatest authority upon toy bulldogs is Lady Kathleen Pilkington who has taken several championships. It was a toy bulldog named Peter, that, until he was succeeded by the Irish terrier, heldthe post of honour among the King's faithful dumb friends. Peter unhappily got run over by a butcher's cart, and though he recovered from the accident he did not live long afterwards. In the modistio world, things are going slowly, more so than has been the case for several years past. Usually by the end of February novelties for the spring and early summer are ready, and modistes are waiting to discuss them with their customers, but so far there seems little that is new to consider. In colours there are a few revivals, magenta being one of them. At present we are spared it in the mass no entire toilette in this ugly shade having yet been seen, taking its walks abroad. It appears as a relief on pale grey or white, and we can but trust this is the worst that is to happen to.p.s in this direction. _ - -</p>
        <p>Yew well-dressed women donned blouses with trans- parent yokes and sleeves, for outdoor wear last summer, and leading dressmakers intend this year to discard the mode altogether. In the first place it has been so eagerly adopted, and so vulgarly exaggerated by the masses as to render it undesirable, and in the second, the idea spells ruin, to the neck and arms. Last, but by no means least, the pairs of lungs that have been, and still are being sacrificed to the outdoor transparent- necked blouse, must have reached a high total. Even when the keenest of east winds is with us, the young person with her neck showing through a muslin, or lace blouse, and a string of pearls taking the place of a collar, has been no uncommon spectacle. Another move in the right direction is the returuof the collar band. Very few women look well in a collarless blouse, or bodice, the high semi-transparent band being much more becoming and styleful. One of the newest sleeves show a wide strip of lace down the inner part, and into this, the material of which the gown is built is pleated very fully, the deep gauntlet cuff being of the lace. Another favourite model is tucked closely from the shoulder to the elbow, from thence falling in very full kiltings over an undersleeve of lace. These wide and often very inconvenient sleeves, the Queen has never yet sanctioned. Her Majesty prefers the closely-fitting variety, which boasts neither bagging puff, or gaping cuff,—an example in which she is closely followed by the Princess of Wales. On the homely lunch or dinner table, the carving cloth has again made its appearance,an item calculated to re- joice the heart of the careful,economical housewife. In cream coloured, hand-woven linen, edged with drawn thread work, it looks particularly effecae on the white table cloth. The new name given it is" gravy- splasher "a name which might sometimes be applied to the carver as well as to the cloth.</p>
        <p>"The Splendid Idle Forties" (Macmillan and C 0.65.) consists of a number of short stories of Old California, all written with vigour and grace. Mrs. Gertrude Atherton excels in making her Spanish people speak the English that Spaniards speak, and the passion and langour of the women, and the .lazy beauty of the men are brought before us in all their worthless loveliness. The clever illustrations which add considerably to the beauty of the book are from the hand of Mr. Harrison</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>THIRTEEN WEEKS LOST.</head>
        <p>THIRTEEN WEEKS LOST.</p>
        <p>Thirteen weeks without the help of the bread- winner is a serious matter for any household. Mr. Jas. Peed, of 7, Burgess-square, Sandy-lane, Wisbech,Cambs, was out of work for such a period, suffering agony from urinary trouble. The fact that he is back again at work is due entirely to Dodd's Kidney Pills which have made him a healthy man again. No worker can afford to ignore Dodd's Kidney Pills. Illness strikes everyone and oftener through the kidneys than any other way. Dodd's Kidney Pills cure the kidneys and cure many diseases—Rheumatism, Sciatica. Backache, Dropsy and Diabetes—because they do so. If you have a pain anywhere; if you feel unwell, be sure the kidneys are well. Well kidneys strengthen and fortify the whole body—keep disease away. There is nothing which makes kidneys so well as Dodd's Kidney Pills.</p>
        <p>The Field Club. An inteiesting paper. entitled "On the Tourist's Track" was read at the eighth meeting of the session in connection with the Warrington Field Club by Mr. Charles White. Mr. Womersley presided. After sketching the geology of the district around Oban and indicating the physical geography of Scotland during the old red sandstone period, Mr. White passed to the indications of glacial action in the Great Glen, and more especially to the parallel roads of Glen Roy. Each of these he explained a.s equivalent to the beach shallow usually seen at the margin of a lake, the lake in this case being a drainage lake dammed up by an ice barrier at the mouth of the glen. The repetition of the so-called " road " he ascribed to a change in the level; and pointed out that the overflow place corresponding to each "road" was known. A very lively discussion followed, Messrs. Greening, Madeley and others contributing, but the lecturer, who has repeatedly studied the phenomena on the spot,was able to deal very effectively with his critics. Mr. John Watson, F.R.A.S., and Dr. Naden were elected mem- bers of the club.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLURES.—According to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the number of New Bankruptcy Proceedings in England and Wales gazetted during the week ending Feb. 21th, was 91. The number in the corresponding wee-k of last year was 94, showing a decrease of 3, being a net decrease in 1903,t0 date,of 3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>LOCAL FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>LOCAL FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>rIIIIB appearance of that unique little " Diary and Corn .1. panion issued by GB(), G. BUSSBY 1 CO., for the use ofiGricketers (which:may be obtained from Sports Dealers Booksellers, or hallway Bookstalls for 6d.), remin is us of the coming season. in anticipation of which Ge0..0. Hussey Co , have prepared a "record" stock of Bats. We are told that the Umber they hold for Bat making alone exceeds 1.000 tone. " Glorious Cricket follows the British Flag everywhere." (1)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION CUP.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CUP.</p>
        <p>SECOND ROUND.</p>
        <p>RESULTS. *Oldham -0... 4 .Wi0an....... ........ 0-0... 0 *Salfcrd -3...11 Leigh 0-0... 0 *Runcorn 3-4...1l Birkenhead' O-0... 0 Rochdale -2 8 *St. Helens l-0... 2 *Hull 6-11...45 Hull Marlb'h ...0-0... 0 *Morecambe -0 0 Millom 0-0 0 *Castleford O-0. .0 Halifax 0-0...0 Batley 3-1. 9 *Normanton 1-0... 2 Bradford O-3 9 *Bramley O-0... 0 Leeds -3..J3 *Wakefield 0-0... 0 *Huns let -1... 7 Outwood Ch. ...0-0... 0 *York. -5..d7 Otley 1-0... 2 *Brighouse 9_3j3 South Shields 1-0... 2 *Barrow 0-5..J5 Wath Brow l-1... 5 sMaryport 1-0: .2 Parton l-0... 2 Reighley O-I_l2 Manuingham ....0-0... 0</p>
