      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>RUNCORN v. BIRKENHEAD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. BIRKENHEAD.</p>
        <p>• Played at Runcorn in very bad weather on , Saturday at Irwell-lane. Both played representative teams : Runcorn lost the toss, the visitors speedily getting to work aided by a strong wind. Scrummaging ensued at midfield the visitors repeatedly gaining possession and forcing the game. Houghton was called on,, clearing nicely. Moran failing to gather lost considerable ground and Runcorn had to defend in their own 25. The attack was successfully repelled, Vernon missing a, splendid opening. The first bit of brilliant play came from the visitors when Vernon beautifully intercepted a pass intended for Butterwarth and was held on the line. An anxious time ensued Jolly eventually clearing. The visiting forwards speedily got to work again and another fusilade was commenced on the line, Pennington after a fine bit of play dropping at goal, the ball going wide. After a long bout of scrumma ing a nice movement was initiated by Jolley, Butterworth, and Jones. The Linnets kept up the pressure, Walker with a fine dribble forcing Harri- man to save. A little later Jolley picking out of the scrum baffled his opponents and crossed the line. Farmer failed at goal. The drop out saw Houghton replying to his opponents with vigour. A penalty taken by Butterworth brought relief. Several passing move- ments initiated by Richardson were nipped by the visitors before they could become dangerous. Uninteresting play continued to half-time, which arrived with Runcorn leading by a try to nil. The restart saw Runcorn make tracks for the opponents' fine, Harriman saving a certain score. Judicious play relieved the pressure momentarily, Harriman having to'kick dead. A fine chance was lost by Moran badly mulling, Clayton carrying the play to centre. A scrum on the line gave the Linnets the oppor- tunity,,Jolley popping out at the corner. Farmer failed to add the necessary points. The visitors were kept on the defensive for the cnict five minutes at the end of which Richardson scored a beautiful try after the run- ning had been. made by all the home• backs. Farmer was successful in his third attempt kicking a fine goal After more scrimmages and wild rushes for the visitors line Langley marked, in front of the posts and Farmer took the kick a goal being the result. The leather was again set in motion Jones getting over the line after a brilliant run by Butterworth. The ball, however was brought back Harriman clearing when a score seemed iniminent.lUp to the presentaßthe play had been inthe visitors quarters,but after a desperate effort,however,he made the play at centre where Butterworth, handling, kicked across to Moran who dodged his opponents and scored. Farmer again added the additional points. Richardson who had been previously injured had to re- tire at this point Walker taking his place. Jones missed a fine opportunity the visitors playing with more cohesiveness than in the early stages. Time arrived with Runcorn leading by three goals four tries to Birkenhead nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>ST. HELENS v. ROCHDALE HORNETS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS v. ROCHDALE HORNETS.</p>
        <p>Played at St. Helens, in a perfect _gale of wind and rain, before about 1,000 spectators. Winning the toss, the home forwards soon dribbled down, and from a penalty Pennington placed a goal for St. Helens. Even play followed. In a scrummage Heap, of Rochdale, was injured and left the ground. The home forwards tried hard to break through the Rochdale defence,and Creevey once got over, but lost the ball. Allan saved splendidly on several occasions, and at half-time the Saints led by 1 goal (2 points) to nil. On ends being changed the Hornets had the best of play, and gained an easy victory. Gibson soon gained a try, and Blincow kicked a goal. The visitors kept up the pressure, and the Saints were quickly driven back; Rigg oompleting the movement by scoring a second try. The result was a win for Rochdale by 1 goal and 2 tries (8 points) to 1 goal (2 points). Teams : St. Helens: Foulkes, back ; Liversage, Barnes, Waring, and Creevey, three-quarters; Banks and Pen- nington, half banks; Briers, Carney, Green, Thompson, Mavity,. Whiteley, IVlawson, and Lea, forwards. Rochdale Hornets: Allen, back; Oldershaw, Blin- cow, Rigg, and Gibson, three-quarters; Schofield and Baxter, half backs; Cooper, Croson, Dearden, Tweedale, Ashworth, Kershaw, Heap, and Leach, forwards. Referee, Mr. McCutcheon, Oldham; touch judges, Messrs. W. Jones, Runcorn, and J. Hallewell, Wigan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>SALFORD v. LEIGH.</head>
        <p>SALFORD v. LEIGH.</p>
        <p>In wet, miserable weather this tie took place at Weaste, before 6,000 people. Molyneux started for Leigh, and play settled down at the centre. Leigh dribbled into the home territory, and Dan Smith cleared by kicking into touch. Messer enabled Norris to put in a good run into touch in Leigh territory. Molyneux punted well, and brought relief, after which Salford again attacked, the visitors being placed on the defen- sive. Norris was held up on the line, and then Leigh b oke away, and Lomas rushing across saved the situa- tion nicely, and at half-time there was no score. On resuming, Salford soon assumed the aggressive, and Harter at length scored smartly for the home side at the corner, and Lomas failed at the place. From the drop-out the Reds were soon attacking again, and Norris made a grand run, which nearly resulted in him scoring. Occasionally, Leigh got possession, and as a result Molyneux did his side a good service by finding touch. There was, however, no holding the Salford men, and Bell eventually scored from Messer's pass. The third and last try of Salford's was the result of a fine individual effort on the part of Lomas, who dribbled three-parts of the field, and scored near the post The same player failed at goal. Result :—Salford 1 goal and 3 tries (11 points), Leigh nil. Teams : Salford—Da.n Smith, back; Norris, Messer, Lomas (captain), and Bell, threequarter backs; Harter and Preston, half backs; Rhapps, Tunney, neath,J. Williams Brown, R. Shaw, Shore, and Buckler, forwards. Leigh—Pearson, back; Ganley, Bennett, Davies, and •Johnson, three-quarter backs ; Molyneux and B. Kirk, half backs ; Boardman, W. Roberts, J. Roberts, Black- burn? T. Kirk, Jameson, Kelly, and Arrowsmith, for- wards.</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. F. Renton (Hunslet). Touch judges Messrs. J. P. Taylor (Oldham), and R. Straw (Tyldesley).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>LEAGUE.---FIRST DIVISION.</head>
        <p>THE LEAGUE.---FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Since being—creit;cii;i-tivz; 'galas in the first round of the cup-ties Warrington had not played a match un- til Tuesday when they played St. Helens again in a postponed league match at Warrington. Both teams were well represented, the only absentees being Fish (Warrington) and Barnes (St. Helens). Unfortunately for the home club the weather was wretched, rain fall- ing continuously, and the gate was only about 1,000 strong. Mr. F. Kennedy was the referee and the teams lined out as follows :</p>
        <p>_ Warrington Hallamtfull back; Hockenhall,lsherwood, Dickenson and Harris, three-quarters ; Edmunds and Duckworth half backs; Naylor, Foster, Edmondson, J. Swift, W. Swift, Warburton, Harmer and Ashton, forwards.</p>
        <p>St. Helens: Foulkes, full back; Waring, Liversage, Kennedy and Creevey, three-quarters • Hall and Banks, half backs ; Briers, Green,Mavity, Whitely, Carney,Lee. , Mawson, and Thompson, forwards. Warrington won the toss and had the advantage of a strong wind in the opening stages. Lee started for the Saints and play settled down at centre. Warrington at once attacked, fine passing by Edmunds, Duckworth, Isherwood, and Hockenhall carrying play t 6 the line. St. Helens had some narrow escapes but managed to keep their line intact for some time. Once Edmunds got clear away by himself and was captured by Foulkes. The home forwards played vigorously and managed to get possession of the ball with some frequency. Edmunds again set the backs in motion, and brilliant work by Duckworth, Dickenson,and Isherwood beat the</p>
        <p>St.Helens defence, Hockenhall having an easy run in. Dickenson failed at goal. The Saints played up with energy and by good kicking forced Warrington back. Harris, however, led the way again and finding his course blocked he screwed across for Hockenhall, who just won the race for possession and scored a second try in the corner. Half-time, Warrington, 2 tries; St. • Helens, nil.—The second half saw St. Helens playing with the wind but they could make very little headway, ' the home backs being far too smart for them. Briers, Carney and Lee frequently led the way with good dribbling but could not pass Hallam,and the score re- mained unaltered to the close, Warrington winning by ! 6,ppints to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <p>Money Talks.</p>
        <p>Your grocer returns your money if you don't like Fels-Naptha soap we pay him to do so, because we want you to know the soap. Do you know of another sold in this way ? There is no other such soap. 2 d a bar. ft` r ELS -N AP TFi A 39 Wil9on Street Loncicn EC</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SWINTON v. RUNCORN</head>
        <p>SWINTON v. RUNCORN</p>
        <p>This return match was played on the Swinton ground on Tuesday, in wretched weather and before a poor attendance of spectators. Runcorn were well repre- sented, but, unfortunately for Swinton, Valentine and Samuel were unable to turn out, and the back division had to be rearranged. Swinton won the toss and Farmer started the game for Runcorn, who were the first to attack. Moran, however, let in the home forwards,who rushed the ball to the visitors' line, but only a minor point resulted. Then Runcorn again took up the attack, and from a round of passing Butterworth scored a try, which Farmer failedto improve. At half-time Runcorn led by three points. In the second half both sides attacked in turn, but Runcorn addedanother try to their score through Moran and won a well-contested game by two tries (six points) to Swinton nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>OLDHAM v. WIGAN.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM v. WIGAN.</p>
        <p>Played at Watersheddings before a moderate attend- ance and in miserable weather. Ferguson started, and play settled in the visitors' territory, where an exhibi- tion of passing by the Oldham backs took place, Spottis- woode being tackled in close proximity to the line. The visitors gradually worked their way to the other end, and almost scored, Rothwell's drop at goal just going wide. For a time the game remained at the centre. Oldham, through Tyson and Spottiswoode, made in- effectual attempts to score, and just before the interval Rothwell again failed with his kick at goal. • The resumption saw Wigan holding their own, and for the first ten minutes neither side could lay claim to any advantage. From this point, however, Oldham began to assume the upper hand, up to the finish operations ruling in Wigan's half. In the last quarter Sam Lees kicked two penalty goals. Result :—Oldham 2 goals (4 points), Wigan nil. Teams:—</p>
        <p>Oldham—R. L.Thomas back; F. Spottiswoode, Tyson, A. Lees, and T. Cash, three-quarter backs; S. Lees, jun., and Brady half backs; G. Prater (captain), J. Ferguson, G. Wilkinson, Topham, Vowles, Owens, J. H. Wilkin- son, and Ellis, forwards. Wigan—Mason, back; Barr, Dickenson, Duffy, and Rouse, three-quarter backs • Anderson and Rothwell, half backs; G, Brown, Hilton, Collier, Hallowell, Hatton, Toppin, Brown, and Vickers forwards. Referee, Mr. J. Oakland, Outwood Church; touch . judges, Messrs. T. Squires (Broughton Rangers)and T.L. Brimalow (Salford). SALFORD v. BRIGHOUSE RANGERS. The first match between these teams earlier in the seasonended in an unexpected win for Brighouse by four - points to nothing, but in the return match, which took place on Tuesday afternoon on the Salford ground, the visitors were outplayed in all positions, and Salford scored an easy victory. With the wind in their favour in the first half Salford kept up a continual attack, and although Baker and Walker tackled strongly they were unable to prevent their lines from being crossed. Shore scored a try from a forward dribble, and Messer also added a try and Lomas placed a goal. At half-time Salford had a lead of eight points. In the second half Salford continued to overplay their opponents, and tries were scored by Harter and Lomas, but neither of them were converted. The game ended in an easy win for Salford by one goal and four tries (14 points) to Brig- house Rangers aothing. HALIFAX v. BROUGHTON RANGERS. The return match between these clubs took place on Tuesday at Halifax, in wretched weather. The home team won the toss and had the wind in their favour during the first half of the game. They immediately began to attack strongly, and Rigg made a mark in a favourable position from which Wedgwood placed a goal. Then the Rangers' forwards put in a fine loose dribble to the other end of the field, where Hogg got a nice pass from Wilson, but was brought down by Riley just in time to prevent him scoring. A penalty kick brought relief to Halifax, and from a forward rush Langford scored a try, which Wedgwood improved. At half-time Halifax led by seven points to nothing. With the wind in their favour the Rangers early began to attack in the second half, and their backs made many fine passing movements, but they could not overcome the home defence, and the game ended in a win for Halifax by two goals and one try (seven points) to Broughton Rangers nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>BRADFORD v. HULL.</head>
        <p>BRADFORD v. HULL.</p>
        <p>At Bradford, on Tuesday in rainy weather, before 4,000 spectators. Laidlaw kicked off for Bradford, who immediately attacked, and from Cooper's mark Mosby placed a goal. After this Hull broke away splendidly, Franks, Cook, and Moxon contributing a useful passing movement. Sinton was safe, and found touch at the centre, where play ruled for a time evenly. At length Sinton got off down touch, only, however, to fall a victim to Taylor. Half-time :—Bradford, two points; Hull, nothing. In the second half Bradford scored a try, and finally won by five points to nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CATHOLIC ASSOCIATION V. Sr. PETER'S SEHL-STRBET, LIVERPOOL. Played at the Association Rooms, Warrington, on Tuesday evening, and resulted in a handsome win for the Warrington team. After the match, the teams sat down to tea and a pleasant social gathering was held. LIVERPOOL. WARRINGTON E. Howley lOO Rev. Father Swarbrick 68 J. Hayden R. Gilhooley lOO B MacDonald . 7l A. Corrigan P. Kean B2 J Riley J. Kelly 9l M. Cunningham lOO E. Greenland lOO 0. Toole 77 R. Cunningham lOO J. Gleeson R. Smerdon 54 J Durkin M. Fox ' 54 A. Richardson ... • 100 J. Conboy C. Raper B2 T. Newns .100 - - 962 1074 962</p>
        <p>Majority for WaYrington:.— 112 Majority for Liverpool (last match)... 44</p>
        <p>Majority for Warrington on both matches.— 68</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN VIADUCT BRASS BAND. Striking out in an entirely new direction the Earles- town band inaugurated a Brass Instrumental Quartet Contest. which was brought to a successful conclusion in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Saturday evening. Four prizes were offered, and the entrants were allowed to choose their own test piece, a very good arrange. ment, as it avoided monotony. The -judge was Mr. C Smith of Gorton. There were 15 . entries, some well known bands sending representatives, such as a Wingate Temperance, Pemberton Old, Wannington Borough, Crosfields, Goodshaw, Sutton-road? &amp;c. The large audi- ence, which was present, had a capital treat, and in the end the first prize was awarded. to Norland, Halifax, whose selection was "0 harmony"; second prize went to Wingate No. 1 set (selection, "Oberon") ; third prize, Sutton Road, ("The Bohemian Girl"): fourth prize. Goodshaw (" The seasons ")4 The prizes were die tributed by Mr. C. W. Emmett.</p>
        <p>With reference to the approaching visit of the Canadian Farmiirs' Delegates to this country announced in our advertisement columns, we are arivised by the Canadian Commissioner of Emigration that a member of the Delegation will be travelling through this county. His itinerery will b3-published within the next few days. Any persons desiring to meet and consult this gentleman, are invited to notify Mr. A. F. Jury, Old Castle Buildings, Preesous-row, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the York Penitentiary Society the Dean of York alluding to the dangers to which young girls were exposed under the conditions of modern life, said that the class with which the Society, , dealt were not the only offendersagainst the purity anc.3 morality of life. He could find more excuse, pity, ani sympathy for these poor girls who had, perhaps, very few opportunities of knowiing what was right, and 140 d. been placed impositions of terrible temptation, than,/or those—their superiors, as they would call themselves, in life and position—who, as they read in the papers.. out- raged the decency of society, and brought shame and discredit on.honoured names.</p>
        <p>It is announced in official circles (says a Montreal correspondent) that Lord Stanley, eldest son of Lord Derby, will succeed Lord Minto when the latter retires from the position of Governor-General in November. The King has conferred the decoration of the -Royal Red Cross on Lady Macdonald, in recognition of her services in tending the sick and wounded soldiers during the defence of the Legations at Pekin in, 1900.</p>
        <p>a Wigan man named Charles Gibbs, apparently about. 3.3 years of age, was cut intwo by a goods train on the Lancashire and Yorkshire Railway near Long Lane Bridge, Ashton, Preston, on Wednesday night, The deceased, wh6 was apparently a navvy or tramp, bad no business on the line.</p>
      </div>
