      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>THE GRIL'AT AUSTRALIIN REM.EDY.</p>
        <p>VITADATIO</p>
        <p>DOCTORS DIFFERED But VITADATIO CURED.</p>
        <p>2, Curzon-atreet, Runcorn. Feb. 4th, 1901,</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—l had been suffering a long time, my legs, arms and body were coverod with lumps, and when I went to bed at nights, could not sleep, used to long for day to come. My doctor ordered me to the Royal Infirmary at Liverpool but neither he nor the doctors there could name my complaint,although they called me to a special ruseting,some thought it one disease, some another, but their treatment did me no good. One day when going down Mill- street, I noticed in your shop window WEBBER'S VITADATIO. I came in to see you about my case, you told me I had a very bad BLOOD DISEASE but viTalciAllo would CURE me, I decided to try It, and am happy to say alter taking it for some time I AM QUITE C RED and now SLEEP NATURALLY. It also cured me of Indigestion. You have my permission to use this letter as you wish, for such a REMEDY cannot be too well known. Signed, JOSHUA FRADLNY. February 28th, 1902. Dear Sir.—A little over twelve months ago I sent you a testimonial of my wonderful cure through taking VITADATIO. and I am pleased to state it is now two years since my troubles left me,which have not returned.—Yonrs truly,</p>
        <p>JOSHUA FRADLHY.</p>
        <p>From all Chemists and Grocers. The price of Medicine is 4s. 6d. and 2s. 9d. per bottle. For further particulars, S• A. PALM ER,</p>
        <p>MOUNT PLACE, BROOK-STLUCET. MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>Correspondence Invited, Write for Testimonials. OBTAINABLII LOCALLY FROM BOOTS, CASH OH/MIST&amp;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <p>"THE country is gett- A GOVERNMENT OF ing tired," said the MAKE-BELIEVE, member for the Whitby division "of the policy of mess, muddle, and make-believe." Not an al- together pleasant description of the policy of his Majesty's Government. Yet Mr. P.scEErr belongs to the straightest sect of the Tories. He would ! speak favourably of the Government on all occa- sions if he could, and it may be said quite confi dently that he would not have carried his amend- ment to a division if he thought that by doing so he ran any risk of defeating the Administration. Mess, muddle, and make-believe are bad things, but they are much more tolerable to the bulk of the supporters of the Government than the pros- pect of a Ministerial defeat. That makes it all the more gratifying that in a House of Commons constituted like the present there should be some Conservative members who are not in- disposed to speak their minds, however little they are inclined to go to the extreme length of bring- ing to an end the life of the Administration they were elected to support. Mr. BECKETT'S attack on the Army system introduced by MT. BRODRICK was well-informed, caustic, and sweepini). The present SECRETARY tor War rose into fame on a trick division which defeated the late Liberal Government on the question of the supply of cor- dite. Since that time the supply of ammunition under it Conservative Government has been so low that Mr. 'BALFOUR, as he admits, was scarcely able to go to sleep in his bed an account of his apprehensions of the consequent danger; but on the prestige of the trick division on cordite Mr. BRODRICK has risen to the giddy eminence occupied by a Secretary of State for War. He re- organises the Army; he overrides commanders-in- chief; he dresses in khaki to take part in reviews of German troops in Berlin; and he is treated to a salute of seventeen guns when he goes to Malta. The result to the nation of this grand promotion is, accurding to Mr. BECKETT—who proved his case and who was ably supported by other speakers from the Tory benches—that he has reduced our military organisation to a condition of mess, muddle, and make-believe. It would come as a surprise even to some member of the Housa of Commons to hear that the Secretary for War set up his preposterous Army Corps scheme, if not 'without consulting the War Office, at least with- out its approval. That one Who never set a squadron in the field,</p>
        <p>Nor the division of a battle knew more than a spinster should undertake the vast task of setting up in this country an army on tie continental system, and in the face of opposition from our most experi- enced soldiers, is indeed astounding. The War Office repudiates the paternity of the scheme, say Mr. BECKETT ; the army looks askance at it —La</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>for good reasons. It is only's scheme on paper. Of the six Army Corps two are in some sense deserving f the name; two are he merest skeletons; and two are nowhere to be found. Mr. Bncxprr put his case alike admirably and incisively when be enumerated his objections to Mr. BROD- RICK'S plans. The objections, it may be noted,are just; the same in number as the Army Corps. In the first place, said the member for Whitby, the Army Corps scheme is based upon a wrong prin- ciple. Secondly, it is not suited to the real needs of the country. Thirdly, it is enormously expen- sive. Fourthly, it does not remove the &amp;fonts Which were made conspicuous by the war in South -Africa. Fifthly, it is not adapted to the national necessities; and, sixthly, it has no real exist- ence. This is not mere fun or lively satire. It is hard truth. Mr. BRODRIOK who turned out a Government because the stock 8f cordite was alleged to be low, organised the Army on a more expensive basis than was ever known before, and has at the same time contrived to throw it into a condition of inefficiency and most alarming dis- order. Mr. BRODRICK is all the more dangerous as a Minister of War because he cannot conceive the possibility of any falling into errer on his part. All exp3rt opinion is against him, and yet he assures the House of Commons and the country that the Army was never in a better con- dition than now. The present agitation in the lieuse of Commons has been stirred up by those Members who believe that whilst we should lavish money on the Navy_ as our first line of defence, We should keep only a comparatively small army at home, and such an army as could be 'easily despatched abroad. Mr. BRODRICR replies by eulogy of his Continental mode and ' declared that we cannot cut down the present ex- traordinary expenditure by so much as a penny without impairing efficiency. But this is not t reply to the criticism levelled against the new. Army system in the House of Commons on Monday: It is to set up anew ideal—the ideal of inexperience. It is to set aside all professional and all expert Opinion at if it were worthless, and to combine the responsibilities of War Secretary and COMMANDER- IN-CHIEF in the person of an officer of Volunteers. The taxpayer has good reason to quiver before the declarations made by Mr. BRODRICK. We already had the most expensive Army in Europe, but we have rushed up its cost beyond previous flood- marks. That is unfortunately not the worst. The more the nation has spent on its Army the ' more the inefficiency of that national weapon has been impaired. There is hardly a member of the Rouse of Commons who does not feel this, and yet When the division was taken Mr. BRODRICK es- caped censure because he could not be reached Without bringing down the Government.</p>
        <p>It is usual at the present time for people to take a pessimistic view of the future of the iron trade in this country. No such spirit,however,pervaded the annual meeting in connection with the Warrington Branch of the Associated Iron and Steel Workers, held on Saturday evening. It was admitted that the trade was passing through a period of de- Pression, but there was no suggestion that the trade would not assume in course of time its us- ual buoyancy. In alluding to American compe- tition, Mr. Aucott. the secretary of. the Midland WA _ages Board and 'President of the Ironworkers aecation,averrecl that the iron trade had nothing r fear from the Americans, who were unable to naeh Us anything about iron, but in the steel ilArtana there was a different tale to tell, which he !Lanett Air. Cox, the secretary to relate at a th• re meeting. Mr. Aucott was inclined to a, Ink that the iron trade had its greatest :Perny in the growing dissipation of the workers burough drink. He pleaded with even more force than a temperance orator for soberness amongst the workers and contended that with a sober country we had no need to fear foreign com- petition.</p>
        <p>In an exceedingly able speech, Mr. Alderman Alonks showed a thorough grasp of the difficul- ties confronting the trade. He was not in th 3 least afraid of foreign competition if the masters end men could join unitedly in one common cause egaintat the foreigner. Whilst the workmen could do a great deal he was inclined to think the mas- ters could do a great deal more in the way of reorganisation and reconstruction so as to reduce .crthe cost of manufacture. The result of Mr. •in-onks's inspection of continental iron and steel Works has convinced him that they are better Inbvanaged, and better organized, than our English Zrirs are. Such a grave reflection on the F„Pfteity of English managers and manufac- /fors would, we feet sure, not be made by Mr. „,'"_?oks without adequate knowledge. It is the T. very of "efficiency." Not only do we wat.t ,esq leiency in the public services, of which we have ;r4rd so much of late, but manufacturers must „..,ave efficiency in their private business concerns, 'u.lese they are content to see the more enter- Prising foreigner book orders which should come 14 England. It is not an uncommon practice of Mavoufacturers to lay the blame for any ill-success whvieh may befall them at the .door of the trades- unionist. Mr. Alderman Monks, as an iropmaster of wide experience, does nothing of the kind. He wel.oomes combination amongst the workers, be.heving that in that way.both masters and men wvill be able to get unity of action in overcoming defieulties and better results generally.</p>
        <p>Not the least interesting portion of Mr. Monks's t'ernarkS Waf that referring to the need of Improv- ing the educational facilities of Warrington. He is determined to make the utmost use of the new Education Act and has already propounded a scheme for establishing a series of scholarships s ) as to enable the child of the poorest citizen, if endowed with sufficient brain power, to pass from the elementary to the higher grade schools or the Grammar school without paying any fees. He is also anxious that promising students should be allowed to enter the higher departments of manu- facturing establishments without paying premiums. 'or the carrying out of this excellent idea, Mr. 4onks is anxious to secure the co-operation of all I)).anufacturers and other persons interested in z!chicational progress in Warrington,and it is tc tloPei for the credit of the town that he will not appeal in vain.</p>
        <p>,Just now the action of the managers of Silver-st "437 Schools,Watrington in introducing a.measura of Popular control over the schools, will be viewed with particular interest. It was decided a few eFlaYs ago that the parents of the children qtendo IN the schocls, together with the subscribers t the schools, should be allowed to mominate tw 9ilt of the four foundation managers, who accord- to the Education Act were to be chosen by the trustees, and last week a meeting of Parents and subscribers was held, with the result ,ntl! two managers were appointed by "the people." cla is a distinctly new departure in school 1331nagement, end redounds very much to the credit of the Wesleyan denominatim. They at a4,11Y rate, are not content with merely advocating Popular control" in elementary education, but are evidently prepared to recognise at once the right of the vox populi to be heard on the !flanagerial board of their schools. The example le admirable and way—we hope it will—have far- reaching effects.</p>
        <p>BABIES' FOOD ROBINSON'S Patent BARLEY, nj3&amp;BIBS' rooD' . with milk, forms a splendid --IRS' MOD. Food for Babies, the Patent BABIES' FOOD BALBIES' FOOD. Barley acting as a digestive : .of the milk. Babies cannot "BM. FOOD digest cow's milk alone</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NATIONAL SCHOOL</p>
        <p>The annual concert was held on Monday evening in the Drill Hall, the principal feature of the evening being an historical comic opera entitled, "Columbus in A-Merry-Key." The entertainment was repeated on Tuesday evening there being a very large audience.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION. TO RUNCORN VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>The Volunteers orders for the week, signed by Call- tain A. Timmins, state after parade on Friday watches will be presented to those members of the Volunteer Service Company and Imperial Yeomanry who served in South Africa, and have not yet received them. The members are requested to attend in uniform.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>THREE WERKS FROM RUNCORN TO LONDON.</head>
        <p>THREE WERKS FROM RUNCORN TO LONDON.</p>
        <p>The large four-masted ship Comma has , had a pro- tracted passage from the Mersey to London owing to heavy gales and adverse winds. She left Runcorn on January 24 for London in ballast .to load for Sydney. N.S.W., but owing to heavy gales she remained in the Mersey, anchored between Egremont and New Brighton until February 3, when she left in tow, but was obliged to put into Holyhead the following day, and remained until February 9,when she again started, and her arrival in London is announced, after a protracted passage of twenty days from Runcorn on a voyage which, under ordinary circumstances, would occupy about four days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>PEARL LIFE INSURANCE COMPANY.</head>
        <p>PEARL LIFE INSURANCE COMPANY.</p>
        <p>A glance at our advertisement columns will show that during the past year the above Company has made very satisfactory progress. During the last live years the income and assets have been more than doubled the actual figures being as follows:-1897 income £565,238, assets, £718,466; 1002, income, £1,136,353, assets £1,710,338. A most satisfactory feature of tbe Com- pany's progress is an increase of £13,036 13s. id. with a total income of £1,136,353 7s. 2d. compared with that of the previous year. Everything 'points to the fact that despite active competition, the Company is forging, ahead at a comparatively rapid rate. The local superin- tendent is Mr. H. Marsh, 5, Victoria-road Runcorn, who will be pleased to enlist any suitable men for insurance work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>SUICIDE OF AN OLD MAN AT RUNCORN</head>
        <p>SUICIDE OF AN OLD MAN AT RUNCORN</p>
        <p>Mr. Fishel, deputy coroner, conducted an inquiry at ' the Runcorn Police Station_ on Saturday afternoon into the circumstances attending the delth of John Hayes (75 years of age), a retired boatman living at 6, Stone- street, Runcom who committed suicide by hanging himself on Friday morning.—Ann Hayes, wife ode- ceased, said that on Friday morning he got up and went downstairs. He was rather a long time away cud she went down andzfound him hanging from a clothes line behind the door. He was dead. Deceased had not wOrked for about nine years. For some time his con- duct had been strange and he rambled in his talk. Frank Hayes, son of deceased corroborated his mother's evi- dence.—A verdict of "Suicide whilst temporarily insane," was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>STEALING " CIIA.NGE "</head>
        <p>STEALING " CIIA.NGE "</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday, before Messrs. A. Themes (in the chair) and A. R. Norman, Maryl Ellen Wakefield (16), 22 Lock-street, Rimcorn,was charged with having stolen bid. from a boy named Cyril Spruce; at Weston on February 18th. She pleaded guilty. Lily Spruce, Overhill, Weston, said that On Wednesday morning she sent her boy Cyril to the Co- operative -Stores for some groceries. She gave him a purse containing the order and some money. Heshould have brought 11c1 back, but did not, and in consequence of what he said she went to the Stores and made in- quiries. Ezra Darlington, manager of the Stores said he served the boy, and gave him the purse with ld. in it. P. C:Wylie said he received certain information from Mrs. Spruce. He,was unable to trace defendant in Weston, and came co Runcorn, where, after considerable diffitulty he found her address. She denied that' she knew anything about the matter, but there had been several complaints about herat Weston and .Weston Point. The Chairman said that if defendantwent on in this way she would find herself in prison. They had decided on this occasion to bind her over to come up for judgment when called upon. • _ EMBEZZLEMENT 1W A RUNCORN MAN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn 'Police Court on 'Fridai, before Messrs. A. R. Norman tin the chair), and E. unison, Frank Howard, Wivern7place, Runcorn, was charged on a warrant with fraudulently obtaining by means of false wages sheets the sums of £5 Bs. 11d. from Messrs. E. Timmins and Sons, Limited, Bridgewater Foundry, Runcorn, between August 7th and December 31st. Mr. J. Burton, who appeared to prosecute, said the prisoner had been a foreman in. their employ for 14. years. In July last the prosecutors were conducting boring operations at a place called Skilts near Redditch and the prisoner was foreman in charge of the work. He had under him three men. Two were Runcorn men and the third, a man named Parker, he engaged locally at 44d. per hour. It was part of the prisoner' duty to make up ,the time sheets on Wednesday and enter the amount due to each amen. The sheets walla then forwaided to the prosecu- tors. On one of the time sheets there was the name of A. Price. There was no such man working there and without doubt. the name Should have been Parker. For the week ending August 6th the time sheet showed that £l. 35. Id. was payable to the man and from then until the end of the year. the time sheets showed that 21 Bs. id. weekly was due to Parker. That amount was remitted to the prisoner in due course, but he paid the man only 235. and kept the balance each week. By keeping the balance each week prisoner had defrauded Messrs Timmins of £5 Bs. lid. The Chairman said they were very sorry to have to deal with prisoner on a charge of this sort.. The least they could do would be to sentence him to six weeks' imprisonment with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Labour Candidate for Town Hall Ward.</p>
        <p>At ft meeting of the Trades Council held on Tuesday evening Mr. Alfred Evett, :Yeast Importer, of Market- street, Earlestown, was, unanimously appointed as Labour Candidate to contest the Town Hall Ward at the forthcoming elections. Mr. Evett's replies to the questions put by the deputation which waited upon hint gave entire satisfaction, and he will therefore be nomin- ated to oppose Mr. W. Gihnour—the retiring member. It will be remembered that Mr. Evett was nominated for Crow-lane Ward last year, but owing to his nomina- tion paper being deelared invalid, he was not permitted to go to the p9ll. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH, EA ELESTOWN,</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH, EA ELESTOWN,</p>
        <p>A tea party and Concert in aid of the new chapel fund was given by the young ladies of the church on Thurs- day evening. Tea Was served at 4.30 and 6.30, about 350 sitting down. The young ladies of the congregation presided at the tables. After tea a concert was given in the schoolroom. The programme opened • with .a chorus "Of Spain we sing by the choir, Miss and-L. Toombs followed with a. capital rendering of " Chin- Chin." A duet "Beware " by Misses L. Stronach and M. .Baxter was received with much applause. Miss Smith delivered a recitation " Little Jawcob Strauss "in splendid style and as an encore gave "What makes a gentleman"; Miss Wood followed with a song "Come back home," after which the choir rendered "The fairest day." A humorous dialogue en- titled " My aunt's heiress " in which eleven characters took part created great amusement. This completed the first portion of the programme. After a short interval the same artistes again rendered good service. The concert terminated with a tableaux entitled "Should old acquaintance &amp;c." Miss Toombs presided at the piano in an efficient manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>"LOTHIAN"</p>
        <p>SHO RTBRE AD</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Examiner Friday, February 27, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner Friday, February 27, 1903.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Trades Unionists and Workhouse Visitation.</p>
        <p>[By. A CORRESPONDENT.) As will be seen from ,the report of the School Board meeting, the Board of Education have refused to pass ; the plans for the extension of Victoria-road school. 1 Other correspondence, however, bearing on the matter I The monthly meeting of the Warrington Board of has recently been received by the School Board and Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday. the Education department may reconsider their decision. Dr. Patterson presided and there were also present the it seems a distinct anomaly to allow the National Revs. Father Wilson and T. R. Matthews Dr. Murray, schools to be enlarged and yet to refuse the enlarge- Messrs. Brooks, A. J. Jolley, A. Jolley, ,G. Pilling, T. meat of Victoria-road on the ground that the schools Ashcroft, J. Billinge, J. Robinson, J. Clarke, J. Payton, are large enough already. 1 S. Davies, J. Ball, W. Houghton, G. Mather, J. Turton' The Runcorn Guardians have at last decided to T. Ritchie, J. C. Sutton (clerk), F. Gate (master), and endeavour to move the Government re the last vaccina- T. Wright (superintendent Padgate Schools.) tion Act. Messrs. Linaker and Wright produced some THE COUNTY RATE. , startling percentages respecting the cost of vaccination The CLERK intimated that the County rate for the to the ratepayers,under the new regime compared with rural districts of the Union for the coming half-year the old. One thing is absolutely certain. The Bill is good was £3,025, or £4OO more than the amount a year ago. for the doctors. In the Runcorn Union the vaccination This did not include the part of the Union within the practice has gone up ai times while the receipts county borough. He had known the time when the have gone up 8 times compared with 1898. If the county- rate for the Union was only £1,200. Local Government Board can be induced to bring pres- Mr. W. HOUGHTON: Is anything included in that sure on the Government to alter the Bill a vast amount sun of £3,025 withrespect to the new Education Act" of money will be saved the Unions of the country. The CLERK replied that he could not tell. He did not think so. His plan hitherto had been, when they had agreed upon their estimate and their rate to add to the amount the last Union rate. He had then divided the three sums and put them in one precept. If the increase was a matter of only £5O or £6O, it was not material, but when it came to • £4OO it disturbed the balance of the Union, and he would be obliged to de- ' part from that rule. He would make precepts only for the poor rate proper. He would leave the preparation of the precepts for the county rate until he received the county rate itself. He did not see Why the county should not prepare their own rate, instead of placing it on the Guardians and making it appear as part of the poor rate., The amount of the future rate in regard to education, too, was now an unknown quantity. MORE ACCOMMODATION WANTED AT PADGATE.</p>
        <p>The Old Folks Treatwill be held on Wednesday next. T earnestly ask •the co-operation of all the tradesfolk, and townsfolk to make this pleasing annual a success. Full particulars can be obtained from Mr. G. Howard School Board offices.</p>
        <p>Thfil Rural Council and the Board of Guardians have resolved to alter materially the time of the Committee meetings and Council meetings. In future, after a certain date, the Council will onlymeet monthly for the transaction of public business. The Guardians will -meet fortnightly as usual and the various committees are to be entirely re-arranged. The scheme is, of course, open to modification as experience dictates. Mean- while there is every reason to believe that the scheme will be eminently successful.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning the Runcorn Urban Council at, a special meeting joined eighteen Urban and Rural Dis- trict Councils in petition against certain clauses of the London and North-Western Power Gas Bill whioh will shorPy occupy the attention of Parliament. '</p>
        <p>A recommendation of the Padgate Schools Committee 'which was approved was that in view of the congestion of Roman Catholic Certified Homes and the children's wards at the Workhouse, beds and other necessaries for six small children be provided in each of the four homes at Padgate. THE WARRINGTON INFIRMARY SUBSCRIPTION. It was decided that the Board, with the consent of the Local Government Board, should contribute a sub- scription of £lO ICs. per annum to the Warrington Infirmary.</p>
        <p>Magnetic healing is an art or tather a profession generally discounted .by practical men. Either from ignorance or supeficiality we ignore mental laws as though they were non-existent. Prejudice against personal magnetism is usually. owing to the abuse made of the science by charlatans or to the fact that the alleged cures have been effected so many miles away as to make investigation impossible. It will, then, come as a sur- prise to many Runcornians to know that there is a, gentleman living in Runcorn, Mr. T. Leach, of Church- street, who actually ckims to be acquainted with the art of magnetic healing to such an extent that tooth- ache, headache, insomnia, rheumatism, bad habits, neuralgia, etc., all vanish at his magnetic tonch. In .fact, practically all disease, it is alleged, is amen- able to magnetic treatment. After this the doctors will have to put up their shutters</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>It was a very poor game at Runcorn on Saturday. The mud, owing to continuous rain, was inches deep and the players had not been twenty minutes on the ground be- fore they were bespattered from head to foot. Naturally there was a very poor attendance—with such ,a bad day and a poor set of opponents. In spite, how- ever' of the odds Birkenhead played up right manfully. The forwards wheeled the ball out of the scrummage more times, on the whole, than the home pushers. And had the backs shaped differently there would not have been such a heavy score registered against them. Moran for Runcorn played a wretched game, and was decidedly off form. Even the try he scored was by no means the result of brilliant play. Jolly, on the other hand, works a trifle too hard individually for the success of his side. In the last two matches he repeatedly stuck to the ball and tried to sheer through an impervious mass of opponents instead of setting his line in motion. 'lrrespective of selfish- ness it is bad form to keep the rear guard waiting for work, to tire.the fol•vvards out with pushing, and to irritate the spectators with the everlasting scrum.</p>
        <p>Bravo Runcorn! The lions have been vanquished on their own ground in right royal fashion. If the Linnets can maintain their form there is no reason why the Northern Union and League Cups should not find "a resting place and borne" in Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above authority was held on Tuesday at Dutton. There were also present : Messrs. A. R. Norman, J. Douglas, T. Lea, the members of the Runcorn District Council, with Messrs G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk),J. Cunnah (master), T. Cooke, DePrez and J. Riley (relieving officers.)</p>
        <p>THE SPREAD OF SMALLPDX.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote under date Febru- ary 9th stating that representations had been made to them on the subject of the spread of smallpox by tramps. It was urged on the Board that, the present diffusion of the disease was largely due to infection conveyed from place to place by . persons of the vagrant cls. The danger to the public health as a consequence ap- peared-to the Board to be a matter of serious importance and they referred the Guardians to a circular letter of January 22nd 1902 impressing upon them the urgent nwessity of securing, as far as possible the vaccination and revaccination of all vagrants relieved. The Board understood that in some cases Guardians have been in doubt as to whether they could pay for the vaccination of vagrants who failed to present themselves for subse- quentinspection, and the Board directed were of the opinion that in such cases payment could be made. The Clerk said the letter would be forwarded to the proper authorities. The Guardians of the Congleton Union requested the support of the. Board tea petition praying that a Bulb brought before the House of Commons immediately providing for the compulsory vaccination of all vag rants not recently .vaccinated, admitted into casua wards during the month of April next. The Board decided to support the petition. VIE CENTRAL POOR LAW CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wright moved that delegates be sent to the above Conference, and that the delegates be Messrs. Mclntyre, Linaker, and G. F. Ashton (clerk) ; failing these Messrs. Lea and Antwiss. Mr. Woodward seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Talbot moved that delegates he not sent. They sent delegates to London who spent the ratepayers' money, and the Board did not receive any adequate recompense. Mr. Clarke seconded. •</p>
        <p>Mr. Edwards said the game was not worth the candle,</p>
        <p>A heated discussion (limed it being shown that the expenses of the delegates would be about £l5 and that the annual Conferences exerted a great, influence on the decisions of the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>On a vote being taken only three voted for tho amend- ment the Board voting solidly for the proposition.</p>
        <p>ALTERATIONS OP MEETLNG DAY. : • • Mr. Baxter moved that the scheme for the alteration of the Guardians meeting day be adopted. Mr. Lina.ker seconded, and the motion was passed. EXCESSIVE CHARGES OF VACCINATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker moved that the attention of the Central Poor Law Conference and the Poor Laws Union Association be drawn to the great increase of the vaccination fees and expenses consequent on the Vaccination Act of 1898, and that they re- spectfully be asked to pass resolutions requesting the Local Government Board to issue instructions to enable the Guardians to re-establish vaccination stations for the attendance of patients instead of domiciliary vac- cination, which would reduce the fees. In moving the motion Mr. Linaker said that in 1898 the number of persons vaccinated in the Union was 500 at a ccst of £5B ss. 4d. ; in 1902 therewere 1498 vaccinated at a cost of £470 18s. In other words there were three times as many people to be vac- cinated in 1903 compared with 1898, at eight and a half times the expense. if the delegates who attended the conference could persuade the Local Government Board to induce the Government to alter the Bill aconsiderable amount of money would be saved to the Unions.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wright seConded remarking that in Runcorn the increase numbers 100 per cent; Frodsham, 400 per cent; Budworth, 420 per cent; Halton, 450 percent. The increase in expenses was Frodshaan, '6OO per. cent; Halton, 800 per cent; Runcorn,B4o per cent; Budworth, 940 per cent. The resolution of the Committee was approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>AN UNQU.A‘LIFIED DENTIST AT EARLMTOWN.</head>
        <p>AN UNQU.A‘LIFIED DENTIST AT EARLMTOWN.</p>
        <p>At Newton Petty Sessions, on Thursday, Frederick Taylor, of Earlestown, late of Manchester, was charged, by the British Dentists' Association with fraudulently practising as a registered dentist. Mr. Turner, barrister of London, prosecuted, and said a private detective visited the defendant and asked him to examine his mouth, but he would not be tampered with unless de- fendant could prove he was a fully qualified man.. The defendant said he was qualified, and produced a piece of parchment purporting to be a diploma from the Medical Council. He admitted Dr. Latham had refused to ad- minister chloroform to one of his clients. Defen den was not a registered dentist under the Dentists Act,and it did not matter because he was a bankrupt. .Mr. Riley defended, and said the defendant had studied at Owens College, but had not been able to bebOthe fully qualified. Defendant WM fined .£5, includingitosts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>1.0.G.T. HOFF. OF E ARLESTOWN LOD GE.</head>
        <p>1.0.G.T. HOFF. OF E ARLESTOWN LOD GE.</p>
        <p>OnMonday evening the juvenile branch of the above lodge was visited by the Wesleyan Baud of Hope, when a good programme of songs and recitations was rendered. Songs by Miss Olive Booth, Miss E. King; recitations by Masters G. Cawley, Trevor, Francis, Miss M. Jollie, Miss E. Teece ; piano solo,Master R. Knowles. A short and interesting address was given by the Chair- man, Mr. Graham, supported by Mr. J. Gordon secre- tary, Miss Kate Chandley acting as accompanist. The visit was very much enjoyed and the hope was expressed that it would not be the last. A vote of thanks was passed to the visitors. The adult lodge was then held and an interesting report of the District Lodge held at Liverpool on Saturday was given and other business was transacted. There was a fair attendance of members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>LATE DR. WATKINS</head>
        <p>THE LATE DR. WATKINS</p>
        <p>The CLERK alluded to the death of Dr. Watkins, who, he said, was appointed medical officer for the Newton district in the year 1868 on his (Mr. Sutton's) motion as a guardian at that time. Dr. Watkins resigned that post in January, 1900, when, for the purposes of the Superannuation Act, eight years were added to his 32 years of service, and he was granted a superannuation allowance of £63 13s. Bd. DELEGATES.</p>
        <p>Mr. PILLING moved that the chairman, vice-chairman and the Clerk should represent the Board at the 31st annual Central Poor Law Conference. He . remarked that these conferences were of a most instructive char- acter, and lessons most valuable to Boag,ds of Guardians -were learned from them. (Hear, hear.) _</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLEY secondesd,and the Motionwas agreed</p>
        <p>LOAN OR INCREASED RATE?</p>
        <p>The CLERK intimated that the Local Government Board had approved of the plans for theconstruction of vagrant cells in the Workhouse at a cost of £270, also for the extension and improvement of the washhouses at a cost of £2,225. The CLERK said that with regard to securing die money for these purposes, he would advise the increasa of the rate by a penny for the next half year, instead of the borrowing of the money, the wisdom of which course he much questioned. Mr. Sutton went on to refer to the questions of labour colonies, old age pen- sions and 'education, and. said he could see, from the improvements being made in public life just now, that a series of additional charges were going to come on the poor law unions. As to where the money was to be got, particularly in agricultural districts, he would not ven- ture to express an opinion. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY said a considerable number of members of the Board consented to the adoption of the plans for these improvements on the distinct under- standing that the improvements should lie paid for out of a loan. That was a question to which there were two sides, and he knew that the feeling of many mem- bers of the Board was adverse to that of the Clerk on the matter. They felt that that and other improve- ments should be payable by loan, and that they should not saddle the present generation with an undue amount of expense.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY afterwards intimated that later he would give notice of a resolution that the money required should be borrowed.</p>
        <p>DISTRESS</p>
        <p>The CLERK said that Mr. Jenner-Pust, on behalf of the Local Government Board, had written inquiring as to the amount of distress existing in the Union. He (the Clerk) ilitd replied that although, unfortunately there were rffil,ny- people out of work in the Union, there was no material difference from the state of things which ordinarily existed; and as the: weather improved they hoped for additional opportunities for employment. VISITING THE WORKHOUSE. A .PERSISTENT TRADES COUNCIL SECRETARY. The question of the application of the Earlestown Trades and Labour Council to send delegates to visit the workhouse periodically was again raised. The CLERK intimated that in reply to the communi-- cation from the Earlestown Trades and Labour Council he had written to the effect that he knew of no author- ity the Guardians had to enable them to comply with The Council's request. The Board was empowered to appoint a ladies' cominittea, including other than Guardians, and had exercised that power, but the authority was limited to women. Later on, continued the Clerk, he wrote that the Board had come to the same conclusion as himself in . the matter. After that the Secretary to .the Earlestown Trades and Labour council seemed to have written to the Local Government Beard, asking if that authority were aware of any restrictions prohibiting Boards of Guardians from granting permission to del- egates of Trades Councils to occasionally visit work- houses. The Local Government Board replied that it, rested with the Board of Guardians in each case to de- termine whether permission to visit a workhouse should continually or otherwise, be granted to ahy such del- egates. The secretary to the Earlestown Trades Coun- cil then wrote acquainting the Warrington Board with the Local Government Board's views, and stated that his (Mr. Sutton's) former reply was :based on a mis- understanding of the Board's authority. Upon this he (Mr. Sutton) wrote to the Trades Council. He contended that unless the nature of their application was misunderstood, the 'Guardians' decision was not based on a misunderstanding. The Guardians were aware of their authority to permit ratepayers to see the workhouse, but person.s'so privileged could not examine the inmates on any matter, and the master or matron must be present, even if the visitor was a guar.: dian. He did not know of any instance .of objection being made to ratepayers visiting the workhouse at a period convenient to the master or matron. The request by the Trades Council was considered to be something more than mere visiting of the workhouse. Mr. SUTTON then read the orders of the Local. Government Board bearinr, on the subject. They pro- vided that a ratepayer could not as a right visit the Workhouse, nor could he, if admitted, insist upon examining the inmates with reference to their treat- ment, or on any other matter; or oblige them to answer hisinquiries. ..17t was customary to admit those desirous of seeing the Workhouse, but it was right that the mas- t ter or matron should be in attendance on the visitor, whether a Guardian or not. There was no regula- tion conferring !power on individual Guardians to , visit the Workhouse without the permission of the master, but the Poor Law Commissioners were of o- Jiinion that it was competent for the Guardians to effect an arrangement, by a bye law, enabling them to do so. The Warrington Board met that difficulty some years ago, as a result of which they could visit the , Workhouse at any time. They must take no part in the administration, but reserve their opinion's for Com- mittee or Board meetings. There was something else, continued MR. SurroN, they Might to consider, and he spoke . from long experience. There was no doubt the intention of Trades Council and others anxious to visit the Workhouse were good, and they had no desire to offend anybody; but Ito could ' tell them, from his experience that many had been in- mates of that Workhouse who had seen better days,who were there from no fault of their own. To them it was a terrible thing to have to conic into the Workhouse at all, and it was an additional humiliation to them to be subject to the gaze and inquiry of all sorts of visitors. That was apoint which ought to impress itself on those who desired to visit the institution—that they were humiliating many of the inmates. (Hear, hear). Of course inmates were entitled to be visited by those near to them, but even in that case the Local Government Board stipulated that precautions should be taken that the interviews should take place in such a way as not to disturb the minds Of other inmates.</p>
        <p>Mr A. J JOLLEY moved that the action of the Clerk in the matter be approved. Mr. A. JOLLEY seconded, and the motion was agreed to. FREE VACCTNATION FOR CASUALS.</p>
        <p>With reference t-O--t-b-e--d-anger --of smallpox being spread by tramps, the Board approved of a notice being placed in the casual wards offering free vaccination to :casuals. There was no other business.</p>
        <p>UNDER the CLOCK for GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. BASSINETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR ALT.—UNDER the CtocK.—Advt.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER the CLOCK for BEST BOOTS in LANCASHIRE.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
