      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>MAKERFIELD MALE VOICE CHOIR.</head>
        <p>THE MAKERFIELD MALE VOICE CHOIR.</p>
        <p>IWhen the Makerfield Male Voice Choir was formed some six months ago it was felt that a long established want in the district bad been supplied and the success which attended the opening concert which was held in the Town Hall,Earlestown, on Thursday evening, fully con- firmed this belief. The Hall was packed and the splen- did audience could not do anything but encourage the Society. The choir numbers about 40, the majority of the ".musical men "of Earlestown and Distriot being in- cluded in its ranks, and the audience were treated to a magnificent performance of what they have i hitherto had to travel outside the neighbourhood to ob- tain viz: Male Voice Singing. Mr. Edwin Leyland acts as conductor of the choir, who opened the entertain- ment by a spirited rendering of "Spartan Heroes," Mr. Entwistle taking the tenor solo part. This was followed by a song entitled "Softly sighs the voice of evening" by Miss Ignatius Beatty who possesses a lovely soprano voice. "She alone charmeth " a bass solo from "Irene," was next rendered by Mr. Fowler Burton t followed by a humorous recital entitled "Love in a balloon," by Mr. Fred Duxbury, of Manchester. "0 dry those tears," from Miss Ethel Peploe, a sweet contralto singer, was vociferously applauded; as also was "Tinker's Song" by Mr. Fowler Burton. The first part of the programme was I brought to a close by a rendering of "The Crusaders" by the choir, Mr. J. Nicholls taking the tenor solo. DuriNg a ten minutes' interval the efforts of the choir were fully commented upon, and it was generaliyagreed that the choruses had been handled with a great deal of skill. Mr. Leyland had his singers well in hand an this undoubtedly contributed in no small degree to the sue-1 cessful rendering of the parts. The choir opened the second half of the programme with a chorus "The Pilgrims." For her rendering "Our Ships "Miss Ethel I Peploe received an encore. singing A little silver ring," as also did Miss Beatty for "The Nightingale's Trill," afterwards giving "The Swallows." Mr. Fowler Burton followed with a couple of songs" Bois Epais " and" Border Ballad" for which he was deservedly en- cored. "How Rubenstein played the piano," a humor- ous recital by Mr. Duxbury, was followed by a song " For all Eternity" by Miss Beatty after which the pro- gramme was brought to a conclusion by the" Soldiers' Chorus" from "Faust" by the choir. Mr. S. Dring acted as accompanist in his usual efficient manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above authority was held on Tuesday under the presidency of Mr. J. Pritchard. THEIBOARD OF ,EDUCATION AND THE PROPOSED PLANS</p>
        <p>FOR EXTENSION.</p>
        <p>j At a meeting of the School Management Committee ' accounts amounting to £6Ol us. 4d. were passed for payment. The following letter was read from Mr. Montague Sabin of the Board of Education "Adverting to your letter I am directed to return the enclosed plan • and to State that the proposed method of enlarging the school by adding to the accommodation in the already existing departments is not satisfactory. lam to point out that these departments have already reached the limit of size sanctioned by this Board. H.M. Inspector suggests that if a further. increase in the accommodation for children in the school district is necessary, plans for a separate junior department :Should be submitted. Recommended glat 40 children ! be transferred from the Victoria Boys School to, Gran- ville-street. An application for increase of salary by Mr. A. Parr was declined on the ground of the Board's approaching dissolution.—Recommended that the Chairman and Clerk attend the annual meeting of the Association of , School Boards to be held in London, and that the Clerk's expenses be paid by the Board. I Mr. Handley moved the confirmation of the minutes. He said, referring to the letter from the Board of Edti- cation that H.M. Inspector had paid them a visit and- other correspondence had been received by the clerk j within the past few hours. He therefore moved the re- appointment of tha sub-committee—Messrs. Hindley, Imison, and Pritchard—who carried out the temporary I arrangements for the overcrowding to bring up a report lat the next meeting. It was satisfactory to note that the percentage of attendances for February was 87 com- pared with 842 last year. This was the highest yet at- tained and would have been 10 per cent, more were , there no boat children in Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Mr. Posnett seconded and the minutes were approved</p>
        <p>RESIGNATION OF FATHER CHAMBERS,</p>
        <p>A letter was received from the above rev. gentleman resigning his membership with the Board. 'Ellison Mr. 'son gave notice of motion that at the next meeting he Would 'Move that Father Chambers successor FatherDouleVy be appointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ANOTHER RESIGNATION.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER RESIGNATION.</p>
        <p>The resignation of Mr. Brown, assistant master at Victoria-road. was considered and the Clerk was in- structed to advertise for another assistant master.</p>
        <p>--..-_. i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above authaity held on Tuesday at Dutton Workhouse under the p dency of Mr. Woodward ;—Present C. Roylance,.A. IL Talbot, E. Clarke, J, Mclntyre, J. Stokes, T. Baxter, C. E. Linaker, T. Lewis, •W. H. Bradford, T. Wright, G. Pilling, P. Gleave, E. Bretherton.J.Edwards,J. Spruce, J.Mountfield, J. B. Chadwick, T. Antwiss, W. Mewes, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk),Dr. Adams (medical officer). .3. Swinton, J. bigglec (sur- veyors), J. Farrington (inspectors).</p>
        <p>ELECT toss.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the County Council forwarded print of an order made by the Council on February 12th, fixing Saturday, April 4th, as the day for the. election ,of Urban District Councillors and Guardians in urban parishes. The date for the election of rural district councillors was fixed for April 6th.</p>
        <p>MEETING DAYS.</p>
        <p>The Committee appointed to consider future meetings of the Rural District Council, and the re-arrangement of committee meetings, recommended that from April 15th next the District Council should meet monthly, that on those days the Board of Guardians should trans- act relief business only, considering general business once a month only. __</p>
        <p>On the motion or Messrs. Baxter and Linaker, the scheme was approved.</p>
        <p>AN ALLEGED SOURCE OF INFECTION. ,The Clerk read a letter from the Moore Parish Council stating that the hospital van was allowed to remain in the centre of the village after taking a patient to the hos- pital, without being disinfected, and expressing the opinion that if this were allowed, the disease would never be stamped out. Dr. Adams said he had inquired into the complaint and found there was absolutelyno cause for alarm what- ever. The permanent van in Mr. NValton's yard was for disinfecting .bedding and could not possibly spread disease. Another van probably the one meant did stay in Moore a single night but no diseased cases had been previously removed in it.</p>
        <p>THE SPREAD OF SCARLET FEVER</p>
        <p>Admiral Jones-Parry wrote under date 20th February regretting that scarlet fever was spreading near the "Dog and Dart." If a hospital had not been found, the Lymm authorities had a house just on the boundary where,perhaps patients,could be taken in. The Sanitary Inspector might also watch that the stream running into Thelwall did not get polluted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WEASTE-LANk; SEWERS, THELVVALL</head>
        <p>WEASTE-LANk; SEWERS, THELVVALL</p>
        <p>Mr. R. A. Naylor wrote from St. Michaels House, London, requiring a definite answer to his request re- specting the Syphons at the lower end of the two sowers. The Clerk's first letter was but an evasion of the ques- tion and the last merely referred the matter to the medical officer. The sewers which would be confined more than before would set as air shafts and send the effluvia up them. If in dry weather any of the traps became short of water the poisonous fumes would enter the houses. The Council would also have to deal with other questions which he was now precluded from deal- jug with., . . .</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams said he did not think it added to the dignity of.the Council to engage in a controi-ersy over the sewer question. The better plan would be to finish the work and then let Mr. Naylor make what complaints he liked. Dr. Adams then entered into the sewerage of Weaste-lane, pointing out that it was impossible. for "sewers to be confined" and yet act as "air shafts."</p>
        <p>THE THELWALL NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>• Dr Adams said that the report of Professor De La Pienne would be ready for their next meeting.</p>
        <p>It was resolved that a committee. consisting of the Council be appointed to meet before the Council meet- ing in order to consider the report.</p>
        <p>THE RE-APPOINI'MKNT OF DR. ADAMS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wright gave notice that at the next meeting he would move the re-appointment of the medical _officer. THE NORTH-WESTERN ELECTRICITY POWER GAS BILL.</p>
        <p>The committee appointed to consider the above Bill recommended to send the Chairman (Mr. LinakeY) to Crewe to discuss the details and to sign,a petition pro- testing against some of the clauses of the Bill. , Passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>S ANA TOR it .</head>
        <p>THE S ANA TOR it .</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams said he had carefully inquired into the alleged overflowing of the sesspools at the sanatorium and was satisfied that if there had been such a nuisance there Was none at the present time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>SMALL-PDX AT HALTON.</head>
        <p>SMALL-PDX AT HALTON.</p>
        <p>Dr. Adams, in his report, stated that there had been two cases of small-pox at Halton. The premises, clothes etc., had been disinfected, and the contacts vaccinated and placed in quarantine. The bedding &amp;c. had been destroyed. There was nothing to show how the out- break had occurred. Possibly ithad arisen through the giving out of handbills in the village some ten days before. Dr. Carruthers had vaccinated 203 cases during the past week. • This was all the business.</p>
        <p>LETTERPRESS PRINTING, AND BOOKBINDING.</p>
        <p>WITH THE GREATEST DESPATCH.</p>
        <p>EXAMINER PRINTING WORKS. BEWBEY-STREET,WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>VIADUCT INSTITUTE, EARLESTOWN. Lecture on "Federal Australia."</head>
        <p>VIADUCT INSTITUTE, EARLESTOWN. Lecture on "Federal Australia."</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening, Mr. Herbert Jones, F.R.G,S., the Australasian lecturer, delivered a lecture at the Viaduct Institute, Earlestown, On "Federal Australia, the new Commonwealth in the far South." In the absence of Mr. J. W. Emmett, the chair was occupied by the Rev. S. D. Thomas, the Institute being well filled. • Mr. Jones, who delivered a lecture in the Earlestown Town Hall twelve months ago on "New Zealand" and is therefore well known in the district, commenced his lecture by describing Australia as "the land of tbe Wattle, the Waratah and the Eucalyptus; land of . the Emu, and the Kangaroo, of the Lyre bird, the Bower bird and the Ornithorhynchus." The land of contraries where things are found the reverse of what they are in Old England; where, stone will swim and wood will sink; where oysters grow on trees; where the ducks go on four feet, anti the quadrupeds on two. A continent which is twenty-six and a quarter times the size of Great Britain and Ireland, and yet with a population only two-thirds of London. A vast country with eight 'thousand miles of coast line; two tkousand miles from east to west; nineteen hundred and seventy miles from south to north'. Some portions of the interior of the continent are below the level of the sea. A country that may be des- scribed as the smallest of the five continents; and the largest island in the world. it is possible to make a railway journey in this continent equal to the great ran across the American continent, covering altogether considerably over three thousand miles, and passing through four out of the five states. If Australia were divided into 100 parts, West Australia would take 33 parts of the 100; South Australia 28; Queensland 20; Victoria 3, and New South Wales IM. Undere the old order of affairs in Australia, muddle, confusion and inconvenience reigned supreme; thers was no cohesion. What was the reason? The answer was simple enough. These • colonies were founded in the very early days. There were no rail- ways at that time, no postal telegraphs, telephones, nor I any up-to-date appliances. As a result each colony was as isolated from each other as the whole continent now is from England. One colony had nee trade whilst another had no such protection, one colony with a narrow gauge, and another with a broad gauge. In the early days of the railways if you embarked on a railway journey from New South Wales to Victoria' you were forced to get out at some wayside station at midnight and submit to having your baggage searched by an insolent Customs official. The great industry of Australia is sheep farming. A 'snaall" flock of sheep is estimated at 20,000; a " large " flock would be a quarter of a million. 340 millions of moneyare invested in the.ipastoral industry of Australia.. The country has suffered terribly by the recent drought through which they have passed, but the resources of the Commonwealth are so great that she could stand a good many droughts. The sheep farmers of Australia are the aristocracy of the country. The next great industry of Australia is mining. It is to the gold-mining that Australia owes most of her pros- perity today. The Commonwealth is one of the richest mineral countries in the world ; her wealth in this direction is fabulous ; what she has not got above the soil, she seems to possess below the surface. The eight hours labour system obtains all over Australia and has proved a huge succoss. The working man is the great factor, and he believes—to use his own words—in " hours work, 8 hours play, 8 hours sleep, and 8 . bob a day" and the greater part of his belief is realised. In describing the chief towns of the Com- monwealth the lecturer remarked that one million letters, post cards etc.. pass through the Post Office at Sidney every day, The people of Australia greatly believe in holidays. They have more holidays than any people in the world with the exception of the Russians, the only difference being that in Russia they are " Holy Days," in Australia " Holidays." The lecturer said that he had once made a statement to the effect that in case of need, Australia could put 30,000 men in the field, and although he was freely ridiculed, this fact w,!s Upheld during that dark December two yea Ts ago, when New South Wales alone offered to place 100,000 men into the field if required. Some eleven years ago a certain London paper contained an article -from the pen of a man who had spent as many months in Australia as he (the Lecturer) had years, stating that the Australians were a .disloyal race, and were only waiting for an opportunity to round on the old country. He (the lecturer) was asked to reply to this article, but he had preferred to give his reply from the platforms of the country. He would give the lie direct to that statement, and after thesplendid achie, - meats of the Australian forces during the recent war, no further proof of this was needed. (Applause.) Votes of thanks to the Lecturer and Chairnum brought the proceedings to a close, the large audience having I thoroughly appreciated the lecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE BALL AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The annual ball promoted by the Earlestown Con- servative Club took place on Friday evening at the Town Earlestown, and was of a .successful character. The room was very prettily decorated for the occasion 20 dancing was opened by Mr. and Mrs. C. F. 11.rrtin. Music was supplied by Mr. Sudworth's band, and danc- ing was kept up with much spirit until about 3 o'clock on Saturday morning. The .M.C.'s were Messrs. H. Hibbert, E. Walker and A. Jones, and the catering was carried out by Mr. G. Edwards. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>MACHINE MEN'S DINNER AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>MACHINE MEN'S DINNER AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The men employed in the machine shop at the Vulcan Foundry held their third annual dinner at the Blue Bell Hotel Newton on Saturday last. About 40 sat down to an excellent repast provided by Mrs. Hibbert, the hos- tess. The usual loyalltoasts were drunk. After dinner a smoking concert was held. Mr. E. Jones presided sup- ported by Mr. J. Boyer (Hon. Secretary). Musical items were rendered by several of the company present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND DISTRICT P.S.A</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND DISTRICT P.S.A</p>
        <p>In connection with the above Society and in aid of the organ fund, an entertainment was given in tEe Co- operative Hall, Earlestown- on Wednesday evening. Mr. J. Jackson presided and there was a very fair at- tendance. The entertainment-consisted chiefly of se- lections on the Gramaphone and Phonograph by 'Air. J. Smith, songs were given by Miss Allen, and Mr. T. Macklin, and recitations by Mr. T. Kenny. An item which was well received was the descriptive song " Chin, Chin, Chinaman "by the boys from the Earles- town- District School. Miss Will honson officiated at the piano.</p>
        <p>EA RLESTOWN WESLEYAN CH: U [lc 11.—WEsLE v Gu ILD SOCIAL.</p>
        <p>• A social evening in-connection with the Brunswick Wesleyan Guild washeld in the Lecture Hall under the chapel on Monday evening. There was a crowded., attendance and in the absence of the President (the Rev C. E. Dove) the chair was occupied by Miss Newman An excellent musical programme was submitted and thoroughly. enjoyed. "Instrumental trio," Messrs Edmunds, Sudworth and Patten ; song, " The Better Land," Miss Wright ; song, " Good Company," Mr. J. Mather, song, "In Mellow Autumn Tide," Miss Wright; song, "The Sailor's Grave," Mr. J. Mather; Miss E. Gatridge accompanied at the piano. Games, &amp;c. were afterwards indulged in. Refreshments were p rovided at intervals. ST. PETERS DAY SCHOOLS, NEWTON. The thirteenth annual scholars entertainment given in connection with St. Peter's Day Schools, took place on Saturday and Monday evenings in the Town Hall, NN'ton. So popularhave these entertainments become that oneach evening the hall was packed with an en- thusiastic audience, who applauded the various items and sat down in no half-hearted fashion. As in previous years the entertainment opened with a display by the infants, who had been trained by Miss Baybutt (head mistress) and her assistants. The opening chorus was by the senior scholars, and consisted of "'Let our songs ! of joy ring out." This Wits followed by an action song "The Chinaman " by the Infants; The next item \Ps a: dialogue. and song by a juvenile nigger troupe, and the . " business " was capitally performed. Mach amuse- ment was created by the action song. " Won't, you buy a Dolly," given by the very young- est of the scholars, whose training must have taxed the energy and patience of those who had thent in hand. The closing item of the first part of the programme was the dialogue " The Tragedy of a Pin," by four mites. The second part of the enter- tainment comprised the Japanese Operetta "Princess ! Jo Ju," or the Golden Amulet "—(hy Clementine Ward). The charactors were as follows:—" The Emperor" (Hugh Meakin) ; " Ching Ching " (William Hindley) ; • Prince Shee Ma. Guin" (Harry Fletcher) : ". Prince , Fit Shu " (Maurice Banks) ; " Prince Go Bang " ! (Arthur Emmett) ; " Abud Hiram ". (Wilfrid Reeves) ; " Executioner " (Tom Parkinson) • Princess Ju " (Winnie Boyer). ; " Lunar "(Lily kitchen) ; " Spirits of the Night" (Mary Hassell and Margaret (lriffiths.) In addition there were Courtiers Princesses, Maidens and Messenger. The dresses were quite in keeping with the piece, and the representation was capitally gone through. Briefly the argument, is that Princess Ju Ju who reaches her 18th birthday was of a marriageable age, and .it - was expected that, a noble Prince would claim her for his baide. The Prince, with two com- panions duly arrives, disguised as minstrels, but having been robbed on the way of all their documents and jewels, they are mistaken for impostors and cast into a dungeOn, under sentence of execution. The Princess seeks the aid of Abud Hiram, a magician, who enables her to prove the identity of the musicians, and all ends happily. The actin,' and singing was extremely well done, and the children deserve the greatest creiht for their efforts. A vast amount of labour must have been expended in the preparation of the piece, but the reward of the teachers and the Headmaster was found in the packed audience and the unstinted applause. The pro- ceeds were in aid of the tower funds of the Church.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER the CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Advt. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF Toncs.-6.3, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL W ATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC is the KING OF TONICS.-63, SiLNKEY-STREET, WarrillgtOlL—AdVt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>GOLBORNE ASSAULT CA SE.</head>
        <p>THE GOLBORNE ASSAULT CA SE.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool Assizes last Friday Edward Forrester, 60, collier, indicted for an assault on a gin named Lily Dutton at Golbome, on the 30th of January, was sen- tenced to penal servitude for five years. Mr. Oultois appeared for the prosecution. FALSE PRETENCES AT HA Y DOC K</p>
        <p>I Elizabeth Hodgson was, at Newt-Zlieil.lows Police I Court, on Thursday, sent to goal for three months for I obtaining 10s. from Messrs. Richard Evans and Co., Haydock, by false pretences. She represented herself at the colliery offices to be the mother of Robert Allen, collier, of Earlestown, and stated that the lad's father was dead, and wanted money out of the lad's wages to tide her over immediate difficulties, which representa- tions were totally untrue. There were several convic- tions for similar offences. CRUELTY TO HORSES. A batch of cruelty to animals ,cases was heard at Newton-le-Willows Polled'. Court on Thursday morning, Joseph Davey, of Hindley, was fined 10s. and costs for .working a pony with a sore back at Golborne Dale-road, Newton-le-Willows; Mark Wood, Tanners'-lane, War- rington,for working a spavined horse at NVinwick on the 30th ult., was mulcted in costs; and Arthur Rogerson driver, and David Bennett, foreman, of Newchurch, Warrington, were fined 10s. and costs for working a horse with an injured knee at Newton.</p>
        <p>P. S. A.</p>
        <p>. The usual weekly meeting of the Earlestown and District P.S.A. was held in the Co-operative Hall,Earle- street, on Sunday afternoon. The hall was well filled Mr. George Warburton presiding. A stirring address on "Manliness "was given by Mr. P. H. Jackson. Miss Williams contributed several recitations, and violin. solos were rendered sby Mr.- George Edmunds. Mr *own took the hymnsolos and Miss Williamson accom 'polled at the piano. Mr. Smith presided at the organ JOHNS' CHURCH, EARLESTOWN.—SOCIAL EVENING.</p>
        <p>The annual ShroveTuesday social evening and dance promoted by the mothers of the above church was held in the Mission.liall on .Tuesday evening. There were about 130 present and Mr. Woodcock's orchestra played for dancing from half-past seven till 10.30. At inter- vals refreshments in the shape of pancakes were handed round and much enjoyed. The duties of M.C. were ably carried out by Mr. J. Warburton. The proceeds are to be devoted to the purchase of a new bell to go with the peal when the enlargement of the church is completed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WESLEY BAND OF HOPE.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEY BAND OF HOPE.</p>
        <p>An entertainment in connection with-iheTiVO.ve was given in the Lecture Hall of the Wesleyan Chapel, Earlestown, on Wednesday evening. Mr. J. Latham presided, and the programme was sustained by members of the Haydock Wesleyan Band of Hope. Songs, read- inrr's, recitations and piano solos were submitted and well received. A temperance address was given by Miss Pearson, and the entertainment concluded with a humorous dialogue entitled "Drunk a,nd Sober." - IVA RG RAVE CHURCH, EARLESTOWN. • . .</p>
        <p>A tea party and concert, in aid of the funds of the above church were given in the schoolroom on Tuesday evening. Owing to the very inclement state of the weather there was only a meagre attendance, probably about 150 being present, but the tea was excellently served and thoroughly enjoyed. The entertainment was given by'the Warrington Entertaining Society andone or two local artistes, including Miss L. Wright, Mr. J. Molyneux and Mr. J. Mather. Ttae Warrington artiste's included Messrs. W. H. Kendrick, S. Rowlands, E. A. Sanders, G. Kendrick, Greenough, and the various items submitted were very well given, and received with ap- preciation..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>DRUNKEN BOILERM AN.-SMART PENALTY.</head>
        <p>A DRUNKEN BOILERM AN.-SMART PENALTY.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, last Friday, Patrick Kelly, Earlestown, was charged by Messrs Riclutrd Evans and Co. with. drinking intoxicating liquor on the works of the colliery without permission of the management, and further charged with endangering lives. It was stated that on_ January 21 defendant was in charge of a set of boilers. The engine winder was obliged to go to him, and found there was very little water. He went to the donkey engine and started the pumps, with the .intention of refilling the boilers. Then defendant threatened him. Afterwards Kelly said he was drunk, and -wanted to go home. There was a narrow escape from a fatal explo- sionowing to defendant's conduct.—The Chairman said it was intolerable that defendant should have acted as he had, and the Chief Constable informed the Court that the man had a very bad record, a previous fine not having been paid.—Defendant was sent to prison for five weeks.</p>
