      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>INFLUENZA INCREASING.</head>
        <p>INFLUENZA INCREASING.</p>
        <p>The Influenza Epidemic is again spreading over the Country. The visitation is of a very prostrating nature, and the disease is certainly on the increase. The origin of the disease or rather the circumstances which bring it about is as much a mystery as ever. Youcannot trifle with Influenza. It undermines the Nervous System to a greater extent than almost any other disease, and it is Pneumonia that kills, and Pneumonia strikes the man who imagines he has only a mild form of Influenza, and can go about the street just as usual. It can hardly be doubted that the prevalence of Chest and Lung Complaints must be ascribed to the baneful influence of Influenza, whose after-effects so frequently take this form. It was a bad day for this Country when this malignant scourge first visited our shores. Year after year it has never since failed to revive at its due time, and to extort its tribute of human life. Having observed its ravages, and its dreadful effects upon the constitution on the occasion of its former visits, we cannot but regard its recurrence with much fear, as a great many have not yet, if they ever will,regain their former health. As a preventative, we know of nothing equal to Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. A few strong doses taken in time have often provedeffectual in ward- ing off attacks of this Malady, and always succefid in mitigating their severity when a patient is under their influence. Do you suffer from Chills, alternating witty Sudden Flushes. Severe Head-ache, accompanied by difficulty of breathing, and symptoms of Catarrh, Pains in the Chest,, under and between the shoulders, and a stiffness and soreness of the Muscles, Loss of Appetite and Nervousneis. If you suffer from any of these symp- toms, know that they are the forerunners of Influenza, and it behoves you to resort at once to a course of Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters. Br common consent Patients everywhere admit that they feel more depressed and miser_ able after an attack of Influenza than while under its influence, and more than they feel after an attack of any other illness. The best way to banish these miser_ able feelings is to take Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters The Vegetable Tonic. We can strongly and confident ' recommend this preparation, as it has treated seveer cases of Influenza, Heavy Colds, Extreme Weakness' and Langour, with greater success than any other known Remedy. We may also add that as a Remedy for Indi- gestion, Sleeplessness, Low Spirits, Nervousness. and Loss of Appetite, Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters stands unequalled. It is sold in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d., by all Chemists aud Patent Medicine Vendors, or sent direct, carriage free, for the above prices, by the sole proprietors: Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company Limited Llanelly, South Wales. Avoid imitations. See the name "Gwilym Evans" on Label, Stamp, and Bottle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A WARR INGTO'.4 MAN</p>
        <p>ASSAULTS HIS FATHER.-</p>
        <p>MAGISTRATE GIVES EVIDENCE. Benjamin Hatton(a clerk residing at 29, Froghall "Lane, appeared before Mr. Hayward and other mag- istrates at Warrington Police Court on Saturday,charged with being drunk and disorderly at 6.15 the previous night in Froghall Lane, and assaulting his father Joseph Hatton.—P. C's Andrews and Houghton spoke to ar- resting prisoner in his father's house. in the street he made use of filthy language. Prisoner's father said the prisoner came home in a drunken condition, and wit- ness asked him to leave the premises, but he refused to do so and butted him three times in the stomach. On being again requested to leave he struck witness who sent for the police. The language he used was unbear- able. Prisoner denied that he was drunk or that he struck his father. inspector Rowbottom said he saw prisoner at the Police Station.He was drunk andvery ex- cited.—Mr. Birchall,who gave evidence from the Bench, said that about six o'dlock on Friday evening Mr.Hatton sent his daughter for him (Mr. Birchall), as the prisoner was kicking up a row that was simply unbearable. He went across to the house. Three weeks ago Dr. Braide and a relieving officer consulted him as to the condition of the prisoner, who was supposed to be a lunatic. He was ordered to be detained 14 days until he had been thoroughly 'examined, but he had to be liberated at length as the doctor certified that the prisoner was suffering from violence and scoundrelisra of the worst type. Mr. Birchall described the conduct of the prisoner in the house. He had heard vile language in that court, but nothing to surpass what the prisoner said in the presence of his father and mother and sister. He thought the prisoner was "about the worst com- pound of wickedness be had ever met in that court."— Prisoner was committed to gaol for three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>WNLL-KNOWN CHARACTERS IN</head>
        <p>WNLL-KNOWN CHARACTERS IN</p>
        <p>TROUBLE.-LAWYER</p>
        <p>• AM...., • BECKETT GETS THREE MONTHS. • At the Warrington Police Court on Saturday a youth named Alfred Beckett, better known as "Lawyer" Beckett, was charged with having been drunk and dis- orderly in Bewsey-street on Friday afternoon, and with assaulting Police Constable Hayes. Hayes stated that he was on duty in Bewsey-street and saw the prisoner fighting with another man named Moran. Slmeone told them the police were coming and they ran away, but came back again. Witness requested Beckett to go away but he refused and on being taken into custody kicked witness severely about the legs. With the assistance -of Police Constable Hewitt he got him to the Police Station.. He was very violent NB the way down. P.C. Hewitt corroborated, and said he saw the prisoner kick P.C. Hayes.—Superintendent Monaghan stated that he had known the prisoner since he was a boy. He never worked and was incorrigible. He was ac,onvicted thief and slept out at night and stole suppers.— Prisoner was sent to goal for three months. Patrick Richard Moran, a loiterer, was charged with having been drnnk and disorderly in Bewsey-street, as in the last case, and also with making a loud and un- seemly noise in Winwick-street on February 14th. P.C. Hayes proved the charge of drunkenness, and said prisoner also used very bad language.—P.C.'s Newey and Stewart gave evidence as to the second charge and said the prisoner was shouting and, using bad language. —lnspector Rowbottom said he was present at the time and the prisoner was very impudent. He told him to put his stick down and he would shown him something —Superintendent Monoghan said Moran never did any work. This was his 17th appearance,—Prisoner was sent tolgaol for a month for the drunkenness, and fined 20s. and costs or another month on the other charge.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTO:sI MAN FINED FOR SMOKING LNSIDE A</p>
        <p>TRAIICAR.</p>
        <p>A fine of ss. and costs (l3s. in all) was imposed upon Henry Burrows, 50, Academy-street, at the Warrington Police Court on Monday for smoking inside a tramcar travelling between Wilderspool and Rylands-street on February 16th.—Mr. Stanley Wilson, deputy Town Clerk, prosecuted, and stated that defendarit entered the car smoking a cigarette. The conductor told him that smoking was not allowed inside the car, and defendant then went outside on the platform and continued smok- ing near the controller. The conductor told him that smoking was not allowed there and defendant put out the cigarette and again went inside the car. When the car reached Bridge Foot defendant struck a. match on one of the panels of the door, scratching it in two places, and lighted a cigarette. On being told that he would be reported defendant made use of an objection- able expression to the conductor. Defendant wps a very respectable man, and the Corporation always took these proceedings with reluctance, but they felt bound to protect their employes and the public from annoy- ance.—Conductor Clarke and Inspector Clarke gave evidence.—Defendant stated that he threw his cigarette away when requested, and denied striking a match on the panel. He knew that smoking was not allowed inside the cars.—The Chairman (Mr. F. Monks) said that as defendant knew that smoking was not allowed he ought not to have attempted it. He would be fined Zs. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Minor Cases.</head>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Messrs. F. Monks (in the chair), J. W Ha.yward, S. Wright, J. R. Young and H. Thornton. . FOOTBALL IN TITE STREET.—Nine youths, named Frank Smith, Enoch Smith, Albert Bennett, Percy Poole, William Spencer, Percy Fearnl ey,Arthur Bouchier and Frederick Wright, all of Hovle-street, and Percy Cleaton, G 4, Bewsey-street, pleaded guilty to playing football in Hoyle-street on February 13th at 8.40 p.m.— P.C. Baxendale proved the ease, and defendants were each fined 2s. 6d. R.S.P,C.A. PROSECUTIONS.— William Bate, 205 Winwick-road, was summoned for ill-treating a horse by working it whilst in an unfit condition on February sth.—P.C. Bruen said he saw the defendant in charge of a pony and cart, and noticed that the animal was lame on three legs—lnspector Print, said the pony was aged and quite unfit for work. It had since been destroyed. Afine of 58. was imposed.—Samuel Herbert Bridge. 2, Battersby-lane, was lined ss. for working a horse whilst in an unfit state,lnspector Print stating that it was lame and suffering from spavin. There was a large sore under the saddle.</p>
        <p>Hitherto certain Dutcls: Cocoas have been eon- siderro the highest in quality or any in the markr' , but</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>MAZAWATTEE COCOA</head>
        <p>MAZAWATTEE COCOA</p>
        <p>le even superlor to them —greatly superior indeed. With such a Cocoa you soon realize what Cocoa really is, and means, and should be."</p>
        <p>GORDON -TABLES, CM.. M.D., B.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDAY—Before Messrs. A. Thomas and A. R. Norman.</p>
        <p>AN UNLIGHTED VEHICLE.—Enoch Ashbrooke, of Halton, was summoned for neglecting to have a lightat the rear of a vehicle which was carrying a load project- ing more than six feet to the rear.—P.C. Upton proved the case stating that the defendant had a light in front but none behind.—Ashbrooke, who was represented by his master was fined 14s. 6d. including costs. A CANDIDATE FOR THE BLACK LlST.—Michael Coulan was summoned for being drunk and disorderly on the 15th February.—P.C. Gilpin proved the case.— Fined 10s. including costs or 7 days in default. EVADING PAYMENT OF RENT.—William Gillet, for- merly of Shaw-street, was summoned at the instance of John Owen, 85, Greenway-road, for removing goods to the value of £5 in order to avoid distress.—Applicant said that the defendant owed £4, and that he had told him he must not move the goods. He moved them on February 10th, and had since found out that he resided at 10, Limekiln-lane, Northwich. He asked the magis- trates to make an order for payment.—The Bench ordered that the arrears be paid at the rate of 10s. a month.</p>
        <p>OBSCENE LANQUAGE.—Thomas Killgrass, Weston,was summoned for using obscene language on the 14th inst. —P.C. Rowlands proved the case, and defendant was fined 10s. including costs or 7 days in default.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>EDUCATION ACT.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION ACT.</p>
        <p>Co-optative Members of the Warrington</p>
        <p>Committee.</p>
        <p>Recommendations by Local Bodies.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Fulton Smith has been appointed to represent the Warrington Chamber of Commerce on the new Education Committee. A special meeting of the Chamber was held on Monday for the purpose of select- ing a representative. The Mayor presided over a good attendance of members. _</p>
        <p>Mr. MONKS proposed the election of Mr. C. E. Parker, of Penketh.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pußpoorr seconded. Mr. J. H. SMETHURST moved as an amendment the appointment of Mr. Fulton Smith, and remarked that the Chamber had already honoured Mr. Parker by elect- ing him their president. If there was a further honour to be given it should be given to another member. In his opinion that member was Mr. Fulton Smith, who regularly attended their meetings and represented one of the largest firms in Warrington. (Hear, hear.) Mr. HANKDTSON seconded, and the motion was cairied.</p>
        <p>, Mr. FULTON SMITH returned thanks, and Mr. PARKER expressed the pleasure it afforded him that Mr. Fulton Smith had been elected to the position, although he would have been quite pleased to serve on the com- mittee if it had been the wish of the Chamber.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>WOMAN WHO GOT GOALS IN.</head>
        <p>A WOMAN WHO GOT GOALS IN.</p>
        <p>"To all women her story is of vital interest." So wrote the chief reporter of nne of the leading North of England Newspapers when referring to the remark- able case of Mrs. Isabella Dawson,the wife of a Durham collier. She lives now at 41, High Westwood, near Shotley Bridge, Durham, and is at present so hale and hearty that the other day she "got in "a load of coals. Chas. Forde's Bile Beans have transformed her in a few months from a weak sickly woman, who was unable to do a bit of housework, who had palpitation if she stirred, and was so dizzy when she tried to walk that she had to cling to furniture in order to support herself, to a woman who can get coals in and feel no worse for it. Here is her story! If you are ailing don't miss it. "Just over twelve months ago " she says, "I was seized with sick headaches of a most distressing kind, accom- panied by a choking sensation after meals. At night I had ringing noises in my head, which kept me from sleeping. My heart was affected at the same time, beating so heavily as to mako me throb from head to foot.</p>
        <p>I gradually became so yellOw that my family believed I was going to have jaundice. I used to vomit for hours and alter every attack of vomiting I was knocked up and unable to do the least bit of housework. If I hurried or turned round suddenly I became dizzy, and was obliged to cling to the furniture, or sit down to prevent myself from filling; while the palpitatiou became most painful. I could not eat a bit of sweet cake, or the smallest piece of meat, without vomiting it again. In- digestion and acute liver trouble were the real cause of my illness, but although I took no end of doctor's medi- cine I got gradually worse instead of better. I was at my worst when a friend came to see me, specially in order to advise me to try Bile Beans. My mother and sister at Consett had also been taking them, and had found them so good, that they, too, were persuading me to try them. I bought a box and began to take them regularly. To my great delight I soon felt an improvement; and in a few weeks I was free from the ailments which had made my life a burden. I am now as well and hearty as if I had never ailed in my life. Only the other day my daughter and I got a load of coals In, I didn't feel tired with the job in the least." Chas. Forde's bile beans are purely vegetable in composition and the finest remedy which science has yet discovered for all ailments which rise from a disordered state of the digestive system and liver. Again and again they have been shown to be an absolute specific for indigestion, biliousness, sick head- ache, weakness, liver and kidney disorders, pains in the side, loins, and back, constipation, piles, dizziness, sleep- lessness, nervousness' anaemia, and all female ailments. Also for colds, chill, neuralgia, lumbago, rheumatism, and as a preventive of influenza. Bile Beans may be had of all chemists, or post free from the Bile Bean Manufacturing Co., Red Cross-street, London, E. 0., on , receipt of prices one and three-half-pence or two and nine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>SALVATION ARMY ARMY WORK.</head>
        <p>SALVATION ARMY ARMY WORK.</p>
        <p>Meeting in Bold-street Schools,</p>
        <p>Warrington.</p>
        <p>All people are more or less familiar with the Salva- tion Army, but probably comparatively few are fully aware of the magnitude of the work carried on by that organization. Some interesting details of the social operations of the Army were submitted at a meeting held in Bold-street 'Wesleyan schools on Monday even- ing, when Mr. Ben H. Mullen delivered a lecture upon the subject. There was a fair attendance, Councillor James Dodds presiding. There.were also present on the platform :—Messrs. C. E. Parker, H. Thornton, and Dr. Ferguson, and Captain Simms, who has charge of the work in Warrington. -The Chairman alluded to the great and varied work of the Salvation Army, the operations of which, he said, were carried on in 49 countries and colonies, the Gospel being preached in no fewer than 31 languages. The army was doing a groat work in our own country among the degraded and debased,and was leading people from squalor and sin and putting them on the platform of the redeemed and regenerated. The army had sus- tained its freshness and vigour and had retnined its or- iginal methods and had not gone dead like so many of the churches by becoming too respectable. It had re- ceived praise from the highest quarters in the world. The late Queen Victoria in a memorable, letter said "Praise God for the work of the Salvation Army." The present Bishop of Manchester had also eulogised the work of the organization and he (the Chairman)was sure it would have the support and practical sympathy of everyone present. He had no doubt when the end came that the Salvation Army would take its place a- mong the very first of those who had worked out the Salvation of the world (Applause). Mr. MULLEN then delivered his lecture, and said that the subject was one of enormous magnitude and it would be impossible for the work to be fully described from any one standpoint at any one time by any single individual. He spoke from experience and could say that no institution could approach the Salvation Artily as regarded the amount of success attending their efforts to do the greatest good for the greatest number. He appealed to his hearers to give the workers a helping hand in order to raise poor people out of the mud in which they had fallen not all by their own fault, but many by misfortunes. Mr. Mullen went on to describe various branches of the work, referring to shelters for the homeless, the providing of cheap meals homes for young people who desired to lead a better fife ltc. The Army did not exist to fight the churches and chapels and religious communities. It existed to fight sin, misery, drunkenness and gambling, and to turn people from sin to righteousness, from the service of Satan to the service of God. (Applause.) Statistics as to the number of cheap meals and lodgings supplied were fur- nished, and the Lecturer said the work of the Army was increasing year by year by leapsand bounds. (Applause.) The CHAIRM AN, in commenting upon the motive which animated those engaged in the work of the Army, said it must be love of God and man which prompted them to do it. He was informed that during January and February no fewer than 3,500 free meals had been supplied by the Army in Warrington, and that ought to induce Warrington people to help the cause. A collection having been taken Mr. C. E. PARKER moved a vote of thanks to the lecturer and said he thought there was a growing appreciation in all the Churches, of the good work that was being done by each Church. He spoke of the splendid work carried on by the Salvation Army at the homes at Hadley. He thought everyone would acknowledge that, the Army was doing a very grand social work and expressed the hope that assistance would be forthcoming locally to further their efforts. Mr. THORNTON seconded the motion which was heartily agreed to. The CHAIRMAN having been thanked for presiding, the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Examiner, Friday, February 27, 1903. THE FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, February 27, 1903. THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>A LAST VENTURE.</p>
        <p>Liverpool Woman's Thrilling Story.</p>
        <p>The case of Mrs. Mary Mann has excited such wide- spread interest in Liverpool, that a representative was sent to call upon the lady at her residence, 19, Rice- street, off Hardman-street, with a view to ascertaining the correct and unexaggerated facts. Mrs. 'Mann, a pleasant-faced little body, narmtedhow she would mall probability have been a sufferer to this day had she not been fortunate in discovering the remedial virtues of' Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. "For," said she, "I always enjoyed splendid health and nothing ever ailed meat all up to two years ago, when misfortune overtook me in the form of indigestion. I had severe pains all about the chest and between the shoulders. My head always felt heavy and hot, and I was often very sick. I had no spirit. Even going to bed was of no use. I could not sleep. I felt hungry, but I could. not eat because to do so used to give me such fearful pains afterwards. I felt no interest in 1 life, and I did not care whether I lived or died. I tried all sorts of things from the chemist to obtain relief, ' and I also pla,ced myself under the care of skilled doc- tors, but in spite of all their sciencethe acute symptoms Icontinued, and the longer I suffered the more my interest in life decreased."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Mann went on to tell of her recovered happi- ness, which came upon her, shesaid, like a burst of sun- shine through heavy clouds. She was in a situation in Birkenhead, and a young lady, who had been cured of neuralgia and other painful afflictions, by Dr. Williams' pink pills for palepeople,after medical men had failedto do 'many good, recommended her to give them a trial. After she had taken them .for some time her digestion began to improve, and with a little perseverance she was eventually restored to her former state of complete health. Her appetite has returned. The pains have gone, and, she said, "I have never felt better in my life, and can complain of nothing." "And you attribute your wonderful recovery entirely to Dr. Williams' pink pills?" our informant asked. "Yes," she answered, "to them alone. They are the only thing that did me any good, and I shall certainly recommend them to other people who suffer as I did."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLARY IN SOUTH-WEST LANCASHIRE.</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY IN SOUTH-WEST LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>Annual District Lodge Meeting.</p>
        <p>Good Tempters assembled in strong force in the Gordon Smith Seamen's Institute, Paradise-street, Liverpool, on Saturday, for the annual session of the District Lodge. Bro. the Rev. J. A. Lee, D.C.T. pre- sided over the meeting. representatives and visitors being present from Liverpool, Warrington, Earlestown, Southport, Leigh, and other parts of the district. At the outset, Bro. Lee feelingly announced the death of Bro. D. P. Sykes, P.D. Chaplain (Southport) and the lodge passed a resolution of condolence with the rela- tives. A large number of distinguished visitors were received with honours from W. Cheshire, and Denbigh- shire.</p>
        <p>Bro. Lee, D.C.T. presented his annual report, and stated that the year was not as favourable as he had hoped it to be, but they were progressing. The mem- bers were firm in their convictions with regard to the drink traffic as being the greatest curse of our land. They were not even prepared to take the dictum of a Lord Mayor on that subject. (Hear, hear.) He referred in glowing terms to the services of the district visitor, who had done good services in Liverpool, Leigh, St. Helens, Ormskirk, Ashton and several other places. Several donations had been received towards retaining the services of Sister M'Cloud. (Hear, hear.) The Dis- trict was greatly indebted to Bro. G. 13. Walker, D.Treas., for his willing services as D.Sec., pro tern, during the serious illness of Bro. Morris Jones. In con- clusion, he heartily thanked the Executive for the help they had given him during the past term of his office. (Applause.) .Bro. G. B. Walker, D. T. presented the report of the D. Sec. which showed a slight increase on the quarter. At the last quarterly session there were 2,419 members in 52 lodges, and now there were 53 lodges with a wen- bership of 2,562. This was, however, a slight decrease on the year. The largest increase reported was in the "Norwood "lodge Liverpool, which had an increase of 36 on the quarter, while the" Offspring " lodgestoodat the other end with adecrease of 30. Two lodges were reported sleeping: "Forward," Runcorn, and" Rescue" Leigh. He referred in fitting terms to the German- speaking lodge, " Friihlings Anfring "which had re- cently been opened in Liverpool with 22 members and wished them every success (applause). The 'report of the District Executive contained some very good suggestions for reform. Special mention vvas made of the good work done by the No. 1 Convention and also by the district visitor. The committee appealed for funds for the purpose of carrying on Mission work, and an interesting discussion followed on the subject. Bro. E. Boreland, D.E.S. (Liverpool) reported on the electoral work of the Order. He severely criticised the action of the authorities at Port Sunlight in apply- ing for a license under the "Trust" system, and the speecheslmade by the Lord Mayor of Liverpool on the drink question. Mention was made of the action of a large number of Licensing Benches in refusing the renewals of licences. The year had been a very hopeful one as they would see from the passing of the Chil- dren's Bill and the New Licensing Bill. The time had come for a further effort on behalf of the Sunday Closing Bill. There was as much prospect of getting. this Bill passed bya Tory Government as from a Liberal Govern- ment. (Applause') He had an idea that a Government which practically represented the National Church would not seriously support the National curse. (Hear, hear). He moved a resolution re the further introduc- tion of the Sunday Closing Bill. Several members spoke in support and the motion was unanimously carried.</p>
        <p>Bro. E. Newman, D.S.J.T. (Earlestown), in report- ing on the juvenile work, said that good work had been done in most of the temples. The last report showed 1,741 members in 21 temples, and now there were 1,753 in 23 temples. There were also 79 adult members. Men- tion was made of the excellent work of the "Young Mission " Temple, Warrington, which opened six months ago and now has a membership of 75. (Applause.)Seveml Liverpool temples reported decreases the chief reason being said to be that Church of England Bands of Hope had opened in the same room. The dis- trict had been represented in the National Competitive Examination and two members had obtained honours, eight first class certificates, 11 second, 13 third, and there was one failure. (Applause.) Sister Mrs. Walker reported that she guaranteed to collect five guineas in the year for Grand Lodge Funds, and she had collected 19s. more ,than that sum. (Ap- plause). Sister Duncan, Orphanage collector, reported that .C 1 2s. 6d. had been collected at the session for the Orphanage. There had been 9 more children admitted to the institution, and the schools were being enlarged. Bro. J. Oliver, Foreign Correspondent, reported having received letters from India, Natal and Jerusalem, showing good work being done in these places. He had sent a large number of letters and pamphlets to Aden, Transvaal, and other parts of the world. (Applause). The reports were all accepted with thanks to the officers.</p>
        <p>The election of Grand Lodge representatives resulted as follows :—Bros. Morris Jones, G. B. Walker J. Oliver (Liverpool), E. Newman (Earlestown), Sisters Mrs, Lee and Mrs. Walker (Liverpool) ; alternates, Bros. Patter- son, Oliver, (Liverpool), G. Clarke (Easlestown), Parr, (Leigh)Sisters Petterson and Duncan (Liverpool). The following officers were elected for the ensuing year :—D.C.T., Bro. J. A. Leo, (Liverpool) ; D. Coun., Bro. D. P. Petterson (Liverpool), D.E.S., Bro. G. T. Schofield (Liverpool) : D.S.J.T., Bro. E. Newman, (Earlestown) •, D,V,T„ Sister Mrs. Griffiths (Liverpool); D. Chaplain, Bro. A. A. Lee (Orrnskirk) ; D. Marshal, Bro. W. Room, (Liverpool) ; P.D.C.T., Bro. R. W. Kirkins (Bootle) ; D.A.S., Bro. J. P, Jones (Liverpool) ; D. Guard, Bro. P. Smith (Ashton) ; D, Sentinel, Bro. Hodgson (Leigh), The officers were installed by Sister Robson, P.G.V.T. During the afternoon, the challenge black and gavel for the largest proportionate increase during the year was presented to the Gordon Smith Lodge who report an increase of over 300 per cent. It was decided to hold the next session at Southport on May 30th..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WEsI,Ev Gutim.—The members of the Padgate branch of the Wesley Guild met in the Wesleyan School on Monday evening, Mr. W. Brough presided. A paper was given on A plea for enthusiasm," by Mr. F. Wright. The customary vote of thanks was accorded, on the motion of Messrs. King and London, supported by Dr. Bennett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>FREE SAMPLE BOTTLES</head>
        <p>FREE SAMPLE BOTTLES</p>
        <p>OF VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>The remarkable rapidity with which Veno's Lightning Cough Cure absolutely cures the worst cases of chronic Coughs, Bronchitis, Asthma, Influenza, Catarrh, and children's coughs has gained for it a scientific standing and many recommendations from leading authorities. GRANVILLE H. SHARPE, F.C.S.' Analyst. 11 and 12, Gt. Tower-street. London says in his certificate of analysis: "I am of opinimi that Veno's Lightning Cough Cure will prove a valuable remedy in the cure of Coughs, Colds' Bronchitis, Asthma. and all bronchial complaints." Ask for Veno's Lightning Cough Cure; 1/14 and 2/9 at chemists. Send 3d. postage for a large free sample bottle. Address: THE VENO DRUG CO., HULME MANCHESTER. MENTION THIS PAPER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>BRIGHTON MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>THE BRIGHTON MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>The Free Church world is eagerly anticipating the annual meetings of the National Free Church Council, to be held at Brighton on March 9, 10, 11, and 12. It is now evident that the attendance will be larger than on any previous occasion, and the Sessions will be of a thoroughly national character—delegates attending from the most northerly parts of Northumberland, and the most distant extremities of Cornwall and Wales. The programme has been pi epared with great care, and some of the most pressing problems of the day will be dealt with. The Sessions will commence on the Tuesday morning, but on the previous evening the Mayor of Brighton will hold a reception to welcome the delegates. It is interesting to note that the Mayor is a Con- servative and a Churchman, but sectarian differences do not prevent him extending 'a hearty welcome to Free Churchmen. It is also noteworthy that many Anglicans are providing hospitality for the members of the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>MEMORIAL SERVICE,</head>
        <p>MEMORIAL SERVICE,</p>
        <p>The opening Session on Tuesday, the 10th, will be a solemn occasion. Its interest will centre in the memorial service for the t*o great leaders'of Nonconformity who have joined the tnajority since the last annual meetings—Rev. Hugh Price Hughes, M.A., and Dr. Parker. The presidential address was already prepared by Dr. Parker before his death, and will be read by Dr. Vaughan Price. That the departed President was on the closest terms of intimacy with the reader of his last message to the Free Churches will add to its impressive character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>SECRETARY'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE SECRETARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>From the very nature of the Education Cam- paign, which has stirred the Free Churches to the depths, the report to be presented by Rev. Thomas Law, the General Secretary of the National Council, will be longer, and perhaps weightier, than on any previous occasion. Numerous and varied have been the activities of the Councils and Federations with which the report will deal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>SPECIAL TOPICS.</head>
        <p>SPECIAL TOPICS.</p>
        <p>Rev. C. Silvester Horne, M.A., will present to the Council what will, no doubt, be a very prac-. tical paper on the "Modern Misuse of the Lord's Day." The question of denominational overlapping is one upon which Dr. Alfred Rowland will doubtless throw clear light and be ready with valuable suggestions. "J. 8.," of the Christian World (Rev. J. Brierley, 8.A.), in view of the recent church census, is sure to have some true and keen things to say with regard to the "Free Churches and the Outside Masses " ; while the Rev. W. J: Dawson knows quite well how to treat effectively the inquiry, "Are we Losing our Young Men ?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>EDUCATION.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>One whole session, Wednesday morning, wiA be devoted to the discussion of the supreme question of the education of the children of the nation. No fewer than six resolutions are to be submitted, which will embrace in a most comprehensive fashion the many points that bristle in the Government's erratic measure. The movers and seconders of these resolutions, which have been unanimously passed by the General Committee, are as follows: Dr. Clifford, Dr. Robertson Nicoll, Dr. Monro Gibson Mr. R. W. Perks, M.P.' Mr. George White M.P., the Revs. J. Scott Lidgett and J. Hirst Hollowell, and Professor Massey, of Oxford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>COUNCIL'S PRESIDENT.</head>
        <p>THE COUNCIL'S PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>In consequence of the removal of Dr. Parker, the Rev. James Travis is to occupy the presiden- tial chair. He is an honoured minister of the Primitive Methodist Church. The Free Church Chronicle says of him: "For many years he has held a distinguished position in his own Church, and from time to time has been entrusted with some of its most important offices, among which may be named that at Secretary to the Missionary Society. He is a good debater, possesses a large knowledge of the law and constitution, has a fine judicial temper of mind, and not least in importance, when pressed can see the situation, and is able to retreat with grace and good humour where the ground is untenable. Above all, however, are these two facts—his highmindedness and thivalry that have compelled confidence, and the splendid service he has rendered his Church that has won the gratitude and admiration of her people."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>ROBERTSON, OF BRIGHTON.</head>
        <p>ROBERTSON, OF BRIGHTON.</p>
        <p>The Brighton meetings naturally call to niind the Rev. F. W. Robertson, M.A., popularly known as "Robertson of Brighton." August 15 of this year will be the fiftieth anniversary of his death. A sympathetic article from the pen of Montague Herbert appears in the _Free Church Chronicle for March. The writer com- ments on the fact that Robertson was unknown to fame at the time of his death—at the early age of thirty-seven—and his influence has almost wholly been post-mortem. He attempts to show what was the secret of this extra- ordinary influence which Robertson's sermons have exerted, and gives a fine appreciation of the great preacher's high character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>MINISTERS IN THE MAKING.</head>
        <p>MINISTERS IN THE MAKING.</p>
        <p>Candidates for the ministry no doubt view college life with anything but humour, but that it has its lighter sides is amply proved by W. Scott King in a sketch in the Free Churchman. The article is one of a series of "Humorous Side- lights of Church Life." and is entitled "Ministers in the Making." Mr. King recalls some funny incidents that happened in his college career.</p>
        <p>"SAME OLD EXCUSE!"</p>
        <p>"I wonder," Mr. King says, "whether my readers are familiar with the old chestnut of every college—the traditional story that is told year after year over study fires with unabated gusto. It relates how an old minister returned to his alma mater after forty years IV minis- terial service, and was being conducted over the old place. 'Same old corridors !' he ejacu- lated, as he entered, 'same old corridors!' They took him to the dining-hall. Ah me! same old dining-hall ! ' Then he opened a study door. Same old study—same old study! Half round the fire a screen was drawn, and behind it he saw a student talking with a young lady. Dear me ! ' exclaimed the old minister, pensively, same old practice! same old prac- tice ! ' The student sprang up indignantly. Excuse me. sir, this is my sister.' A smile broke over the ancient one's face. 'Ah! and the same old explanation! same old excuse ! '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>BRIGHTON'S FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>BRIGHTON'S FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>An excellent illustrated article is to be found in the Free Church Chronicle of the Free Churches of Brighton. Its publication is timely, and gives a deal of information concern ing the history of the various denominations of the town, which will be of value to the dele- gates who purpose attending the meetings. Portraits of the leading- ministers are given, and illustrations of Union Chapel, where the Rev. R. J. Campbell, M.A., ministers, the Dome, and the Royal Pavilion.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. G. Pidduck (captain) presided over the annual meeting of this club which was held in Cairo- street schools on -Monday evening. There was a large attendance of members—The annual report was sub- mitted by the Secretary (Hr. D. L. Owens) who referred to the various events of the season. "The attendance at club runs" Mr. Owen states, "compares favourably with other seasons, but there is still room for much improvement in this respect. Out of a total of 53 runs 16 were cancelled owing to wet weather and other auses. A. Buchanan is the winner of the medal for Thursday and Saturday runs combined; H. J. Powell, for the first Thursday runs ; C. Plinston, for second Thursday runs.; I), L. Owens, for first Saturday runs; and J. G-. Pidduck, for second Saturday rune. The club membership now stands at 50, which is a slight increase on the previous season: I have to record a loss which the club has sustained in the person of S. Warburton, who has emigrated to Canada, and also E. Myles who has joined Baden-Powell's Police. The club's success is keeping pace with its increase of membership, but if the members would take more interest in the working. of the club, we should have an organization second to none in the district." (Applause.) The report was unani- mously adopted, and the Treasurer's report and balance sheet were also approved.—The following officers were elected for the ensuing year :—President, Mr. W. H. Bellian ; . vice-prest dents( Messrs. J. C. Parr, R. C. Parr, R. Pierpoint M.P., E. Pickavance, E. Draper, W. H. Robinson,and • voCTOR NEARLY BLACK-LISTED.—Dr. Allen Wilson, who until a short time ago was in practice at Orncastle, L. W. Davenport; Captain, Mr. H. Davenport; sub- ! but who now lodges at Lincoln, On Wednesday had a captain, Mr. H. Powell; hon. auditors, Messrs. W. narrow escape of being placed on the black-list. The Jones, and T. Critchley ; hon. treasurer, Mr. J. G. Chief Constable of Lincoln asked the magistrates to Pidduck ; hon. secretary, Mr. D. L. Owens • assist- adopt this course. He was before the Court four times ant hon. secretary, Mr. O. J. Newton ; dele- last year, and on the 21.1 th December was bound over to gates, Messrs. 15. L. Owens; and C. J. Newton.-- appear for judgment in a month. Ile did so, and Votes of thanks were passed to the retiring officers promised to become a teetotaler, and on that under- standing he was discharged. Yet he was now before and to the Chairman. I the Bench again. The magistrates described the case .as a disgraceful one, but said .they would give the de- fendant a last warning. He wodd be fined 15s.</p>
        <p>TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>Most gratifying is the action of• the Free Church Councils throughout the whole country in regard to the issue of new licenses at Brewster Sessions. They have been up and doing with memorials, petitions and resolutions, which have been presented to the Justices and ht most cases courteously received. With many Councils Temperance Reform is essentially a religious question, and into it has been put the best energy and the keenest enthusiasm possible. Few things have given them more substantial joy than the knowledge that the working of the new Act has already produced an appreci- able reduction in the number of charges of drunkenness, with which magistrates have had to deal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Latchford.</p>
        <p>There were good congregations at this Church on Sunday, the preacher being the pastor, the Rev. J. F. Matthews. In the morning a baptismal service was held, and in the evening, when the church was crowded, Master W. H. Pearce,lhe boy organist gave an organ recital with considerable skill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was an average attendance at the meeting of this society on Sunday afternoon. Mr. Spruce occupied the chair and referred to his early associations with Wycliffe Church and School many years ago. Mr. B. S. Wilson, secrstary of the P. S. A. Federation, Liver- pool, delivered an interesting address on having a pur- pose in life. Mr. Spruce, A. of the Warrington Male Voice Choir was the soloist. Methodist Free Church Buckley-street.</p>
        <p>There were large congregations at the above church on Sunday when the evangelistic mission was continued by Captain W. and Mrs. Clark of Liverpool. In the morning Mrs. Clark took for her text the words: "The Lord is ray shepherd," and in the evening Luke x. and the verses 38 to 42. In the afternoon Captain Clark gave an address to the children in the Sunday school. The mission has been very successful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</head>
        <p>Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</p>
        <p>The anniversary sermons in connection with the above place of worship, were preached on Sunday by the Rev. J, Gardham Reed. Mr. Reed is very eloquent, and a scholar of the highest order, and the congregations that assembled to hear him, considering the severe weather, testified to the respect thathe is held in the town. The chapel is far too small to hold special services in, and steps are being taken to build a new and much larger place of worship. The Bible class was held in the after- noon, conducted by Mr. Reed. This claim is growing rapidly, every Sunday, and already over 100 members have joined. At the evening service, the choir rendered IMendelssohn's solo and chorus "Hear my prayer." Miss Alice Bridge was the soloist. The chorus was sung magnificently, especially the part where the choir accompanies the solo, and Miss Bridge is to be compli- mented on the admirable manner in which she sang the effective solo. Mr. Perri also sang with deep feeling a solo "Flee as a bird.' Mr. Albert Spruce, deserves Ithe best thanks for the resourceful way in which he conducted. Mr. Pred Podrnore efficiently accompanied on the organ. The collections far exceed any previous collections.—On the Monday evening, a tea and enter- tainment was held. About 60 sat down to a capital tea, several lady members of the congregation presiding at the tables. Mr. J. T. Rowlands took the chair at the entertainment, and was supported by the Pastor,Rev. J. G. Reed, and Mr. Griffiths. The Chairman at the outset, complithented the congregation on the financial result of Sunday's services, and said it showed a great improvement. Legh-street's golden days were yet to come. They should be more enthusiastic in church matters, and success would be certain. Speaking from a temperance standpoint he would like to see inaugur- ated what he might term "a serni-vigilanoe committee." Their young men and the young ladies could all do a good work. The former might see some younf men they knew going into a public-house, and use their in- fluence to persuade them not to do so. Again, some of the young ladies who walked the streetil of the town on the Sabbath might be induced to go to church, and by that means much good would be accomplished. (Applause.) —Mr. Griffiths said there was nothing like having plenty to do, and the more they did for the church the better they would become. Self-sacrifice was a very important part of religion. If they were to help one another—morally or otherwise—it could only be by some personal sacrifice.—The Pastor remarked that their service for God should be whole-hearted. They should work hard for the good of the church. In Messrs. Griffiths, Rowlands and Broadhurst and some of their lady members they had actors as well as suggesters. He thought Mr. Rowlands suggestion was a very good one and likely to be productive of much good if carried out. The young men and women who Jauntily walked the streets on Sundays only needed a medium to bring them to Christ.—During the evening an enjoyable programme was submitted by members of the choir. Quartettes were creditably sung and songs rendered by Mr. Spruce and Miss Bridge with good effect.—At the close ahearty vote of thanks was passed to the ladies, artistes, and all who had contributed to the evening's entertainment, on the proposition of Mr. Broadhurst, seconded by Mr. Cotton.--Mr. Rowlands was cordially thanked for pre- siding, and, in returning thanks, said he was always pleased to render what services he could on behalf of Legh-street Church.</p>
        <p>CURED, AND GRATEFUL.—The interesting statement, of a Liverpool woman concerning her remarkable cure printed at length in another column of this issue, si one of the latest examples of the curative powers of Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people. These pills are praised by all olasses of people for the way in which they have cured paralysis, koomotor ataxy, rheumatism, and sciatica ; and all diseases arising from impoverishment of the blood,scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas, consumption of the bowels and lungs, anmmia, pale and sallow complexion, general muscular weakness, loss of appetite, palpitations, pains in the back, nervous headache and neuralgia, early decay, all forms of female weakness and hysteria. These pills are a tonic, not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name. Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, and are sold by chemists, and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, Holborn Viaduct, London, E.C., at 25.9 d. per box, or six boxes for 13s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Shepherdry in Warrington The annual report and statement of accounts in connection with the Warrington district of the Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds has just been issued. It is signed by Joseph Williams, Prov. C.S., Joseph Adams, D.P. C.S., James Parr, district treasurer, and Peter Edgar, district secretary. Reviewing the work for the past tw lye months, they state that it is very gratifying and encouraging to them to notice that on the whole, steady progress has been made both financially and numerically. During the year, 103 members were admitted; suspended ' through non- payment 70;, died 14, leaving the number at the end of the year at 941; .being an increase of 21. The sick pay to members ;amounted to £602 15s. 9d. as against :£.566 2s. 2d. the previous twelve months. The funeral claims amounted to £250 being an increase of £6O on the 811 M paid under this heading in the year 1901. The total accumulated funds of the district and lodges nmr stands at £5,338 6s. 2d. as against £5,136 18s. 20. the year previous. The postponement of the Coronation celebrations were a bitter and sore trial to all. The nation stood aghast when at noon on that eventful day, the sad and sorrowful news was made known, and for many days they were in fear, doubt, and sorrow, but by the grace of God, the King even- tually recovered, and the ceremony took place on. Saturday, August 9th, 1902. The local demonstra- tion took place on that day in honour of that historical event. The Friendly Societies of the town took a most prominent and imposing part, and the shepherds of the district were complimented upon the grand turn out. The new regalia that was worn not only by the district 0111c3r5 but also by the different lodge officials, was much admired by the general pub- lic and brethren of kindred societies, and sincere thanks were due to all those who helped and took part in making the demonstration a success. The Royal Cycling Club.</p>
      </div>
