      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <p>WARRINGTON BUTCHERS' ANNUAL DINNER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Councillor Bennett and the Trade of Warrington.</head>
        <p>Councillor Bennett and the Trade of Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Blue Bell Hotel on Thursday last week, the annual dinner in connection with the Warrington Butchers' Association was held, and about 50 members and friends gathered round the festive board. The President (Mr. G. Bowcock) and the vice-president (Mr. R. T. Bridge) occupied the seats of honour and amongst the visiting butchers present were Councillor Rarasden, Mr. A. Bickerton, and Mr. J. Ellis (Liver- pool), Mr. J. Unsworth (St. Helens), Mr. R. Gatley and Mr. .1. Ernill (Altrincharn), and Mr. G. Unsworth (Wigan). Several local farmers and breeders were pre- sent as invited guests. An excellent repast was served by Mr. Thomas Cooper, after which the usual loyal toasts were proposed and received with musical honours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>TIIE TOWN AND TRADE OF WARRINGTON."</head>
        <p>TIIE TOWN AND TRADE OF WARRINGTON."</p>
        <p>Mr.Bowaocx, in proposing " The Town and Trade of Warrington "said the butchers were deeply interested in the trade of the town and it behoved them to be interested in its welfare and in its local government. (Hear, hear.) Considerable progress had been made within the last two years by the introduction of elec- tric light, tramways, technical schools and other insti- tutions; They welcomed these evidences of progress, the technical schools especially. (Hear, hear), They would be a great advantage and if the young people availed themselves of the opportunities which these schools provided, there was no doubt they would be of great use in after life. The carrying out of the new Education Act would cause an increase in the rates,but from what he could hear Warrington was behindhand in its educational facilities and they would have to follow the lead of other towns. (Hear, hear.) As butchers they had always got on very well with the local authori- ties. They had never had cause for any serious com- plaint. They were always prepared to meet the Council and the Medical Officer in a fair and impartial spirit. They had no desire to cloak or cover anything. They wanted everything fought in the open, and in an amicable and fair English spirit. (Hear, hear.) Councillor BENNETT, in responding, said the two inter- ests represented that evening—the butchers and the , farmers—were specially interested in the town and trade of Warrington. The farmers of Lancashire and Cheshire had a reputation for producing the best beef, and the butchers of Warrington knew how to buy the best quality, and Out it up and sell it to the best advantage. He could only say that his personal relations with the butchers of Warrington in his offizial connection with the Warrington and District Farmers Association Com- pany, had been of the- pleasantest kind possible, and he thought the good feeling which existed between both associations was something they might congratulate themselves upon, and which they hoped would long continue. (Hear, hear.) He was sorry to say that the trade in Warrington was not what they would like it to be and no doubt the butchers were a sort of thermometer as to the state of trade (Hear hear). He believed that the present con- ditition of trade in the town was due to some little ex- tent to the agitation. He.was not going to say one word as to the rights or wrongs of the agitation. He had expressed his feelings upon it in other places but whether the agitation was right or wrong he thought they could not close their eyes to the faet that the agitation must have some influence on outsiders. He wanted to see War- rington develop and increase its industries,but while it had got such a bad name and was under a cloud, out- siders could not be attracted by the reputation of Warrington or be inclined to come to establish themselves here. HQ:hoped that i a little while the cloud would pass away. Steps ought to be taken to bring the matter to an issue one W lay or another. (Hear, hear.) Referring to education, Councillor Bennett said it was very im- portant that we should not be behind any nation in our educational requirements. Germany and America had been taking oar place in the world's markets and from all he had been able to read and learn that was due to the fact that they were better educated. We did not like to admit that; we liked to think we were on the top of the tree, but undoubtedly the English race was behind other countries in educational matters. There must be a great levelling up, and although they would be called upon to pay an extra rate, they would.realise that they would get full value for it in due time. (Hear, hear.) Alluding to the water facilities of War- rington, Councillor Bennett said it was of the greatest importance that the navigation of the river should be maintained and something done to develop not merely a local traffic or a coasting traffic but ocean- ' going traffic with all the nations of the world. It was little less than a scandal that we should have a water- way like the Manchester Ship Canal gging past our boundaries without a dock or a decent slrrt of wharf. It was true we had a lay-by near the Stag Bridge, but he hoped the Council and the people of Warring- ton would insist that in the near future greater use should be made of this magnificent water-way and that the town should have the docks of which it was unfairly deprived when the Ship Canal was opened. (Hear, hear.) He was present at the meeting of shareholders of the Ship Canal Company this week and there was an atmosphere of confidence at the meet- ing which seemed to promise that in the not very distant future the Canal would be a. paying concern. As far as Manchester was con- cerned, it had done enormous good to Manchester. They did not expect to rival Manchester but he objected to being left behind. (Hear, hear). War- rington had had few advantages and he did not think the Corporation was doing its duty, and he did not think they as citizens were doing their duty unless they used the facilities which they possessed. (Hear, hear). Adverting to the rates of the town, Councillor Bennett observed that they compared very favourably indeed with those of other towns, especially when they con- sidered the enormous expense of transforming an old town into a modern one. The reproductive enter- prises• of the Corporation, such as gas, water electricity and tramways were 'doing splendidly. He was. informed that the tramways would not only pay in- terest and sinking fund but would also have a balance to be used towards the reduction of the rates. By repay- ing their capital year by year they were gradually re- ducing the debt on these undertakings, and in course of i time there would be more profit to go in relief of the , rates. He found that the profits of the water works : were somethiug like £lO,OOO a year but this large sum I was absorbed in repaying the loans and the interest. He believed that in the course of time the sums handed over in relief of rates from these enterprises would be very considerable, and their rates would be much lower in consequence. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>"NATIONAL FEDERATION."</head>
        <p>"NATIONAL FEDERATION."</p>
        <p>IMr. H. SINGLETON, in proposing the toast of the " National Federation," alluded to the question of tuberculosis, and stated that it was time a line was drawn so as to prevent any unfair treatment of butchers. in the seizure of cattle. (Hear hear). Medical Officers had a perfect right to their own opinions ,but butchers did not wont persecution (Hear hear). He had seen a beast that was seized in Warrington and there was not the slightest sign of tuberculosis on its body. The in- surance scheme which had been started to recompense butchers to some extent for animals seized had been , of great use, but he believed the knowledge that butchers had an insurance scheme, made officers of the Corporation more liable to seize cattle. There had been so many seizures lately that the butchers had been called upon to pay double fees for insurance. He hoped steps would he taken by the National Federation to get the matter, settled by Parliament at an early date. (Hear, hear.)--Councillor Ramsden (Liverpool) replied. Other toasts were also ,honoured, but lack of space prevents our reporting the speeches. Mr. J. Unsworth proposed' Kindred Associations "and Messrs. Unsworth (St. Helens) and Gatley (Altrincham) responded. Mr. R. C. Shawproposed the toast of' Success to the War- rington Butchers Associations," and the President res- ponded. The toast of "The visitors" was proposed by Mr. A. Mynekyme, and responded to by Mr. Joseph Knowles (Fanners'Auction Company), Mr. Arthur J. Titmes (representing Mr. J. Mainwaring), Mr. R. Tickle, Mr Shearer and Mr. Whitlow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Warrington Elective Auditors.</head>
        <p>Warrington Elective Auditors.</p>
        <p>There will be no contest for the two elective audi- torships of Warrington, Mr. John Harrison, who has been an elective auditor for about twenty years, having decided not to seek re-election. Consequently Messrs Edwin Bradshaw and Laurence Bolton are returned unopposed. Had there been a contested election Mr. Bolton would have had the support of the Municipai Non-Trading Committee.</p>
