      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>LOCAL OBITUARY, Mr. John Houghton.</head>
        <p>LOCAL OBITUARY, Mr. John Houghton.</p>
        <p>Another highly respected townsman. has passed away in the person of Mr. John Houghton, of Wood- bine Cottage, Sankey, whose death occurred at his residence on Saturday afternoon. The deceased gentle- man, who was 70 years of age, contracted a cold about three weeks ago, and pneumonia supervening death ensued. The late Mr. Houghton was brother to Mr. W. D. Houghton, the proprietor of Sankey Wire Mills, and had occupied the position of manager of the works since the formation of the firm in the eighties. He was held in high esteem by the em- ployes, and his death has occasioned deep regret. The deceased gentleman was prominently identified with Cairo-street Unitarian Chapel and took a great, interest in its work. He leaves four sons and a daugh- ter with whom much sympathy is expressed in their bereavement. __ _ _</p>
        <p>Testimony to the deep respect in which the deceased was held was forthcoming at the funeral which took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Tuesday afternoon. Although miserable weather prevailed, rain, falling heavily, a large number of townspeople assembled to witness the last rites,and no fewer than 250 of the employes at Sankey Wire Mills headed the procession. The coffin was covered with beautiful floral tributes, and Many wreaths were placed on the top of the hearse. The first part of the burial service was held in Cairo-street Chapel, the Rev. E. W. Lummis officiating and delivering an impressive address in which be referred to the de- ceased's many good qualities. Mr. Houghton,he said, had been a member of the congregation for a long period. In the Kingdom of Heaven that was to be there would be only one King and they would be sub- jects. The best preparation was humble and simple service for God whiLst .here. Their deceased friend was a good master, a good servant, a good husband, and a good father, and in all relations of society he was an upright, kindly, loyal, and good man. The hymns "For ever with the Lord," . ," Lead, kindly light," and "Nearer my God to Thee" were feelingly sung by therarge congregation. The service at the graveside was conducted by the Rev. E. W. Lunimis. The principal mourners were. the deceased's daughter andllus four sons, Messrs. John William, Percy and i Herbert, and his grandson, Arthur Houghton; Mr. and Mrs. W. D. Houghton, Colwyn Bay; Messrs. John I and Frank Houghton, Appleton; Mr, flarrY Houghton, Warrington ; Mr. Ernest Houghton, Moore; also I Messrs. James Houghton, P. Grange,' Robert Wright, !Runcorn ; R, Dixon. J. Hill, J. Simonette, J. Taylor, E. Graham, J. Graham, W. Bramall, J. Forster, J. Boardman, A. Pemberton, W. Light; P. Jackson, W. ! Bingham and J. Walker. The following employes at the Wire Mills acted as bearers :--Messrs. J. P. Taylor. John Forster, ,T. Clarke; W. Light, J. Gregory, John Graham, J. Dickenson, A.Pemberton, P. Pownall, ud P. Jackson. ; The procession of workmen was under the direction ; of Messrs. Joseph 'Massey, R. Siddall, and J. Foden. The Cairo-street congregation and Sunday School ! were represented by Messrs.' W. H. Woodcock, J. S. ; Hill, T. C. Hollingworth, and J. W. Davies ' (secretary.) Floral tributes were forwarded by the following.: --- Congregation of Cairo-street Chapel ;"the teachers and officers of Cairo-street Sunday School ; • the members of the Younc, Ladies' Qlass, Cairo-street Sunday School, Mr. and Mrs. Percy Grange ;In loving mem- ory of Grandpa, from "Kathleen"; Mr. and Mrs. Sydney White,. London; " John " " Polly " and " Frank" "His loving wife and family " ; Mr. and Mrs. W. Hatton and family; Mr. and Mrs. W. D. Houghton, Colwyn Bay; the Rev. F. K. and Mrs. F. Freeston, London ; Mr. and Mrs. Forsha w; Mr. and Mrs. A. Harris and family; Mr. and Mrs. Wilcox and family; Mr. and Mrs. F. W. Monks; Mr. and Mrs. John C. Young; Mr. and Mrs. H. Houghton, Mr. and Mrs. Joseph Smith, Mr. and Mrs. J. S. Hill, the Office Staff, Mr. and Mrs. J. F. Taylor. from all at the Firs;•Moore, In loving memory of dear grand- father from his grand-children. Mrs. Pemberton and family, Mr. and Mrs. C. A; Pemberton, Mr. and E. Williams, John Melbourne, Limited, Mrs. William Houghton and family, Penketh ; employes, Sankey Wire Mills.</p>
        <p>The coffin was of oak with handsome brass mount- ings, and the plate bore the following inscription 'John Houghton, died February 21st, 1903, aged 70 years." Mr. Edward Connor had charge of the funeral arrangements which were satisfactorily carried out under the personal supervision of Mr William Connor. Mr. Edward B. Bowser.</p>
        <p>Probably many Warringtonians will have a re- collection of Mr. Edwilrd Brown Bowser, who was the •postmaster at Warrington from 1862 to 1882, and who died at his residence.at York- on Friday last. Mr. Bowser was a native of York and came to Warrington from that city to take up the position which he effi- ciently occupied for twenty years. During his residence in Warrington the deceased gentleman was prominently identified with the Wesleyan body, and held offices, including that of a circuit steward'. He was held in deep respect by all who came into contact with him. On leaving Warrington Mr. Bowser took up the post- mastership of Taunton, and ten years later went to live in retirement at York. He was 84 years :of age. The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Tuesday afternoon, the Rey. G. H. Carnson, superintendent of the Wesleyan Circuit, officiating. The mourners were : --Dr. Bowser (nephew), Penrith, Mr. J. Boggis (son-in-law), and Messrs' E. H. and Frank Boggis (grandsons), Wigan ; l‘tr. T. Hunter (brother-in-law), York; Mr. T. B. Hunter (nephew), Leeds. The ftmeral was also attended by Mr. George Weaver, steward of the Warrington Wes- leyan Circuit; Mr. A. H. Dunn, circuit trustees' sec- retary ; Mr. J. W. Hayward, trustee of the circuit.; Mr. Gorton, superintendent of the telegraph depart- ment, Warrington Post Office; Mr. Mellor, inspector of letter carriers ; Mr. Banks, assistant superintendent in the postal department; Mr. Dannett, late postmaster of Wigan, and formerly deceased's chief clerk ,at War- rington; and Mr. W. Bennett, postmaster at Runcorn. Mr. E. Connor had charge of the funeral arrange- ments at Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>Mr. James Muir.</head>
        <p>Mr. James Muir.</p>
        <p>The death occured somewhat suddenly on Monday evening of Mr. James Muir, a former member of the Warrington Town Council. He had been in declining health for some time but this did not keep him indoors and he was seen taking a walk last week. He died at the residence of his son, Mr. J. M. Muir at 184, Wilderspool-road, at the age of seventy six years. Mr. Muir came to Warrington from Dumfries and had resided here • for the greater part of his life. For many years he was traveller for the firm of G. Arting- stall and Company and served the firm with ability and success. -He was a Conservative in politics and re- presented St. Austin's Ward on the Town Council. For several years he was also a member of the Board of Guardians. Latterly he had lived in retirement and devoted some of his declining years to literary work. Several poems and articles from his pen have appeared in the" Examiner."</p>
        <p>A 14 Ton Load of Seeds. Farmers attending the Warrington Corn Mark et on Wednesday, were much interested in an extraordinary load of seeds, weighing in all 14 tons, which was passing through Horsem4rket-street, from Messrs. Gartons Seed Warehouse, to the Central Station. These seeds were being despatched by Messrs. Gar- tons to customers in various parts of the country, and the fact of one lurrey being able to take such a large consignment caused mere than unusual interest in the same. This particular vehicle has been made in War- rington for the Great Northern Railway Company, by Mr. R. Spann, . Waggon Builder, Friars .E.4:reen. Following this on a smaller conveyance was a further consignment of Messrs. Gartons seeds, whieh they are sending out to the Minister of Agriculture of the Victorian aovermnent, Melbourne, Australia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</head>
        <p>SEAWEED AS A MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>ITS MARVELLOUS EFFECT UPON THE</p>
        <p>STOMACH, LIVER, KIDNEYS AND BOWELS. SEAWEED possesses a natural strengthening, healing and purifying power, far greater than other known remedies. This is now conceded by important authori- ties. It was first introduced by Mr. Veno. and Veno's Seaweed Tonic is the only remedy in the world contain- ing seaweed, for that reason Veno's Seaweed Tonic is used in hospitals and by doctors themselves because of its superior curative properties. Its most brilliant effect is produced in stomach, liver and kidney diseases, and the extraordinary cures ill performs, even in the worst cases, is positive proof of its efficacy. It is a god-send to sufferers front indigestion, wind, headache, general weakness, kidney trouble, weak and painful back, torpid liver, female troubles, poorness of blood, and HABITUAL CONITMATION. Ask for VENO'S SEA- WEED TONIC. Prices is. lid. and 2s. 9d. at chemists and medicine vendors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>Examiner, Friday, February 27, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Friday, February 27, 1903.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON IRON- WORKERS.</p>
        <p>MEETING OF TRADES' UNIONISTS.</p>
        <p>Speeches by Mr. Alderman Monks, Mr. A. H. Crosfield and Mr. Parker.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington branch of the Asso- ciated Iron and Steel Workers of Great Britain, to the number of 400, attended the tenth annual tea and meet- ing in the Thewlis-street schoolroom on Saturday even ing. Tea was served at 4.30 and the catering of Coun- cillor James Evans gave entire satisfaction. The sub- sequent proceedings were presided over by Mr. Joseph Elwell (president of the Warrington Branch), and he was accompanied on the platform by Mr. Aucott, (secretary of the Midland Wages Board and president of the Associated Iron and Steel Workers), Mr. Alderman Monks, Mr.. A. H. Crosfield Mr. C. E. Parker, Mr. Coun- cillor Tinnion, Mr. Councillor Evans, Captain Crosfield, Mr. Rudge, Mr. Callaghan (Stalybridge Branch). THE CHAIRMAN AND TRADES UNIONISM. The CHAIRMAN said it was a source of regret to them that Mr. Cox, the general secretary of their Society, was unable to be present through illness. They had been looking forward with interest to his visit and to the report which he was to present to them on his recent visit to America. (Hear, bear.) He (the Chairman) thought that the bad trade daring the last two or three years and the great difficulty they had experienced in making their expenditure fit in with their income had helped them to realise the necessity of being members of their trades organisations so that they might obtain the best results for their labours. (Hear. hear.) They were all pleased to see their old president, Mr. Aucott. (Hear, hear.) He had repre- sented them for a good many years and they trusted he might be spared for many years to continue his help to the society. in his hands their interests were safe. There were some people who had the impression that trades unions were formed for the purpose of creating disturbances between capital land labour. Such was not the case. The interests of capital and labour were identical. Although they were trades unionists they were not revolutionists. They believed that the best method of settling disputes was by conciliation and arbitration. In the iron and steel trade they had had more ex- perience of conciliation and arbitration than any other trade and they believed they had gained more by these methods than by taking extreme action. Strikes brought in their train misery and suffering and only aggit.vated the issue. Whatever difficulties they had had in their trade had been amicably settled by the conciliationand arbitration board. The Chairman proceeded to state that he had received a communication from Mr. A. H. Crosfield asking that he might be their host instead of their guest on the next occasion. On a vote being taken it was unanimously decided to accept Mr. Crosfield's kind invitation.</p>
        <p>GOOD ADVICE FROM THE PRESIDENT. Mr. Aticorr, who had a hearty reception, then addressed the meeting. Referring to the recent visit of :Nlr. Cox to America, he said one result was to assure them that in the iron trade they had nothing to fear from the Americans and nothing to learn from them, nor was there anything in which they could damage us. But in regard to the sbael trade there was another tale to tell and he would let Mr. Lox tell them the story on some futureoccasion. America.was undoubtedly a coun- try of great resources but he felt that' the greatest, enemy of the British working man was the growing dis- sipation through drink. (Hear, hear). If they would takeeome homely advice he wouldsay "Don't drink be- tween meals and don't have any then." (Laughter). There was a time when it was customary to remark of a bride when she had married a puddler. "Oh, she is all right," but to-day when it was known that a woman had married a puddler, the usual expression was "God help her! " (Loud laughter). Let the British nation be a sober nation and he did not care for the competition of the world. (Hear, hear). In referring to the results obtained by means of their trades union, he said they had been able to obtain better prices by means of the Wages Board which regulated prices. He believed the relationships between the employers and employed in the iron and steel trades were clos3r than any which ex- isted in other trades. Both sides had learned the ad- vantage of conciliation. They now looked to the general good of the trade and according to the state of the market they regulated the tonnage rates. Both employers a.id employed found that they could not injure the other side withoet feeling the re-action. He advised the workers when prices were good to inculcate the habit of putting a portion in the Post Office Savings Bank to enable them to meet emergencies when times were bad. He had never any trouble with the workman who had an account in the Post Office Savings Bank. (Hear, hear.) The difficulty arose with those who were con- tinually spending more than their income. (Hear, hear).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>RESOLUTION.</head>
        <p>THE RESOLUTION.</p>
        <p>. Mr. CALLAGHAN proposed the following resolution: —" That this meeting of the Ironworkers Association does hereby urges upon all ironworkers the importance of becoming members of the Union.lts aims and objects are well-known, but the preservation of the rights the workingman is supreme. Believing that the working- men hold the potential instruments of production and of independence in their own hands, it urges all workmen to take an intelligent interest in political, social and civic affairs, and to wield the power already in their hands for the amelioration, not only of their own con- dition, but of the less fortunate section of the com- munity. It also earnestly hopes that the conflict between capital and labour may soon cease, and that the day may soon come when there shall be a fusion and amalgamation of interest common to all."—The speaker said the chief difficulty experienced by trades unionists was to bring workingmen to see the necessity of com- bination.</p>
        <p>Mr. BUDGE seconded the resolution and minted out that a healthy sign in connection with the Warrington branch was the prompt pAyment of subscriptions at a time of bad trade.</p>
        <p>MR. PARKER AND THE INVENTIVE GENIUS</p>
        <p>OF WORKINGMEN.</p>
        <p>! Mr. C. E. PARKER supported the resolution, and ! observed that he did not think he had been to a meeting I which had so much interested him as the present one. IHe had been much impressed for years with the fact that so far as British industry was concerned, although Iwe were holding our own in many respects, the industry was menaced in a way which it had not been in former years. He was convinced that unless the manufacturers secured the co-operation of the brain power and the inventive genius of the workingmen of England, it would be very difficult for them to hold their own. (Hear, hear.) At the annual meeting of the Warrington Chamber of Commerce he touched upon a question which found an illustration in America in the operation of the American Automatic Gash Register Co. Suggestion Boxes " were placed in each de- partment, and the operatives, numbering 3,000, were in- vited to put in s.iggestions for the improvement of any process, machine, or method. As an Incentive and en- couragement prize money amounting to £2BO a year was distributed amongst the one hundred employes who made the best suggestions. The amount of the prize money. was allocated es far as pos- sible in proportion to the value of the sug- gestion. In one year 4,000 suggestions were made,l and it was found practicable to .adopt 2,000 of these. ! One improvement made by an employs was a device to do away with the necessity of the work he himself was doing. (Laughter.) In regard to the help that work- men could give the manufacturers, they had, as it were, but scratched the surface, and there were down below I in the brains of the workmen ideas, thoughts and in- ventions which, if turned in the right direction, and brought to play upon British industry which was handi- capped in many ways, would cause it to advance by leaps and bounds. The spirit in which trades unions were working was in the right direction, and the more they could turn the attention of the unions to the prime question of making proht, it was an after question about the divi- sion. (Applause). There would always be differences of opinion; there might be conflict,but they could con- tend as friends. Let them turn their attention as masters and workmen to the principles and conditions which made the profit, and afterwards they would gather together and try to settle the division. He had always contended that against the power of capital and amal- gamation, isolated bodies of workmen had no chance, and not only was that so from the workman's point of view, but he was in a weaker position. As mastem,they were far more likely to come to an amicable and satis- factory arrangement with a representative body of men the best men coming to the front as representing the whole of the workmen, and settling any differences that arose. Therefore he was in hearty agreement with the resolution. He was indifferent under what political flag he sailed or worked so long as they could, by work- ing together, help in the elevation of the condition of he workers. (Applause). IMPORTANT SPEECH BY MR. MONKS. PROPOSED SCHOLARSHIPS.</p>
        <p>Alderman Moncs, who had a hearty reception, said he rose with great pleasure to support the resolution. No reasonable man could for a moment be in disagreement with the terms and objects which the resolution sub- mitted. Like many other things, perhaps the most im- portant part of it was in the last paragraph, which earnestly hoped that the conflict between capital and labour might soon cease. Tn the past there had been a good deal of conflict between capital and labour on many points. No one had regretted it more than he had done; but he felt sure he saw signs, notlronly amongst the workmen but amongst leaders and capitalists, that they had come to look at it as a huge blunder and as something very much to be regretted. As far as he was personally concerned he should always try to bring capital and labour closer and closer together. (Applause). They had seen over and over again what -ruin had been brought to both sides as the result of fric- tion, and they were all coming to see it might be self- interest, but self-interest of the lowest kind. But they were all coming to see that there was more money, more comfort, and more satisfaction for all of them by meet- ing together, and calmly discussing these various points, and thus coming to a mutual satisfactory arrangement. (Hear, hear.) He was waiting for some suggestion to be made which would enable the working man to have '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <p>a larger proportion of the profits and of the results of his industry. (Hear, hear.) There was •no doubt the capitalists had had the best end of the stick,and it was only fair as things were going that working men should get more out of their industry than they 1117 d got in the past. (Applause.) He for one welcomed such a change in the condition of things, and he would only add in regard to that matter one . word more, namely, that when.they got that larger share of- the results of their industry they shonld take care of it. (Hear, hear.) He could assure them that he lead never listened ton speech with greater pleasure than that which had been , delivered by Mr. Aucott who had put matters beforel them better than he (Alderman Monks) ever hoped to , he able. He asked all Present to rethember what Mr. j Aucott had said to them, and not only to remember but to act upon it. If they would only take better care of the money they earned and received they would have I more self-respect, make better workmen, and not only help the manufacturers but themselves more thananyone else. He spoke upon that subject rather feelingly, and he would illustrate it by referring to what happened in their own works within the last three or four years. All in the iron trade knew that in 1899 and 1900 theywere suffering from having too much good trade if they could suffer at all in that respect. Prices ran away and got beyond control, and the result was they had been going through a period of depression which had been hard for all of them. Neither • side could suffe: without the .other suffering; If working men ' I suffered employers • suffered as much in a similar way, and working men could help far more than they realize:4 if they would follow the precepts which had been • given by Mr. Aucott. . They had at the time he had named their books full of orders which would take them six or seven months to get through. Their total production went down to the extent of 25 per cent. and where they used to get out 1,000 tons a week they could not get out more than 750 or 800 tons. That showed how they suffered together. IThe men would take a holiday. They were getting 371 per cent. more in their wages, but they would not put it in the bank, and provide for a rainy day. Theywordd only work four or five days a week instead of six. No one enjoyed a holiday more than he (the speaker) did, and he should be glad to think that every man in. their employ was able to take it when he wanted one. If it •was merely a question of taking a holiday when he wanted one and not interfering with the work,Mr. Burton would not have so much to say to them as he occasionally had. (Laughter.) The mistake they made was that they did not work full time whenthey could take the benefit of the extra work and wages: It would be better to work for the extra money, and to put it in the Post Otlice Savings Bank. (Applause.) He had.always found that it was much easier to get along with a man who had something at stake in the shape of a bank balance. He knew from experience the advantage of having something in the bank available against a rainy day. He was • glad to see how strew*' the ironworkers ,union was, and he hoped it would go on and prosper. (Hear, hear.) He was not afraid of unions in anyway. On the contrary he welcomed their advance becaase he recognised that through the union and the leaders they could do more to help the general trade of the country against the rest of the country than any- thing else. In the Midland Wages Board they had a striking example of how successful a conciliation board could be made, and what advantage could accrue as the result of its efforts to the trade as a whole. He did not think that any other Board had been established in the country which had been of more service and advantage to thetrade than the Midland Conciliation Board. He had the highest opinion of the Board, and of the presi- dent and secretary, and if all conciliation boards were managed by such men as Mr. Aucott they would do more good than had ever been done in the past. (Applause). Therefore they could easily believe he-was anxious to see the Board strengthened in every way, and if he could do anything to help them or the members to strengthen the Board he should be pleased. (Hear hear.) They had heard a good deal these last few years about foreign competition from America and Germany, and he was afraid they would hear more about it in the future, and would feel it a great deal more. But per- sonally he was not in the least afraid if everyone in the trade would give the matter careful consideration, and if the masters and men could join unitedly in one common cause against- the foreigner. (Applause). The men could do a great deal, but the masters in some res- pects could do a great deal more. He thought the mas- ters had been largely to. blame in the past for not having adopted the newest methods, in not hiving given more thought and consideration in their business, and spent more time on it and done more than they had done in the way of reorganization and recon- struction so as to reduce the cost of manufacture, and in that way to put . trade on a sounder basis than it had ever been on before. He had spent some time in going through ContinentaHron and steel works, and he must admit that they were better managed as far as the iron and steel works were concerned, better arranged, better organized than their English works were. That reflected to smile extent on the proprietors and mana- gers of the English works, but he could not help it. He must speak of what he saw. (Hear, hear.) The Ger- mans especially could beat us hollow in the organiza- tion and re-arrangement of their works, and it was for the masters to wake up and show that they had the grit which their fathers and grandfathers had when the iron trade was established. A great deal could be done in the way of education, and that brought him to a subject he had given great consideration to. He would urge them to keep their children at school as long as they could, and not only, at school but to insist within reason upon them attending the technical school and various evening classes. He had worked very herd to get these classes well established, feeling that by so doing he was doing as much or more than anything else to help the workine classes of this town to keep this country to the front with all the rest of the world. (Hear, hear.) They had only just entered upon the work. Tho new Education Act would give the Council and the Education Committee a chance they had never had before. He hoped the committee would rise to the opportunities which the passing of the Act afforded them. Education in IVerrington now would all be under the control.of one committee, and they could put it in the hands of the parents and children to an extent which hid never been done before, and so arrange it that every child, given the neeessary intellect, would be able to get as good an education as the son of the richest man in the country. (Applause.) He as not only anxious to arrange classes and a series of classes but a series of scholarships so that the child of it workingman could pass through the elementary schools and go (mint() the higher grade or Grammar School without paving any fees. (Applause). lie hoped the employers would assist in forwarding these scholar- ships and not only pay for the education but the pre- miums of the persons to learn their own trade at their respective works. Messrs. Crosfleld had shown the wee- in regard to the metter,and theyhad come forevard very handsomely to assist tlee sons of men who were attend- ing their own worl by offering prizes and scholarships and paying the fees. (Applause). That was good as far as it went and it was an excellent start, but under the care of the new Director of Education they were en- deavouring to arrange it series of acholarships such 'as Ihe kid outlined which would enable any youth or girl Ito improve his or her education. He was afraid that girls had been loft too much in the background. It was just as necessary to educate girls as boys. (Hear, hear). He had done a little in the direction of assist- ance by offering a scholarship for girls. It had been taken advantage of, but-much more conld be done, and I he had no doubt that Messrs. Crosfleld would be willing to fell into line with the suggestion of the committee. I His own firm would do the same, and he hoped otheks • would join in making the question of education a great success. He knew the committee were very anxious to carry it out and he trusted manufacturers would co- operate by taking students without a premium or by I providing a scholarship for them. The scheme would not be dropped without a herd straggle to bring it about. (Hear, hear). The CHAIRMAN, in alluding to the technical classes for iron and steel and metallurgy, called attention to the fact that many ironworkers were prevented from sitting at an examination owing to the nature of their work which compelled them to work nights one week and days another. He thought some arrangement should be made to meet this difficulty.</p>
        <p>SPEECH BY MR. A. H. CROSFIELD. HIS INTEREST IN TRADES UNIONS.</p>
        <p>THE AMERICANS' FEVERISH DOLLAR-GETTING EXCITEMENT. PROBABLE VISIT OF MR. JOHN MORLEY".</p>
        <p>Mr. A. FL CROSPIELD, who had a flatt3ring reception said he felt somewhat ata disadvantage in following the two last speakers, and he thought the simplest thing would be for him to say that he endorsel everything they had heard—(Laughter)— if they would let him off as easily as that. (Laughter.) , There were, however, one or two things he would like to say even though ho could not speak with the inti- mate knowledge which Mr. Monks had just displayed in his deeply interesting and deeply instructing speech in reference to the relations between capital and labour. Coining from so able an iron master he (Mr. Croslield) ventured to think it was one of the most important speeches delivered in Warrington for many a long day. He hoped they would accept his very cordial thanks for the kind invitation which the Chairman gave him to he present that evening. A hint had been given to him (the speaker) that it would be as well if some dis- tinguised man was invited to address them at a special , meeting to ba held for the purpose. , He had written to Mr. Herbert Gladstone and to Sir John Brunner asking them to approach Mr. John Morley and persuade him to pay them a visit to Warrington, and he (Mr. Cros- field) had received letters from them promising to do so. (Applause.) Tt had also been suggested that they should endeavour to. persuade Sir Edward Grey to visit them: (Renewed applause.) He was sure they would be glad to hear Mr. Morley, and he promised them that he would do all in his power to induce him to come. Referring to the speech of Mr. Aucott, the speaker said he did not think there were many men in the country he would sooner listen to than to Mr. Aucott, as there was a personality and a shrewd humour about hint that must endear him to the hearts of all of them ; to employers as well as employed, and all he , had said about trades unionism and what Mr. Monks and Mr. Parker had said had been put before the meet- ing better than he (Mr. Crosfield) could put it. He must apologise for not being in quite top fighting or, if they liked, stumping form—the same thing. (Laughter). He would not like them'to think that the little bout which Mr. Parker and hehad had with a cortainpolitician who</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <p>had recently come to Warrington had taken his wind. (Laughter). No, it was just an attack of the " flu " and neuralgia with it which had rather pulled him down. (Laughter). He was very sorry to see that a short time since his friend Mr. Pierpoint had also been "under the weather." He (Mr. Crosfield) had shared Mr. Pierpoint's bad fortune, but ha regretted to 'tell them he had not shared his great good fortune. (Laughter and applause). He hoped they would not think the re- ference Out of place. (Hear, hear). He noticed the representatives of the Press were there, those friends who were so kind to them, ignoring little slips and repairing loose phrases, i putting n fact, those finishing touches without which sonic of their finest perorations might scarcely parse. (Laughter.) They were all very grateful for this, and as gratitude was a sense of further favours to come, perhaps he might be allowed to take the opportunity to send a message through the Press to Mr. Pierpoint, message of .cordial and sincere congratulation, with their best wishes for health and happiness in the future. (Applause.) Mr. Crosfield said he thought it will always well to bear in mind that by maintaining a proper standard of wages trades unionism gave a generous and fair minded employer a chance, for the simple reason that jt • prevented him being undersold by someone who simply wanted' to play the sweater. (Hear, hear, and applause.) He was the other day reading a very important speech delivered by a competitor, and at the same time a friend of his who was once in business in Warrington, and if the meeting would allow him, he would read an , extract from the speech :—" The Trades Unions Par- liamentary Committees have striven for good govern- ment to protect their members from injustice; they have agitated in Parliament for improved cenditions of labour ; they have agitated for reforms of adminis- tration of justice ; for the appointment of factory inspectors ; for the, improvement of patent laws--a most important matter ; they have agitated for certifi- cates of competency for engine drivers ;and for various improvements of the. Friendly Societies Acts. These ' are the lines along which, J venture to say, trades unions can best gain their object. Trades unions are absolutely necessary. There must be combination of men ; but don't let us mistake either what a combina- tion.of employs can do for employs. : If we take the right view of this we shall see that. any attempt at restriction of output is only another way of reducing the fund out of whick wages are paid and can only have one effect and that is to reduce wages. I venture to say that trades unions might take just one little lesson from-their predecessors,the guilds of the 15th century, and whilst determined as these guilds were in .protecting their members in the main- tenance of the standard wage that they should accompany that by an equally strong m4istence upon a maximum of efficiency in their members. By doing so the fund available for wages would be again increased." (Ap- plause.) It struck him (Mr. Crosfield) that that was a very good summary of what he had to say, and most instructive were the remarks made in the pamphlet trom which he .(Mr. Crosfield) had just quoted. Reference was made in it to short hours and to the excellent wages paid to the working men of this country in the "golden age of labour," dating as far back as the 15th century, and to the remarkably high standard of work that was produced in those days. He thought it must be in their interest in the lons run not to insist upon the feverish activity displayed in a good riviny occupations in America, not in all, but still be thbught Mr. Cox when he visited them on a future occa- sion, would tell them that there was a great. deal of feverish activity on the other side •icif the water, and it was to a great extent to be ascribed to the climate. As a writer had put it the American climate was a very potent drug to be administered to 80,000,000 of people. Sometimes we, on this side, might have gone a little bit slow the other way, and there might be a happy mean to be hit. At any rate money was not everything in this life, and they did not want to see the same feverish dollar-getting excitement here which was seen in America, and which, they told him, cleared off every member of the. New York Stock Exchange by the time he was 40 years of age. They did not want to run to that extreme, but he thought one of the objects of trades unionism should be to maintain that eliciency of labour which, while increasing 'the national dividend and product of labour; was bound in the long run to bring about an improvement in their material and financial position. He most sincerely and kindly en- dotted whathad been said in support of the resolution. The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>; COUNCILLOR TTNNION AND THE DUTY OF WORKINGMEN. ! Councillor Taxattox thanked Mr. Elwell for his kind. invitation to be present at that meeting and proceeded to state that he always had been; and he hoped always should be, a man .who 'believed in trustinc, the. people ' and working men, and he was pleased to find that as far as the iron and Steel Workers' Association was con- cerned the.employers set an example whichwas worthy. to be followed by all employers and workmen through- out the length and breadth of the land. He had been asked to propose a vote of thanks to the Chairman. It had been his (Mr. Manion's) pleasure to be associated with Mr. Elwell in several matters and he had always found him to be • a straightforward, hard-headed, commonsense man, and he being the president was a fair sample of th 3 bulk of the members of the association. (Applause.) •He (Councillor Tinnion) had 'felt for a' long time that the working men of Warrington did not take that keen interest in local affairs they should take. He did not intend to refer particularly to what had been and was still a burning question, although he was prepared at any .time to defend the position he had taken up. There were many things that had suffered in consequence of workingmen not doing their duty. They should insist upon the committees which had the charge of the sanitary arrangements of the town doing every- thing they could to make their surroundings as healthy and as comfortable as they could. They should also ; insist upon having playgrounds and healthy homes. It ; was not right that any man who had to work as hard as i those he saw before him should have to live i4s a hovel, Iwith the result that they were often driven to go else- , where. The men present, however, could take care df themselves, and it was for Ihem to insist that the con- ditions to which he had refbrredshould be altered. He ; felt sure that if they always had such leaders, as those who had spoken that night,and copied their example,they ; would take a keener interest in all matters relating to . their interests. .He hoped they would also take into consideration the future education of their children, as in the immediate future they would have a better opportunity of doing so than had been possible in the ' past. It was no credit to the town that they had not ; had intermediate and secondary schools, such as were Ito be found in the neighbouring town of Widnes. He hoped they would see that the power really lay with the . people, and he hoped those present would do what they I could to improve the conditions that obtained in War- , • rtne,t on. (Applause.) ICaptain 0. IL GROSFIELD said he rose with very great, pleasure to second the vote of thanks. He had only known the Chairman for a short time, but he had ; had one or two most interesting conversations with I him about trades unionism and other matters which had bean most instructive and he hoped to have many mere. I (Hear, hear) He was quite sure that trades unions wilich had such men as the Chairman at the head would do nathing but what was of benefit to the community at large. (Applause) The motion was heartily agreed to. Daring the evening, the Warringtan Male Voice Choir, under the conductorship of Ntr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Rte., sang several seleetions in an admirable manner. Resnonding to a recall for their Rae interpre- tation of " Crossing the Plains," the choir further pleased the audience by sineing "In absence." Later in the evening they sang The Orphans." Mr. 1-las mall ga.-e it sweet rendering of " M try," Mr. Asnip sang The Saldier's Grave " and \lr. Gorigh's robust voice was heard to advantage in "Tiv:. Young Royalist." Mr. i F. Atkinson accompanied on the piano.</p>
        <p>Personal. Mr. Hedley Fitton lia,; been elected an associate of tlu. Royal Society of Painter-etchers and Engravers A GOOD RESOLCTION.—Wear ALP TAYLOR'S Wonderful Boots.—Advt. Poft Cr. tss An CHINA of every description try</p>
        <p>J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Tronnionger, si, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Northern. Congregational School. Silcoates Hall, Wakefield.</p>
        <p>In ay. Christjnas Cambridge Examinations. the results of which are published this week .4.1 boys have been successful, a number representing 60 per cent of the entrre. sehool. Of these successes 5 are in the senior, 17 in the junior, and 25 in the preliminary examination. Thirteen have been placed inshonour divisions, and 15 marks of distinction in special sub- jects have been awarded. The numerous friends in Warrington.of the Principal, Mr. J. A. Yonge will be interested imaearning these results.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. FIRE GRATES of every description.—T. S. GAR- . LAND, Furnishing ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warringion.—Advt. St. Mary's (Latchford) Ball. In connection with the., above mission the annual ball was given on Mondiy evening in the Parr Hall with every possible success. Beautiful music was discoursed by Mr. Ilandall's band and the large body of dancers was ably controlled by the 11.1.1.'s Messrs. J. W. Bibby, Delaney and Wells. ;1'11(1[411g by the bright happy faces and the general air of decorous gaiety we should say this dance is one of the most popular given in the town and • will doubtle-;,-; receive continued support in future years. The arrangements had been admirably carried out by the committee, Mr. J. T. Bibb3- being deserving of special praise for the able manner in which be discharge 'l the duties of hon. secretary.</p>
