      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1903-02-27_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. By "</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. By "</p>
        <p>Pancakes and points on Tuesday-71,w° of tha latter and as many of the former as the capacity o; the 'in- dividual dictated. This is a short history of the 24th day of the current month. Warrington. had a football match, and the day being Shrove Tuesday most of the inhabitants had pancakes: The visitors were St. Helens, the proud conquerors of Warrington in the first round or the cup, the engagement this time being in the Laague tournament. Warrington put" paid ." to the account and rose slightly in the estimation of their follower as well as in the League table.</p>
        <p>• One could hardly expect a 'big crowd for this match. The day was not .a general holiday, the attraction .of the fixture was not great; and the supporters of the club were in a pessimistic mood. Moreover it rained ! It generally does —Dr freezes —when Warrington have a home match, and the "gate" was consequently one of the smallest seen at a premier. league engagement at Wilderspool. There were several hundred faith- fuls, however, and despite wet jackets they cheered on the wearers of the once-famous primrose and blue.</p>
        <p>St. Helens, having also retired from the cnp • corn. petition, devoted their serious attention to the annexa- tion of paints. They brought a strong team—in fact stronger than that which defeated Warrington at Knowsley-road—and were evidently -.determined to repeat the performance. Warringtoh were short of Fish only, and likewise determined. This time they had the benefit of the elements.for the first half, and at once made tracks for the Saintly citadel. Edmunds and Duckworth opened out the .gAme finely, and the backs were nearly through in the first five Minutes after excellent combination. Other attempts also came to nought, but eventually HoCkenhall was given - a walk-over by Isherwood, after a passing run by the hicks in Warrington's old style. Not long after, the same player scored again from a cross-kick by Harris, a line race for the ball ;just ending in favour of the Warringtonian inches from the dead-ball line. This was all the scoring, the second half being productive of nothing but minors for the Saints, while Warring- toll were very near scoring tries on several occasions.</p>
        <p>The form of the WarringtOnians showed much im- provement, though they had nothing • to beat in the " back " line. One really mtirvelled how- such novices in football science as the St. Helens backs ever could have contributed to Warrington's defeat, and the rqsult of .this match emphasizes Warrington's atrocious display at St. Helens. There was a fair sprinkling of "A" team forwards in the front rank on Tuesday, and they quite held their own with the fine eight k Helens possess, in the scrums. Out in the open the Saints were much superior and their clever dribbling was the team's most effective form of attack. • Briers, Carney, and Lee were a fine trio and several times - they menaced Hallam's charge. The Warrincr,ton.halves and threequarters played smartly, particularly Edmunds and Duckworth, who quite out-classed Hall and Banks. The latter, by the - Way, never had a single "pp " at goal, which shows what a different tread the game took from the play at St. Helens. Edmunds was quite the smartest man on the field, and delighted the crowd with his tricky and effective work. Isherwood and Dickenson were better than their wings, while Hallam was -a good as Foulkes. Both full backs have played better, however. The' St.- Helens threequaiters had uo idea of the game, and would make better for- wards than backs. The Saints, so I am told, have a superabundance of good forwards. Could Warring- ton not effect a deal?</p>
        <p>There were several other matches on Tuesday, the results affecting both ends of the table. Broughton Rangers and Swinton were put out of court for the championship by Halifax and. Runcorn, the linnets winning on the Swinton ground by 2 tries to nil. Salford are now second and look like running a close race with Halifax. Warrington's win raised them to sixth place from the bottom, which is encouraging.</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows : For Agt.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G.- T. P. ' P. Halifax 25...17... 6.. 2.. 23 28-130...13 14— 68...36 Salford 25...15... 6-4...24 40-168...17 16— 82...34 *Swinton 24...13... 5:..6...29 43-187...15 16— 78...32 Bro'ght'u R 24...12... 7...5...22 38-158...13 15— 71...29 Runcorn 22-12... 6...4...22 38-158...15 13— 69...28 *Oldham 22...14... 8...0...20 36-148-16 13— 71...28 Bradford 24...12... 9...3...26 35-157...20 24-112...27 Batley 24...12...10...2...25 26-128...24 24-120...26 Hull 25-12-11.. °.. ~. .15 32-126...21 26-120...26 Hunslet 24...11-11..:2...32 20-124...27 40-174...24 Leigh 24... 9...10...5...22 17— 95...18 24-108...23 Wigan 22... 8...10...4...19 18— 92...16 21— 95...20 Warrington 23... 8...11...4...14 17— 79...22 27-125...20 Widnes 24... 9-14:..1...19 17— 89...18 29-123:..19 Huddersfield 24., 7...15...2...10 19— 77...27 32-150...16 Hull K. R. 23.417-14-2-10 18— 74...29 36-166...16 Brighouse R 25... 6...16...3...15 11— 63...26 $5-157...15 St Helens 24... 4...19...1...20 10— 70:..30 58-234... 9 The °Swinton V. Oldham match has been ordered to be replayed, and has been• struck out of the above table.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens match also counted in the South WeA Lancashire Competition, Warrington aud Run- corn now being equal on matches played. The latter have the better chance, however' as they have only Leigh and Wigan to play, and both matches are at Runcorn. Tins .ties Warrington to win at Wigan,and beat Wigan and Widnes at Wilderspool, in the re- maining three engagements.</p>
