        <p>A LOGICAL LUNATIC. • One day Southey, the poet, chanced to meet in St. James's Park a gentleman whom he had not seen since they were students together, more than forty years before. "And pray what have you been doing all these years ?" inquired the poet. Making mistakes," re- plied his friend. "A very singular industry, that," remarked the poet, "Not at all ; everybody is engaged in it. Most of our acts and more of our words are mistakes—blunders, errors, call them what you will so long as it signifies the thing which is not right. My servant says she lights a fire to boil the kettle, not that she she, a fire to boil the water. As if it were possible to light fire ! True, kettles might be boiled, though I never knew of any to be so treated. When I left home, my wife asked me if I were going for a walk (as though she expected me to bring it back on my shoulder), not if I were going to walk. As for material mistakes, I married (disastrous blunder), and you, I hear, make the wretched mistake of writing poetry. I read a little of it once. Bah !" Confounded by thepeculiarity of the man's discourse, Southey inquired concerning him and learned that he I had been insane for years, but being considered harm- ' less was allowed to walk unattended In the park. Mr. William Taylor, of 15, Hanover-street, Haver- stock Hill, N.W., made the mistake of taking 17 half- crown bottles of a medicine which was useless for the cure of his complaint before he found the right remedy. "It was Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup that saved my life three years ago," writes Mr. Taylor, on 24th March,! 1902. "A stifling sensation in the throatand chest, with difficulty in breathing, was followed by a gnawing pain in the pit of the stomach. My appetite was wry poor, and I was always tired and weak. For two months a doctor endeavoured to cure me of my indigestion and debility, but failed. Seventeen half-crown bottles of a certain patent medicine also failing to relieve me, I became very despondent. I would toss about in bed at night for hours, unable to sleep; was often seized with fits of trembling and was in constant dread of some im- pending evil. Such was my condition when I was recommended to try Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup. The first bottle of that medicine eased my incessant pain,and by the time I had finished the third bottle I felt ' well and strong. That. was three years ago, and my health is still good." Warrington P.S.A. Society. Mr. Llewellyn presided at the usual meeting of this society which was held,in the Co-operative Hall, San- key-street, on Sunday. Mr. L. E. Wood rendered the violin solos " Intermezzo " and "Home, sweet Home" I in finished style, and was accompanied on the piano I by Mr. Nelson. Mr. Light gave an instructive address- Third Starr-Bowkett Building Society. On Monday evening the thirteenth annual meetinx of this society wds held at the office of Mr. Georg* Titchett, the secretary, Cairo-street. Mr. R. Lunt presided. The report and balance sheet which I showed a very satisfactory year's working, there be- ing a good balance of profit, were adopted. The re- tiring directrms, Messrs. Aukland, Winstanley and 1 Lunt, were re-elected, as also were the auditors,' Messrs. E. Bradshaw, chartered accountant, and R. H. Pearson. SENSATIONAL MYSTERY. We have all looked with admiring eyes upon a con- juror who deftly fingers an article with the words, 'New you see it—now you don't," Take the canary in the cage, for instance. Blithely it sings before your very eyes: Then like a flash it is gone. What becomes of the canary? We know that it is not really lost, and we scratch our loads and ask, where it is? There is no need for lioseph Barnes, who resides at Frome, Somerset, to scratch his head and look round for what ' he has lost, for it is something which he does not wish to find again. "I am pleased with the great benefit derived from Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills," he says. "For months past I have been a terrible sufferer from indigestion and impure blood. I tried nearly all the advertised medicines, without result. Work was out of the question, and I was growing worse. Then large sores commenced to make their appearance, and my food gave me no strength. My stomach was all out of order. A few doses of your pills cured my indigestion, and the sores commenced to heal. Continuing their use they completely cured me. My general health is now good in every way, and this I owe to Dr. Morse's In- dian Root Pills." Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills are a positive cure for biliousness, indigestion, dyspepsia, I constipation' foul breath, liver complaint sallow com- plexion, sick headache, kidney troubles, and female ailments ; also a perfect blood purifier, curing skin di- seases, such as eczema, pimples and blotches. Sold by Chemists and Storekeepers, price Is. lid. per bottle, or six bottles 6s. Gd. If unable to procure where you re- side, The W. H. Comstock Co., Limited, 21, Farringdon Avenue, London, E. C., will forward same on receipt of price.</p>
